// Physics O/IGCSE – embedded data (generated)
window.physicsChapters = [{"chapter":"Centre of Mass Moments","questions":[{"id":"Moments_1","q":"Define the moment of a force and state its SI unit.","m":2,"ans":["Product of the force and the perpendicular distance from the pivot to the line of action of the force.","Unit: Newton-metre (Nm)."]},{"id":"Principle_of_Moments_2","q":"A uniform meter rule is pivoted at the 40cm mark. A 2.0N weight is placed at the 10cm mark. Calculate the weight of the rule.","m":4,"ans":["Clockwise moments = Anticlockwise moments.","Weight acts at 50cm mark (pivot to weight distance = 10cm).","2.0N * 30cm = W * 10cm.","W = 6.0 N."]},{"id":"Center_of_Mass_3","q":"Describe an experiment to find the center of mass of an irregularly shaped thin lamina.","m":3,"ans":["Suspend lamina from a hole near the edge and hang a plumb line from the same point.","Draw a line along the plumb line; repeat for a second hole.","The intersection of the lines is the center of mass."]},{"id":"Stability_4","q":"Explain why a racing car is designed with a low center of gravity and a wide wheel track.","m":2,"ans":["Increases stability.","The vertical line from the center of mass is less likely to fall outside the base when tilted, preventing toppling."]},{"id":"Pressure_5","q":"A box of weight 600N has a base area of 0.5m x 0.2m. Calculate the pressure it exerts on the ground.","m":2,"ans":["Area = 0.5 * 0.2 = 0.1 m².","Pressure = Force / Area = 600 / 0.1 = 6000 Pa."]},{"id":"Liquid_Pressure_6","q":"Calculate the pressure exerted by water at a depth of 50m. (Density = 1000kg/m³, g=10).","m":1,"ans":["P = hρg = 50 * 1000 * 10 = 500,000 Pa."]},{"id":"Atmospheric_Pressure_7","q":"Why does a mercury barometer's height remain the same even if the tube is tilted (provided the top is still a vacuum)?","m":1,"ans":["Pressure depends only on the vertical height of the liquid column, not the length along the tube."]},{"id":"Moments_8","q":"A 150N girl sits 2.0m from the pivot of a see-saw. Where should a 200N boy sit to balance her?","m":2,"ans":["150 * 2.0 = 200 * d.","d = 300 / 200 = 1.5 m from the pivot."]},{"id":"Pressure_9","q":"Explain in terms of molecules how a gas exerts pressure on the walls of its container.","m":3,"ans":["Molecules collide with the walls.","Force is exerted during the change in momentum of the molecules.","Pressure is total force per unit area."]},{"id":"Manometers_10","q":"A U-tube manometer shows a height difference of 15cm of water. Calculate the excess gas pressure.","m":1,"ans":["P = hρg = 0.15 * 1000 * 10 = 1500 Pa."]},{"id":"Center_of_Mass_11","q":"Where is the center of mass of a uniform circular ring located?","m":1,"ans":["At the geometric center (outside the material of the ring)."]},{"id":"Stability_12","q":"When does an object start to topple?","m":1,"ans":["When the vertical line through its center of mass falls outside its base."]},{"id":"Pressure_13","q":"A thumb tack has a head area of 1cm² and a point area of 0.01mm². If a force of 10N is applied, compare the pressures.","m":2,"ans":["Pressure at head = 10 / 1e-4 = 100,000 Pa.","Pressure at point = 10 / 1e-8 = 1,000,000,000 Pa."]},{"id":"Hydraulics_14","q":"A force of 50N is applied to a piston of area 0.01m². Calculate the force produced on a larger piston of 0.5m².","m":2,"ans":["Pressure = 50 / 0.01 = 5000 Pa.","Force = Pressure * Area = 5000 * 0.5 = 2500 N."]},{"id":"Moments_15","q":"State the two conditions for an object to be in equilibrium.","m":2,"ans":["Resultant force is zero.","Resultant moment is zero."]},{"id":"Liquid_Pressure_16","q":"Why is the wall of a dam thicker at the bottom than at the top?","m":2,"ans":["Liquid pressure increases with depth (P = hρg).","The thicker wall withstands the greater force at the bottom."]},{"id":"Moments_17","q":"Calculate the force F required to lift a 200N load using a crowbar 1.5m long, pivoted 0.3m from the load.","m":3,"ans":["Anticlockwise = 200 * 0.3 = 60 Nm.","Clockwise = F * (1.5 - 0.3) = 1.2F.","F = 60 / 1.2 = 50 N."]},{"id":"Pressure_18","q":"If you climb a mountain, what happens to the atmospheric pressure? Explain.","m":2,"ans":["Pressure decreases.","The density of air decreases and there is less weight of air above you."]},{"id":"Stability_19","q":"A bus carries passengers only on the lower deck in high winds. Explain why.","m":1,"ans":["Keeps the center of mass low, increasing stability and preventing the bus from blowing over."]},{"id":"Density/Pressure_20","q":"Compare the pressure at the bottom of two tanks filled to 1m, one with water and one with oil (density 800kg/m³).","m":2,"ans":["Water pressure = 10,000 Pa; Oil pressure = 8,000 Pa.","Water pressure is higher because it is denser."]},{"id":"Barometer_21","q":"What is the effect on the barometer reading if a small amount of air enters the space above the mercury?","m":1,"ans":["The mercury level drops because the air exerts a downward pressure on the mercury."]},{"id":"Moments_22","q":"A uniform plank of 4.0m and 200N is supported at both ends. Calculate the upward force at each support.","m":2,"ans":["By symmetry, each support carries half the weight.","Force = 100 N each."]},{"id":"Hydraulics_23","q":"Name the property of liquids that allows hydraulic systems to work.","m":1,"ans":["Incompressibility (liquids cannot be compressed)."]},{"id":"Pressure_24","q":"How does wearing snowshoes prevent a person from sinking into snow?","m":1,"ans":["Increases the surface area, which reduces the pressure exerted on the snow."]},{"id":"Moments_25","q":"A spanner is 25cm long. A force of 40N is applied at the end at an angle of 90 deg. Find the moment.","m":1,"ans":["Moment = 40 * 0.25 = 10 Nm."]},{"id":"Stability_26","q":"Define 'neutral equilibrium'.","m":1,"ans":["When an object is displaced, its center of mass stays at the same height (e.g., a rolling ball)."]},{"id":"Pressure_27","q":"Calculate the force exerted by the atmosphere (100,000 Pa) on a roof of area 50m².","m":1,"ans":["Force = 100,000 * 50 = 5,000,000 N."]},{"id":"Liquid_Pressure_28","q":"Does the shape of a container affect the pressure at a certain depth?","m":1,"ans":["No, pressure only depends on vertical depth, density, and g."]},{"id":"Moments_29","q":"A door requires a moment of 12Nm to open. If the handle is 0.8m from the hinges, what is the minimum force?","m":1,"ans":["F = 12 / 0.8 = 15 N."]},{"id":"Center_of_Mass_30","q":"Can the center of mass of an object be outside the object? Give an example.","m":1,"ans":["Yes. Example: A doughnut or a horseshoe."]},{"id":"Pressure_31","q":"Unit of pressure in terms of SI base units.","m":1,"ans":["kg m⁻¹ s⁻²."]},{"id":"Stability_32","q":"Which is more stable: a cone resting on its base or on its tip?","m":1,"ans":["Resting on its base (Stable equilibrium) because its CM is lower."]},{"id":"Moments_33","q":"A non-uniform rod is balanced at its center. Is the weight equal on both sides?","m":1,"ans":["No, the side where the CM is closer to the pivot has more mass."]},{"id":"Manometers_34","q":"A gas supply is connected to a manometer. The liquid level in the open arm is lower than the gas arm. Is gas pressure higher or lower than atmospheric?","m":1,"ans":["Lower than atmospheric pressure."]},{"id":"Pressure_35","q":"Why are aircraft cabins pressurized?","m":1,"ans":["At high altitudes, atmospheric pressure is too low for humans to breathe sufficient oxygen."]},{"id":"Hydraulics_36","q":"If the small piston in a hydraulic jack moves 10cm, and the area ratio is 1:5, how far does the large piston move?","m":1,"ans":["10 / 5 = 2 cm."]},{"id":"Moments_37","q":"Two people carry a heavy 2.0m uniform beam of 500N. Person A is at one end, Person B is 0.5m from the other end. Calculate force by Person B.","m":2,"ans":["Pivot at A. 500 * 1.0 = B * 1.5.","B = 500 / 1.5 = 333.3 N."]},{"id":"Liquid_Pressure_38","q":"Specific gravity of a liquid is 1.2. Calculate pressure at 2m depth.","m":2,"ans":["Density = 1200 kg/m³.","P = 2 * 1200 * 10 = 24,000 Pa."]},{"id":"Center_of_Mass_39","q":"Where is the CM of a uniform triangular lamina?","m":1,"ans":["At the intersection of the medians (two-thirds from each vertex)."]},{"id":"Pressure_40","q":"1 Pascal is equal to how many N/cm²?","m":1,"ans":["1 Pa = 0.0001 N/cm²."]},{"id":"Moments_41","q":"A uniform bridge of 10m length and 50,000N weight is supported at ends A and B. A 10,000N car is 2m from A. Find support force at B.","m":3,"ans":["Pivot at A. (50,000 * 5) + (10,000 * 2) = B * 10.","250,000 + 20,000 = 10B.","B = 27,000 N."]},{"id":"Stability_42","q":"Why is it easier to balance a long pole on your hand than a short one?","m":1,"ans":["A long pole has a greater moment of inertia and higher CM, making its angular acceleration slower and easier to react to."]},{"id":"Pressure_43","q":"If volume of a gas is halved at constant temperature, what happens to pressure?","m":1,"ans":["Pressure doubles (Boyle's Law)."]},{"id":"Barometer_44","q":"If mercury (density 13600) is replaced by water in a barometer, how high would the column be at 1 atm?","m":1,"ans":["h = 100,000 / (1000 * 10) = 10 m."]},{"id":"Moments_45","q":"Can a force act on a pivot and create a moment?","m":1,"ans":["No, because the perpendicular distance from the pivot is zero."]},{"id":"Hydraulics_46","q":"Power is conserved in hydraulics. If force increases, what decreases?","m":1,"ans":["Distance/Speed of the piston."]},{"id":"Center_of_Mass_47","q":"Define the center of mass.","m":1,"ans":["The point where the entire mass of an object appears to be concentrated."]},{"id":"Pressure_48","q":"Pressure at 10m depth in the ocean (density 1025 kg/m³) plus atmospheric pressure.","m":1,"ans":["P = (10 * 1025 * 10) + 100,000 = 102,500 + 100,000 = 202,500 Pa."]},{"id":"Moments_49","q":"Is the moment of a force a scalar or a vector?","m":1,"ans":["Vector (it has magnitude and direction: clockwise or anticlockwise)."]},{"id":"Stability_50","q":"Identify the type of equilibrium for a pencil balanced on its point.","m":1,"ans":["Unstable equilibrium."]}]},{"chapter":"Dynamics Forces Basics","questions":[{"id":"DYN-001","q":"State Newton's First Law of Motion.","m":2,"ans":["An object remains at rest or moves with constant velocity unless acted upon by a resultant force.","Balanced forces = constant motion/rest."]},{"id":"DYN-002","q":"State the formula for Newton's Second Law.","m":1,"ans":["$F = ma$","Resultant Force = mass x acceleration"]},{"id":"DYN-003","q":"Define 'Inertia'.","m":1,"ans":["The resistance of an object to any change in its state of motion.","Reluctance to change motion."]},{"id":"DYN-004","q":"A force of 10 N acts on a 2 kg mass. Calculate the acceleration.","m":2,"ans":["5 m/s²","10 / 2"]},{"id":"DYN-005","q":"State Newton's Third Law of Motion.","m":2,"ans":["For every action force, there is an equal and opposite reaction force.","Action and reaction are equal/opposite."]},{"id":"DYN-006","q":"Distinguish between mass and weight.","m":2,"ans":["Mass is the amount of matter (kg); weight is the force of gravity (N).","Scalar (kg) vs Vector (N)."]},{"id":"DYN-007","q":"A car of mass 1000 kg moves at constant velocity. What is the resultant force?","m":1,"ans":["0 N","Zero (balanced forces)"]},{"id":"DYN-008","q":"What is the weight of a 500 g mass on Earth ($g=10$ N/kg)?","m":2,"ans":["5 N","0.5 * 10"]},{"id":"DYN-009","q":"Identify two factors that affect the magnitude of friction.","m":2,"ans":["1. The nature of the surfaces. 2. The normal contact force (weight).","Surface roughness and pressure."]},{"id":"DYN-010","q":"A skydivier opens a parachute. Describe the change in the air resistance force.","m":1,"ans":["Air resistance increases significantly.","Drag increases."]},{"id":"DYN-011","q":"An object has a mass of 60 kg on Earth. What is its mass on the Moon?","m":1,"ans":["60 kg","Mass is constant everywhere."]},{"id":"DYN-012","q":"Calculate the weight of a 60 kg person on the Moon ($g=1.6$ N/kg).","m":2,"ans":["96 N","60 * 1.6"]},{"id":"DYN-013","q":"A block is pulled with 20 N to the right, and friction is 5 N to the left. Find the resultant force.","m":1,"ans":["15 N to the right","20 - 5"]},{"id":"DYN-014","q":"In which direction does friction always act?","m":1,"ans":["Opposite to the direction of motion/intended motion.","Against motion."]},{"id":"DYN-015","q":"If the mass of an object is doubled but the force remains the same, what happens to acceleration?","m":2,"ans":["Acceleration is halved.","$a \\propto 1/m$"]},{"id":"DYN-016","q":"Define 'terminal velocity' using the concept of forces.","m":2,"ans":["The velocity when air resistance equals weight and the resultant force is zero.","Balanced upward/downward forces."]},{"id":"DYN-017","q":"What is the SI unit of force?","m":1,"ans":["Newton (N)","N"]},{"id":"DYN-018","q":"Explain why a seatbelt is necessary in terms of inertia.","m":3,"ans":["1. When a car stops suddenly, the passenger continues to move forward due to inertia. 2. The seatbelt provides a backwards force to decelerate the passenger.","Prevents inertia-driven forward motion."]},{"id":"DYN-019","q":"A rocket burns fuel. Why does its acceleration increase even if the thrust is constant?","m":2,"ans":["The mass of the rocket decreases as fuel is used, and $a = F/m$.","Mass decreases, acceleration increases."]},{"id":"DYN-020","q":"Does a stationary object have any forces acting on it?","m":2,"ans":["Yes (e.g., weight and normal reaction), but the resultant force is zero.","Balanced forces."]},{"id":"DYN-021","q":"State the relationship between force and acceleration for a constant mass.","m":1,"ans":["Force is directly proportional to acceleration.","$F \\propto a$"]},{"id":"DYN-022","q":"A 2 kg block accelerates at 3 m/s². Calculate the resultant force.","m":1,"ans":["6 N","2 * 3"]},{"id":"DYN-023","q":"What happens to the motion of an object if the resultant force is in the opposite direction of its motion?","m":1,"ans":["The object decelerates (slows down).","Slowing down."]},{"id":"DYN-024","q":"Why is it easier to push an empty shopping cart than a full one?","m":2,"ans":["A full cart has more mass, therefore more inertia and requires more force for the same acceleration.","More mass = more inertia."]},{"id":"DYN-025","q":"A book rests on a table. The weight of the book is 10 N. What is the normal reaction force?","m":1,"ans":["10 N upwards","10 N"]},{"id":"DYN-026","q":"Define 'Centripetal Force'.","m":2,"ans":["The resultant force acting towards the center of a circle that keeps an object in circular motion.","Inward force in a circle."]},{"id":"DYN-027","q":"A car turns a corner. What provides the centripetal force?","m":1,"ans":["Friction between the tires and the road.","Friction."]},{"id":"DYN-028","q":"What is the gravitational field strength ($g$) on Earth?","m":1,"ans":["9.8 N/kg (or 10 N/kg)","10 N/kg"]},{"id":"DYN-029","q":"If an object is moving at a constant speed in a circle, is the resultant force zero?","m":2,"ans":["No, there is a resultant centripetal force because the direction is changing.","Resultant force is towards the center."]},{"id":"DYN-030","q":"A 100 kg box is pushed with 500 N. If friction is 100 N, find acceleration.","m":3,"ans":["4 m/s²","(500 - 100) / 100"]},{"id":"DYN-031","q":"State one way to reduce friction.","m":1,"ans":["Lubrication (oil/grease) / Using rollers or ball bearings.","Lubrication."]},{"id":"DYN-032","q":"Why do cars have streamlined shapes?","m":2,"ans":["To reduce air resistance (drag) and improve fuel efficiency.","Reduce drag."]},{"id":"DYN-033","q":"An object is in equilibrium. What two conditions must be met?","m":2,"ans":["1. Resultant force is zero. 2. Resultant moment is zero.","No net force; no net moment."]},{"id":"DYN-034","q":"State the unit of mass.","m":1,"ans":["Kilogram (kg)","kg"]},{"id":"DYN-035","q":"How does the mass of an object affect its acceleration for a fixed force?","m":1,"ans":["Inversely proportional.","$a = F/m$"]},{"id":"DYN-036","q":"A person jumps out of a plane. At the start, what is their acceleration?","m":1,"ans":["9.8 m/s² (or $g$)","10 m/s²"]},{"id":"DYN-037","q":"What is the formula for weight?","m":1,"ans":["$W = mg$","Mass x gravity"]},{"id":"DYN-038","q":"Name the instrument used to measure weight.","m":1,"ans":["Spring balance / Newton meter.","Newton meter."]},{"id":"DYN-039","q":"Name the instrument used to measure mass.","m":1,"ans":["Beam balance / Electronic balance.","Balance."]},{"id":"DYN-040","q":"What is 'tension'?","m":1,"ans":["The pulling force exerted by a string, cable, or chain on an object.","Pulling force in a rope."]},{"id":"DYN-041","q":"A mass is suspended by two vertical strings. If weight is 50 N, what is the tension in each string?","m":2,"ans":["25 N","50 / 2"]},{"id":"DYN-042","q":"Does mass change when an object is taken into space?","m":1,"ans":["No","Mass is constant."]},{"id":"DYN-043","q":"In a vacuum, if you push a 10 kg mass with 10 N, how long will it continue to move after you stop pushing?","m":2,"ans":["Indefinitely (forever) at constant velocity.","Newton's First Law."]},{"id":"DYN-044","q":"What is the resultant force on an object falling at terminal velocity?","m":1,"ans":["0 N","Zero"]},{"id":"DYN-045","q":"Calculate the force needed to give a 500 g mass an acceleration of 2 m/s².","m":2,"ans":["1 N","0.5 * 2"]},{"id":"DYN-046","q":"If the earth's gravity suddenly disappeared, what would happen to the moon's motion?","m":2,"ans":["It would move in a straight line at a tangent to its original orbit.","Straight line motion."]},{"id":"DYN-047","q":"Define 'Resultant Force'.","m":1,"ans":["A single force that has the same effect as all the original forces acting together.","Net force."]},{"id":"DYN-048","q":"What is the effect of friction on the temperature of an object?","m":1,"ans":["It increases the temperature (converts work to thermal energy).","Heating effect."]},{"id":"DYN-049","q":"A 1500 kg car brakes with a force of 3000 N. Calculate the deceleration.","m":2,"ans":["2 m/s²","3000 / 1500"]},{"id":"DYN-050","q":"If you push against a wall with 50 N, how much force does the wall exert on you?","m":1,"ans":["50 N","50 N (opposite direction)"]},{"id":"DYN-051","q":"What is the direction of the normal reaction force?","m":1,"ans":["Perpendicular to the surface.","At 90 degrees to surface."]},{"id":"DYN-052","q":"A ball is moving in a circle. If the string breaks, in which direction does the ball move?","m":1,"ans":["Tangentially to the circle.","Along the tangent."]},{"id":"DYN-053","q":"State the relationship between mass and inertia.","m":1,"ans":["The greater the mass, the greater the inertia.","$Inertia \\propto mass$"]},{"id":"DYN-054","q":"Calculate the mass of an object that weighs 450 N on Earth.","m":2,"ans":["45 kg","450 / 10"]},{"id":"DYN-055","q":"Why is it harder to stop a massive truck than a small car moving at the same speed?","m":2,"ans":["The truck has more mass/inertia, requiring more force to change its motion.","Higher inertia."]},{"id":"DYN-056","q":"An object moves at constant velocity. Are the forces acting on it balanced?","m":1,"ans":["Yes","Resultant force is zero."]},{"id":"DYN-057","q":"What force is responsible for keeping planets in orbit?","m":1,"ans":["Gravitational force.","Gravity."]},{"id":"DYN-058","q":"Define 'Gravitational Field'.","m":2,"ans":["A region where a mass experiences a force due to gravitational attraction.","Area of gravity."]},{"id":"DYN-059","q":"A tug-of-war team pulls with 500 N, the other with 450 N. What is the resultant force?","m":1,"ans":["50 N","500 - 450"]},{"id":"DYN-060","q":"If force is measured in kg m/s², what is this unit commonly called?","m":1,"ans":["Newton","N"]},{"id":"DYN-061","q":"Does friction depend on the surface area in contact (for O Level)?","m":1,"ans":["No","Independent of area."]},{"id":"DYN-062","q":"A 10 kg mass is pulled by 50 N. Acceleration is 4 m/s². Find the friction force.","m":3,"ans":["10 N","$50 - f = 10 * 4 \rightarrow f = 10$"]},{"id":"DYN-063","q":"What is the weight of a 2 kg object on a planet where $g=4$ N/kg?","m":2,"ans":["8 N","2 * 4"]},{"id":"DYN-064","q":"When a gun is fired, why does it recoil?","m":2,"ans":["Newton's Third Law: The gun exerts a force on the bullet, and the bullet exerts an equal/opposite force on the gun.","Action-reaction pair."]},{"id":"DYN-065","q":"State the effect of a resultant force on an object's velocity.","m":1,"ans":["It causes the object to accelerate (change velocity).","Causes acceleration."]},{"id":"DYN-066","q":"A man stands on a scale in a lift moving at constant speed. Does the reading change?","m":1,"ans":["No","Forces are balanced."]},{"id":"DYN-067","q":"What provides the centripetal force for an electron orbiting a nucleus?","m":1,"ans":["Electrostatic force.","Electric attraction."]},{"id":"DYN-068","q":"What is the net force on a 5 kg object accelerating at 10 m/s²?","m":1,"ans":["50 N","5 * 10"]},{"id":"DYN-069","q":"If you are in a moving car and it turns left, why do you feel pushed to the right?","m":2,"ans":["Due to inertia, your body wants to continue moving in a straight line.","Inertia."]},{"id":"DYN-070","q":"Can an object be accelerating if the speed is constant?","m":1,"ans":["Yes, if the direction is changing (circular motion).","Yes"]},{"id":"DYN-071","q":"Define 'Gravitational Field Strength'.","m":2,"ans":["Gravitational force per unit mass.","$g = F/m$"]},{"id":"DYN-072","q":"A block of 5 kg is on a slope. If the component of weight down the slope is 25 N and it moves at constant speed, find friction.","m":2,"ans":["25 N","Balanced forces on slope."]},{"id":"DYN-073","q":"Is 'Weight' a scalar or a vector?","m":1,"ans":["Vector","Vector"]},{"id":"DYN-074","q":"What is the acceleration of a 20 kg mass acted upon by a 100 N force?","m":1,"ans":["5 m/s²","100 / 20"]},{"id":"DYN-075","q":"Why do athletes wear shoes with spikes?","m":1,"ans":["To increase friction (grip) with the ground.","More friction."]},{"id":"DYN-076","q":"A 2 kg ball is falling. Air resistance is 5 N. What is the resultant force? ($g=10$)","m":2,"ans":["15 N downwards","20 - 5"]},{"id":"DYN-077","q":"State the units of gravitational field strength.","m":1,"ans":["N/kg","Newtons per kilogram"]},{"id":"DYN-078","q":"If an object has zero mass, can it have weight?","m":1,"ans":["No","W = 0"]},{"id":"DYN-079","q":"When a car accelerates, what force actually pushes the car forward?","m":1,"ans":["Friction from the road on the tires.","Road friction."]},{"id":"DYN-080","q":"A 1 kg mass is on a table. A 10 N force is applied but it doesn't move. Why?","m":2,"ans":["Friction is equal and opposite to the applied force (Static friction).","Balanced forces."]},{"id":"DYN-081","q":"Define 'balanced forces'.","m":1,"ans":["Forces that are equal in size and opposite in direction, resulting in zero net force.","Net force = 0."]},{"id":"DYN-082","q":"What is the value of $g$ on the surface of the moon?","m":1,"ans":["1.6 N/kg","1.6"]},{"id":"DYN-083","q":"Calculate the mass of a car that requires 4000 N to accelerate at 4 m/s².","m":1,"ans":["1000 kg","4000 / 4"]},{"id":"DYN-084","q":"What happens to the weight of an object as it moves away from the Earth?","m":1,"ans":["It decreases.","Decreases."]},{"id":"DYN-085","q":"Why is it dangerous to have loose objects on the back shelf of a car?","m":2,"ans":["If the car stops suddenly, they will fly forward due to inertia.","Inertia hazard."]},{"id":"DYN-086","q":"Which of Newton's laws explains why we need to exert a force to start a car moving?","m":1,"ans":["Second Law ($F=ma$)","Newton's 2nd."]},{"id":"DYN-087","q":"If a resultant force is tripled, what happens to acceleration (constant mass)?","m":1,"ans":["It triples.","Directly proportional."]},{"id":"DYN-088","q":"A ship moves through water at constant speed. Thrust is 10,000 N. What is the water resistance?","m":1,"ans":["10,000 N","Balanced forces."]},{"id":"DYN-089","q":"What force prevents an object from falling through a floor?","m":1,"ans":["Normal contact force / Normal reaction.","Reaction force."]},{"id":"DYN-090","q":"Calculate the resultant force on a 50 kg mass accelerating at 0.5 m/s².","m":1,"ans":["25 N","50 * 0.5"]},{"id":"DYN-091","q":"Does a parachute increase or decrease terminal velocity?","m":1,"ans":["Decreases","Lowers terminal speed."]},{"id":"DYN-092","q":"In a free-body diagram, how is a force represented?","m":1,"ans":["By an arrow pointing in the direction of the force.","Vector arrow."]},{"id":"DYN-093","q":"Can weight be zero?","m":1,"ans":["Yes (in deep space, far from any mass).","Yes"]},{"id":"DYN-094","q":"If you drop a ball on a moving train (constant velocity), where does it land?","m":1,"ans":["Directly below where it was dropped.","In your hand/at your feet."]},{"id":"DYN-095","q":"State the formula for density.","m":1,"ans":["$\rho = m/V$","Mass / Volume"]},{"id":"DYN-096","q":"What is the relationship between friction and heat?","m":1,"ans":["Friction converts kinetic energy into thermal energy.","Produces heat."]},{"id":"DYN-097","q":"A force of 100 N acts at 30 degrees to the horizontal. What is the horizontal component?","m":2,"ans":["86.6 N","100 cos 30"]},{"id":"DYN-098","q":"A 5 kg block is pulled up by 60 N. What is the acceleration? ($g=10$)","m":3,"ans":["2 m/s²","(60 - 50) / 5"]},{"id":"DYN-099","q":"Is mass a base quantity or a derived quantity?","m":1,"ans":["Base quantity","Base"]},{"id":"DYN-100","q":"If the resultant force on an object is zero, what can you say about its velocity?","m":1,"ans":["It is constant (could be zero).","Constant velocity."]}]},{"chapter":"Dynamics","questions":[{"id":"Newton's_First_Law_1","q":"A spacecraft moves in deep space with its engines off. Describe its motion and explain it using Newton's First Law.","m":2,"ans":["Moves at constant velocity in a straight line.","Resultant force is zero, so inertia maintains its state of motion."]},{"id":"Resultant_Force_2","q":"A box is pulled by two forces: 12N North and 5N East. Calculate the magnitude and direction of the acceleration if the mass is 2kg.","m":3,"ans":["Resultant Force = sqrt(12^2 + 5^2) = 13N.","a = F/m = 13/2 = 6.5 m/s^2.","Direction = tan⁻¹(5/12) = 22.6 deg East of North."]},{"id":"Newton's_Second_Law_3","q":"A 1200kg car increases speed from 10m/s to 25m/s in 5s. Calculate the resultant force acting on it.","m":2,"ans":["a = (25-10)/5 = 3 m/s^2.","F = ma = 1200 * 3 = 3600 N."]},{"id":"Friction_4","q":"A block of 5kg is pushed with 20N on a rough floor. It moves at constant speed. What is the frictional force?","m":2,"ans":["Resultant force = 0 since speed is constant.","Friction = Applied force = 20 N."]},{"id":"Circular_Motion_5","q":"Explain why a car traveling at constant speed around a circular track is said to be accelerating.","m":2,"ans":["Velocity is a vector; direction changes continuously.","Acceleration is rate of change of velocity, hence it accelerates towards the center."]},{"id":"Newton's_Third_Law_6","q":"A book rests on a table. Identify the Newton's Third Law pair for the weight of the book.","m":1,"ans":["The gravitational pull of the book on the Earth, acting upwards towards the book's center."]},{"id":"Forces_on_Slopes_7","q":"A 10kg crate is on a frictionless 30-degree slope. Calculate the component of weight acting down the slope.","m":2,"ans":["W = mg = 100N.","Component = W sin(30) = 100 * 0.5 = 50 N."]},{"id":"Dynamics_8","q":"A rocket of mass 500,000kg produces a thrust of 7,000,000N. Calculate initial upward acceleration.","m":3,"ans":["Weight = 500,000 * 10 = 5,000,000N.","Resultant Force = 7M - 5M = 2,000,000N.","a = 2M / 0.5M = 4.0 m/s^2."]},{"id":"Terminal_Velocity_9","q":"A diver of mass 70kg experiences 200N of air resistance. Calculate her acceleration at that instant.","m":3,"ans":["Weight = 700N.","Resultant = 700 - 200 = 500N.","a = 500 / 70 = 7.14 m/s^2."]},{"id":"Inertia_10","q":"Compare the inertia of a 5kg lead ball and a 5kg bag of feathers.","m":1,"ans":["Inertia is identical as it depends only on mass, not volume or density."]},{"id":"Tension_11","q":"Two masses (3kg and 2kg) are connected by a string over a pulley. Find acceleration of the system.","m":3,"ans":["Total Mass = 5kg.","Net Force = (30N - 20N) = 10N.","a = 10/5 = 2.0 m/s^2."]},{"id":"Springs/Hooke's_Law_12","q":"A 2N force stretches a spring by 10mm. Find the force needed for 25mm extension.","m":2,"ans":["k = F/e = 2/10 = 0.2 N/mm.","F = k * e = 0.2 * 25 = 5 N."]},{"id":"Braking_13","q":"A car's braking force is doubled. How does the deceleration change?","m":1,"ans":["F = ma; if F is doubled and m is constant, a is doubled."]},{"id":"Circular_Motion_14","q":"Identify the source of centripetal force for a planet orbiting a star.","m":1,"ans":["Gravitational force."]},{"id":"Resultant_Force_15","q":"Three forces: 10N North, 10N South, 5N West. What is the resultant?","m":1,"ans":["North/South cancel out. Resultant = 5N West."]},{"id":"Dynamics_16","q":"Calculate the force needed to stop a 0.5kg ball moving at 20m/s in 0.2s.","m":2,"ans":["a = (0-20)/0.2 = -100 m/s^2.","F = 0.5 * 100 = 50 N."]},{"id":"Newton's_Laws_17","q":"Why does a passenger jerk forward when a bus stops suddenly?","m":1,"ans":["Inertia; the body tends to maintain its forward state of motion."]},{"id":"Forces_18","q":"Difference between contact and non-contact forces. Give examples.","m":1,"ans":["Contact requires physical touch (friction). Non-contact acts through a field (gravity)."]},{"id":"Friction_19","q":"Name two factors that increase the friction between a tire and the road.","m":2,"ans":["Rougher road surface.","Increased normal contact force (heavier load)."]},{"id":"Balanced_Forces_20","q":"An object moves at 500m/s in a straight line. What is the net force?","m":1,"ans":["Zero."]},{"id":"Newton's_Second_Law_21","q":"Force F produces acceleration 'a' on mass 'm'. What is the acceleration if force is 2F and mass is 0.5m?","m":1,"ans":["New a = 2F / 0.5m = 4(F/m) = 4a."]},{"id":"Weight/Mass_22","q":"An object weighs 50N on Earth. Find its mass.","m":1,"ans":["m = W/g = 50 / 10 = 5 kg."]},{"id":"Circular_Motion_23","q":"If centripetal force is removed from a whirling stone, what path does it take?","m":1,"ans":["Moves in a straight line tangent to the circle."]},{"id":"Springs_24","q":"Does a spring follow Hooke's law if it is permanently deformed?","m":1,"ans":["No, it has exceeded the limit of proportionality."]},{"id":"Forces_25","q":"Force on a 200g object accelerating at 5m/s2.","m":1,"ans":["F = 0.2 * 5 = 1.0 N."]},{"id":"Newton's_Third_Law_26","q":"A horse pulls a cart. If forces are equal and opposite, why does the cart move?","m":1,"ans":["The forces act on different bodies (Horse on cart, cart on horse). Motion occurs because the horse pushes the ground back."]},{"id":"Gravity_27","q":"Why is 'g' slightly different at the poles vs the equator?","m":1,"ans":["Earth is not a perfect sphere; distance to center varies."]},{"id":"Friction_28","q":"Does friction depend on the surface area of contact for a solid block?","m":1,"ans":["No, it depends on nature of surfaces and normal force."]},{"id":"Terminal_Velocity_29","q":"Draw a force diagram for an object at terminal velocity.","m":1,"ans":["Two arrows of equal length: Weight (down) and Drag/Air Resistance (up)."]},{"id":"Inertia_30","q":"Why is it harder to start moving a heavy truck than a car?","m":1,"ans":["Greater mass means greater inertia, requiring more force for the same acceleration."]},{"id":"Dynamics_31","q":"A force of 50N acts at 60 deg to vertical. Find the vertical component.","m":1,"ans":["Fv = 50 cos(60) = 25 N."]},{"id":"Resultant_Force_32","q":"Object mass 10kg, forces are 30N Right and 10N Left. Acceleration?","m":1,"ans":["Net F = 20N. a = 20/10 = 2 m/s^2."]},{"id":"Braking_33","q":"List two factors affecting braking distance.","m":1,"ans":["Braking force (tire/road condition) and mass of the vehicle."]},{"id":"Newton's_First_Law_34","q":"Can an object accelerate if the resultant force is zero?","m":1,"ans":["No."]},{"id":"Elasticity_35","q":"Define Spring Constant k.","m":1,"ans":["Force per unit extension (F/e)."]},{"id":"Circular_Motion_36","q":"If speed of a car in a circle doubles, centripetal force required increases by how much?","m":1,"ans":["F = mv^2/r; if v doubles, F increases by 4 times."]},{"id":"Forces_37","q":"Force on a 1.5kg object to change its velocity by 10m/s in 2s.","m":1,"ans":["a = 10/2 = 5. F = 1.5 * 5 = 7.5 N."]},{"id":"Friction_38","q":"How does lubrication reduce friction?","m":1,"ans":["Creates a thin layer between surfaces so they slide over the lubricant rather than each other."]},{"id":"Newton's_Second_Law_39","q":"A net force of 1N acts on 1kg. What is the acceleration?","m":1,"ans":["1 m/s^2."]},{"id":"Resultant_40","q":"Two forces of 10N each act on a body. Min and Max possible resultant?","m":1,"ans":["Min = 0N (opposite), Max = 20N (same direction)."]},{"id":"Weight_41","q":"Is weight a vector? Why?","m":1,"ans":["Yes, it has magnitude and acts towards the center of the Earth (direction)."]},{"id":"Dynamics_42","q":"A 50kg person in an elevator accelerating up at 2m/s2. Find normal contact force.","m":1,"ans":["R - mg = ma => R - 500 = 50*2. R = 600 N."]},{"id":"Free_Body_43","q":"Forces on a ball in the middle of its flight (ignore air).","m":1,"ans":["Only Weight acting downwards."]},{"id":"Tension_44","q":"A string breaks at 50N. Can it accelerate a 2kg mass at 30m/s2?","m":1,"ans":["F needed = 2 * 30 = 60N. No, string will break."]},{"id":"Stability_45","q":"Relationship between center of mass and stability.","m":1,"ans":["Lower center of mass and wider base increase stability."]},{"id":"Forces_46","q":"Unit of Force in base SI units.","m":1,"ans":["kg m/s^2."]},{"id":"Circular_Motion_47","q":"Does centripetal force do work? Why?","m":1,"ans":["No, force is perpendicular to the direction of motion."]},{"id":"Acceleration_48","q":"Object mass 5kg, speed 10m/s. Force of 20N applied for 3s. Final speed?","m":1,"ans":["a = 20/5 = 4. v = 10 + (4*3) = 22 m/s."]},{"id":"Dynamics_49","q":"Force required to lift a 5kg mass at constant speed.","m":1,"ans":["F = Weight = 50 N."]},{"id":"Newton's_Laws_50","q":"State the formula for Newton's 2nd Law in terms of momentum change.","m":1,"ans":["F = (mv - mu) / t."]}]},{"chapter":"Electricity Magnetism Basics","questions":[{"id":"ELE-001","q":"Define 'Electric Current' ($I$).","m":2,"ans":["The rate of flow of electric charge.","$I = Q / t$"]},{"id":"ELE-002","q":"State the SI unit for current.","m":1,"ans":["Ampere (A)","A"]},{"id":"ELE-003","q":"Define 'Electromotive Force' (e.m.f.).","m":2,"ans":["The work done by a source in driving a unit charge around a complete circuit.","Total energy per unit charge."]},{"id":"ELE-004","q":"Define 'Potential Difference' (p.d.).","m":2,"ans":["The work done to drive a unit charge through a component.","Energy per charge across a component."]},{"id":"ELE-005","q":"State Ohm’s Law.","m":2,"ans":["The current through a conductor is directly proportional to the p.d. across it, provided physical conditions (like temp) remain constant.","$V = IR$"]},{"id":"ELE-006","q":"State the SI unit for Resistance.","m":1,"ans":["Ohm ($\\Omega$)","$\\Omega$"]},{"id":"ELE-007","q":"What instrument is used to measure current and how is it connected?","m":2,"ans":["Ammeter; connected in series.","Ammeter in series."]},{"id":"ELE-008","q":"What instrument is used to measure p.d. and how is it connected?","m":2,"ans":["Voltmeter; connected in parallel.","Voltmeter in parallel."]},{"id":"ELE-009","q":"Define 'Conventional Current'.","m":1,"ans":["The flow of charge from the positive terminal to the negative terminal.","Positive to Negative."]},{"id":"ELE-010","q":"Describe the actual flow of electrons in a circuit.","m":1,"ans":["Electrons flow from the negative terminal to the positive terminal.","Negative to Positive."]},{"id":"ELE-011","q":"State the formula for electrical power.","m":1,"ans":["$P = VI$ or $P = I^2R$ or $P = V^2/R$","P = VI"]},{"id":"ELE-012","q":"How do you calculate the total resistance ($R_T$) of resistors in series?","m":1,"ans":["$R_T = R_1 + R_2 + ...$","Sum of resistances."]},{"id":"ELE-013","q":"How do you calculate $R_T$ for two resistors in parallel?","m":2,"ans":["$1/R_T = 1/R_1 + 1/R_2$ or $R_T = (R_1 \times R_2) / (R_1 + R_2)$","Product over sum."]},{"id":"ELE-014","q":"What happens to the total resistance if more resistors are added in parallel?","m":1,"ans":["The total resistance decreases.","Resistance drops."]},{"id":"ELE-015","q":"Name the three wires in a standard UK/Singapore plug.","m":1,"ans":["Live (Brown), Neutral (Blue), and Earth (Green/Yellow).","Live, Neutral, Earth."]},{"id":"ELE-016","q":"What is the purpose of a fuse?","m":2,"ans":["To protect the circuit by melting and breaking the circuit if the current exceeds a safe limit.","Prevents overheating/fire."]},{"id":"ELE-017","q":"Should a fuse be connected to the Live or Neutral wire?","m":1,"ans":["Live wire.","Live wire."]},{"id":"ELE-018","q":"State the law of electrostatics.","m":1,"ans":["Like charges repel, unlike charges attract.","Repel vs Attract."]},{"id":"ELE-019","q":"What is an 'Electric Field'?","m":2,"ans":["A region in which an electric charge experiences an electric force.","Region of electric force."]},{"id":"ELE-020","q":"What is the direction of electric field lines?","m":1,"ans":["From positive to negative.","+ to -"]},{"id":"ELE-021","q":"State the three magnetic materials.","m":1,"ans":["Iron, Nickel, Cobalt.","Iron, Nickel, Cobalt."]},{"id":"ELE-022","q":"Define 'Hard' vs 'Soft' magnetic materials.","m":2,"ans":["Soft (e.g. Iron): easy to magnetize/demagnetize. Hard (e.g. Steel): hard to magnetize/stays magnetized.","Iron vs Steel."]},{"id":"ELE-023","q":"Which rule is used to find the direction of the magnetic field around a straight wire?","m":1,"ans":["Right-Hand Grip Rule.","Right-hand grip."]},{"id":"ELE-024","q":"State Fleming’s Left-Hand Rule.","m":3,"ans":["Thumb: Force/Motion; First Finger: Field (N to S); Second Finger: Current (+ to -).","Force, Field, Current."]},{"id":"ELE-025","q":"What is the 'Motor Effect'?","m":2,"ans":["The force experienced by a current-carrying conductor placed in a magnetic field.","Force on wire in field."]},{"id":"ELE-026","q":"State two ways to increase the strength of an electromagnet.","m":2,"ans":["1. Increase the current. 2. Increase the number of turns in the coil.","More current/turns."]},{"id":"ELE-027","q":"What is Electromagnetic Induction?","m":2,"ans":["The production of an e.m.f. across a conductor when it experiences a changing magnetic field.","Inducing voltage."]},{"id":"ELE-028","q":"State Faraday’s Law.","m":2,"ans":["The magnitude of the induced e.m.f. is directly proportional to the rate of change of magnetic flux linkage.","e.m.f. $\\propto$ rate of change."]},{"id":"ELE-029","q":"State Lenz’s Law.","m":2,"ans":["The direction of the induced e.m.f. is such that it opposes the change causing it.","Opposition to change."]},{"id":"ELE-030","q":"What is a 'Transformer'?","m":1,"ans":["A device that changes the voltage of an alternating current (A.C.).","Voltage changer."]},{"id":"ELE-031","q":"State the transformer equation.","m":1,"ans":["$V_p / V_s = N_p / N_s$","Primary/Secondary ratio."]},{"id":"ELE-032","q":"Why is electricity transmitted at very high voltages?","m":2,"ans":["To reduce the current, which reduces energy loss as heat ($I^2R$) in the cables.","Efficiency."]},{"id":"ELE-033","q":"What is the difference between A.C. and D.C.?","m":2,"ans":["D.C. flows in one direction only. A.C. reverses direction periodically.","One way vs back-and-forth."]},{"id":"ELE-034","q":"How do you calculate energy ($E$) in a circuit?","m":1,"ans":["$E = VIt$ or $E = Pt$","Power * Time."]},{"id":"ELE-035","q":"Calculate the current if 10 C of charge flows in 2 s.","m":1,"ans":["5 A","10 / 2"]},{"id":"ELE-036","q":"What is a 'Short Circuit'?","m":2,"ans":["A path of very low resistance that allows a large, dangerous current to flow.","Zero-resistance path."]},{"id":"ELE-037","q":"What is the purpose of the Earth wire?","m":2,"ans":["To provide a safe path for current to flow to the ground if the live wire touches the metal casing.","Safety from shock."]},{"id":"ELE-038","q":"Explain 'Double Insulation'.","m":2,"ans":["An appliance with a plastic casing that does not require an Earth wire because the outer case cannot conduct electricity.","Plastic case, no Earth."]},{"id":"ELE-039","q":"How do you demagnetize a magnet using A.C.?","m":2,"ans":["Place the magnet inside a solenoid carrying A.C. and slowly withdraw it.","Withdraw from A.C. coil."]},{"id":"ELE-040","q":"What is a 'Diode'?","m":1,"ans":["A component that allows current to flow in one direction only.","One-way gate."]},{"id":"ELE-041","q":"What is a 'Light Dependent Resistor' (LDR)?","m":2,"ans":["A resistor whose resistance decreases as light intensity increases.","Bright = Low R."]},{"id":"ELE-042","q":"What is a 'Thermistor'?","m":2,"ans":["A resistor whose resistance decreases as temperature increases (NTC type).","Hot = Low R."]},{"id":"ELE-043","q":"Define 'Magnetic Field'.","m":2,"ans":["A region where a magnetic pole experiences a force.","Region of magnetic force."]},{"id":"ELE-044","q":"Where is the magnetic field strongest around a magnet?","m":1,"ans":["At the poles.","The tips/poles."]},{"id":"ELE-045","q":"What is the function of the split-ring commutator in a D.C. motor?","m":2,"ans":["It reverses the direction of current in the coil every half-turn to keep the motor rotating in one direction.","Current reverser."]},{"id":"ELE-046","q":"State the formula for an ideal transformer ($100\\%$ efficient).","m":1,"ans":["$V_p \times I_p = V_s \times I_s$","Power in = Power out."]},{"id":"ELE-047","q":"What is a 'Step-up Transformer'?","m":1,"ans":["A transformer that increases voltage ($N_s > N_p$).","More secondary turns."]},{"id":"ELE-048","q":"What is a 'Step-down Transformer'?","m":1,"ans":["A transformer that decreases voltage ($N_s < N_p$).","Fewer secondary turns."]},{"id":"ELE-049","q":"Why is the core of a transformer laminated?","m":2,"ans":["To reduce energy loss due to eddy currents.","Laminations stop eddy currents."]},{"id":"ELE-050","q":"What material is a transformer core made of and why?","m":2,"ans":["Soft Iron; it is easily magnetized and demagnetized.","Soft Iron."]},{"id":"ELE-051","q":"Calculate the cost of using a 2 kW heater for 3 hours if 1 unit (kWh) costs $0.20.","m":2,"ans":["$1.20","2 * 3 * 0.20"]},{"id":"ELE-052","q":"What happens to the brightness of two bulbs in series if one is removed?","m":1,"ans":["The other bulb goes out.","Broken circuit."]},{"id":"ELE-053","q":"What happens to the brightness of two bulbs in parallel if one is removed?","m":1,"ans":["The other remains lit with the same brightness.","Independent branches."]},{"id":"ELE-054","q":"State the relationship between resistance and length of a wire.","m":1,"ans":["Resistance is directly proportional to length ($R \\propto L$).","Longer = more R."]},{"id":"ELE-055","q":"State the relationship between resistance and cross-sectional area of a wire.","m":1,"ans":["Resistance is inversely proportional to area ($R \\propto 1/A$).","Thicker = less R."]},{"id":"ELE-056","q":"A wire is 2 m long and has 10 $\\Omega$. What is the resistance of a 4 m wire of the same material and thickness?","m":1,"ans":["20 $\\Omega$","Double length = double R."]},{"id":"ELE-057","q":"What is an 'Electric Current' measured in terms of charge and time?","m":1,"ans":["$I = Q/t$","Coulombs per second."]},{"id":"ELE-058","q":"Which wire in a plug is usually at 0V?","m":1,"ans":["Neutral wire.","Neutral."]},{"id":"ELE-059","q":"Define 'Coulomb'.","m":1,"ans":["The unit of electric charge (1 C = 1 A $\times$ 1 s).","Charge unit."]},{"id":"ELE-060","q":"What is the p.d. across a 5 $\\Omega$ resistor with 2 A flowing through it?","m":1,"ans":["10 V","2 * 5"]},{"id":"ELE-061","q":"What is the power of a 240 V appliance drawing 10 A?","m":1,"ans":["2400 W (2.4 kW)","240 * 10"]},{"id":"ELE-062","q":"Name the device used to vary resistance in a circuit.","m":1,"ans":["Rheostat (Variable Resistor).","Rheostat."]},{"id":"ELE-063","q":"What are 'Slip Rings' used for?","m":1,"ans":["Used in an A.C. generator to maintain a constant connection between the coil and external circuit.","A.C. Generator."]},{"id":"ELE-064","q":"How does a generator differ from a motor?","m":2,"ans":["A motor converts electrical to mechanical energy; a generator converts mechanical to electrical energy.","Work in vs Electricity in."]},{"id":"ELE-065","q":"State the direction of the magnetic field inside a solenoid.","m":1,"ans":["From South to North.","S to N inside."]},{"id":"ELE-066","q":"Define 'Relay'.","m":2,"ans":["An electromagnetic switch that uses a small current in one circuit to control a larger current in another.","Electric switch."]},{"id":"ELE-067","q":"Why does a magnet repel another magnet?","m":1,"ans":["Because their like poles are facing each other.","Like poles repel."]},{"id":"ELE-068","q":"What is 'Static Electricity'?","m":1,"ans":["The build-up of electric charge on the surface of an insulator.","Non-moving charge."]},{"id":"ELE-069","q":"How can an insulator be charged?","m":1,"ans":["By friction (rubbing two insulators together).","Friction."]},{"id":"ELE-070","q":"What is 'Charging by Induction'?","m":2,"ans":["Charging a conductor by bringing a charged object near it (without touching) and earthing it.","Induced charge."]},{"id":"ELE-071","q":"State one hazard of static electricity.","m":1,"ans":["Fire/Explosion during refueling / Lightning strikes.","Spark risk."]},{"id":"ELE-072","q":"State one use of static electricity.","m":1,"ans":["Photocopiers / Electrostatic precipitators / Spray painting.","Photocopiers."]},{"id":"ELE-073","q":"Which component is represented by a rectangle with a diagonal arrow?","m":1,"ans":["Variable Resistor (Rheostat).","Rheostat."]},{"id":"ELE-074","q":"What is a 'Potentiometer'?","m":2,"ans":["A variable resistor used as a potential divider to vary output voltage.","Voltage divider."]},{"id":"ELE-075","q":"Calculate $R_T$ for a 10 $\\Omega$ and 20 $\\Omega$ resistor in series.","m":1,"ans":["30 $\\Omega$","10 + 20"]},{"id":"ELE-076","q":"Calculate $R_T$ for two 10 $\\Omega$ resistors in parallel.","m":1,"ans":["5 $\\Omega$","Half of one."]},{"id":"ELE-077","q":"What is the typical mains voltage in most homes (UK/Singapore)?","m":1,"ans":["230V to 240V.","240V"]},{"id":"ELE-078","q":"What is 'Mains Frequency'?","m":1,"ans":["50 Hz","50 Hz"]},{"id":"ELE-079","q":"What happens to the current in a circuit if the resistance is doubled (voltage constant)?","m":1,"ans":["Current is halved.","$I = V/2R$"]},{"id":"ELE-080","q":"Define 'Kilowatt-hour' (kWh).","m":2,"ans":["The energy consumed by a 1 kW appliance in 1 hour.","Commercial unit of energy."]},{"id":"ELE-081","q":"Is a magnetic field line a real physical object?","m":1,"ans":["No, it is a representation to show the direction and strength of the field.","Visual aid."]},{"id":"ELE-082","q":"What is 'Soft Iron' primarily used for in magnetism?","m":1,"ans":["Electromagnet cores / Transformer cores.","Temporary magnets."]},{"id":"ELE-083","q":"Which finger represents the 'Field' in Fleming's rules?","m":1,"ans":["First finger.","First finger."]},{"id":"ELE-084","q":"Which hand is used for generators (induction)?","m":1,"ans":["Right hand (Fleming's Right-Hand Rule).","Right hand."]},{"id":"ELE-085","q":"State the effect of reversing the magnetic field in a motor.","m":1,"ans":["The direction of rotation reverses.","Reversed rotation."]},{"id":"ELE-086","q":"What is 'Induced Magnetism'?","m":1,"ans":["When a magnetic material becomes a magnet when placed in a magnetic field.","Temporary magnetism."]},{"id":"ELE-087","q":"Define 'Potential Divider'.","m":2,"ans":["A circuit consisting of resistors in series to split the input voltage into smaller output voltages.","Voltage splitter."]},{"id":"ELE-088","q":"What happens to LDR resistance in the dark?","m":1,"ans":["It increases significantly.","High R in dark."]},{"id":"ELE-089","q":"What happens to thermistor resistance in the cold?","m":1,"ans":["It increases (for NTC type).","High R when cold."]},{"id":"ELE-090","q":"Why is the Earth wire thicker than the other wires?","m":1,"ans":["To ensure it has very low resistance to carry fault currents safely.","Low R."]},{"id":"ELE-091","q":"Calculate current in a 230V, 460W appliance.","m":1,"ans":["2 A","460 / 230"]},{"id":"ELE-092","q":"What is the result of multiplying p.d. by charge?","m":1,"ans":["Work done (Energy).","$W = VQ$"]},{"id":"ELE-093","q":"What is the rule for current in a series circuit?","m":1,"ans":["Current is the same at all points.","$I_1 = I_2 = I_3$"]},{"id":"ELE-094","q":"What is the rule for voltage in a parallel circuit?","m":1,"ans":["Voltage is the same across all parallel branches.","$V_1 = V_2 = V_3$"]},{"id":"ELE-095","q":"How do you increase the induced e.m.f. in a generator?","m":2,"ans":["1. Spin the coil faster. 2. Use a stronger magnet. 3. More turns in the coil.","Faster/Stronger/More turns."]},{"id":"ELE-096","q":"What is 'Magnetic Flux Linkage'?","m":2,"ans":["The product of the number of turns and the magnetic flux through each turn.","$N\\Phi$"]},{"id":"ELE-097","q":"Does a transformer work with D.C.?","m":1,"ans":["No","Requires a *changing* magnetic field (A.C.)."]},{"id":"ELE-098","q":"What is the purpose of the carbon brushes in a motor?","m":1,"ans":["To provide an electrical connection between the spinning commutator and the stationary power source.","Contact."]},{"id":"ELE-099","q":"What is a 'Circuit Breaker'?","m":2,"ans":["An electromagnetic switch that automatically breaks the circuit if the current is too high; it can be reset.","Resettable fuse."]},{"id":"ELE-100","q":"State the energy conversion in an electric motor.","m":1,"ans":["Electrical Energy $\rightarrow$ Kinetic Energy + Heat.","Electric to KE."]}]},{"chapter":"Kinematics","questions":[{"id":"KINE-001","q":"Define 'acceleration' in terms of velocity.","m":1,"ans":["The rate of change of velocity.","$a = \frac{\\Delta v}{t}$"]},{"id":"KINE-002","q":"A car travels 100 m in 5 s at constant speed. Calculate its velocity.","m":1,"ans":["20 m/s","Distance / Time"]},{"id":"KINE-003","q":"State what the gradient of a distance-time graph represents.","m":1,"ans":["Speed","Rate of change of distance"]},{"id":"KINE-004","q":"Describe the motion of an object when its velocity-time graph is a horizontal line above the x-axis.","m":2,"ans":["The object is moving at a constant velocity.","Zero acceleration / uniform motion."]},{"id":"KINE-005","q":"A ball is thrown vertically upwards. At the maximum height, what is its velocity?","m":1,"ans":["0 m/s","Zero"]},{"id":"KINE-006","q":"Explain why velocity is a vector quantity while speed is a scalar quantity.","m":2,"ans":["Velocity has both magnitude and direction; speed has only magnitude.","Directionality."]},{"id":"KINE-007","q":"What does a constant negative gradient on a velocity-time graph indicate?","m":1,"ans":["Constant deceleration","Uniform negative acceleration"]},{"id":"KINE-008","q":"Calculate the distance traveled by an object moving at 15 m/s for 12 seconds.","m":1,"ans":["180 m","15 * 12"]},{"id":"KINE-009","q":"How is the distance traveled determined from a velocity-time graph?","m":1,"ans":["The area under the graph.","Area of the shape."]},{"id":"KINE-010","q":"Define 'terminal velocity'.","m":2,"ans":["The constant maximum velocity reached by a falling object when air resistance equals weight.","Zero acceleration during fall."]},{"id":"KINE-011","q":"An object accelerates from 5 m/s to 25 m/s in 4 s. Calculate the acceleration.","m":2,"ans":["5 m/s²","(25 - 5) / 4"]},{"id":"KINE-012","q":"On a distance-time graph, what does a curve with an increasing gradient represent?","m":1,"ans":["Increasing speed / Acceleration","Acceleration"]},{"id":"KINE-013","q":"A car decelerates from 30 m/s to 10 m/s at a rate of 2 m/s². Calculate the time taken.","m":2,"ans":["10 s","(30 - 10) / 2"]},{"id":"KINE-014","q":"State the value of acceleration for an object in free fall near the Earth's surface.","m":1,"ans":["9.8 m/s² (or 10 m/s²)","g"]},{"id":"KINE-015","q":"A cyclist travels 12 km in 30 minutes. Calculate the average speed in m/s.","m":3,"ans":["6.67 m/s","12000 / 1800"]},{"id":"KINE-016","q":"Describe the motion shown by a horizontal line on a distance-time graph.","m":1,"ans":["The object is at rest / stationary.","Zero speed."]},{"id":"KINE-017","q":"Why does a feather fall more slowly than a hammer in air?","m":2,"ans":["Air resistance acts more significantly on the feather due to its lower weight/larger surface area ratio.","Drag force vs weight."]},{"id":"KINE-018","q":"An object starts from rest and moves with an acceleration of 3 m/s² for 5 s. Find its final velocity.","m":2,"ans":["15 m/s","0 + (3 * 5)"]},{"id":"KINE-019","q":"What is meant by 'deceleration'?","m":1,"ans":["The rate of decrease in velocity.","Negative acceleration."]},{"id":"KINE-020","q":"A runner completes a circular track of 400 m and returns to the starting point. State the displacement.","m":1,"ans":["0 m","Zero displacement"]},{"id":"KINE-021","q":"If the gradient of a velocity-time graph is zero, what is the acceleration?","m":1,"ans":["0 m/s²","Zero"]},{"id":"KINE-022","q":"An object moves with a constant speed of 10 m/s in a circle. Is it accelerating?","m":2,"ans":["Yes, because its direction is changing, which means its velocity is changing.","Centripetal acceleration."]},{"id":"KINE-023","q":"Calculate the average speed of a car that travels 50 km in 45 minutes.","m":2,"ans":["66.7 km/h","50 / 0.75"]},{"id":"KINE-024","q":"In a vacuum, do all objects fall with the same acceleration?","m":1,"ans":["Yes","Acceleration is independent of mass in vacuum."]},{"id":"KINE-025","q":"What does a straight line passing through the origin on a distance-time graph indicate?","m":1,"ans":["Constant speed","Uniform velocity"]},{"id":"KINE-026","q":"A ball is dropped from a height. Describe its velocity as it falls (ignoring air resistance).","m":2,"ans":["Velocity increases at a constant rate.","Uniform acceleration."]},{"id":"KINE-027","q":"Define 'average speed'.","m":1,"ans":["Total distance divided by total time.","$v = \frac{D_{total}}{t_{total}}$"]},{"id":"KINE-028","q":"A velocity-time graph is a triangle with base 10 s and height 20 m/s. Find the total distance.","m":2,"ans":["100 m","0.5 * 10 * 20"]},{"id":"KINE-029","q":"What is the SI unit for acceleration?","m":1,"ans":["m/s²","meters per second squared"]},{"id":"KINE-030","q":"If an object is moving at constant velocity, what is the resultant force acting on it?","m":1,"ans":["0 N","Zero"]},{"id":"KINE-031","q":"Describe the shape of a distance-time graph for an object moving with constant acceleration.","m":1,"ans":["A curve (parabola).","Increasing gradient curve."]},{"id":"KINE-032","q":"A train travels at 60 m/s and comes to a stop in 20 s. Calculate the deceleration.","m":2,"ans":["3 m/s²","60 / 20"]},{"id":"KINE-033","q":"Define 'displacement'.","m":1,"ans":["Distance moved in a specific direction.","Vector distance."]},{"id":"KINE-034","q":"What does the area under an acceleration-time graph represent?","m":1,"ans":["Change in velocity.","$\\Delta v$"]},{"id":"KINE-035","q":"A car travels 30 km at 60 km/h and then 30 km at 40 km/h. Calculate the average speed for the whole journey.","m":3,"ans":["48 km/h","60 / (0.5 + 0.75)"]},{"id":"KINE-036","q":"Explain why the acceleration of a skydivier decreases as they fall before reaching terminal velocity.","m":3,"ans":["As speed increases, air resistance increases. The resultant force (Weight - Air Resistance) decreases, hence acceleration ($a=F/m$) decreases.","Drag increases, net force drops."]},{"id":"KINE-037","q":"An object moves 4 m North and then 3 m East. Find the magnitude of displacement.","m":2,"ans":["5 m","$\\sqrt{4^2 + 3^2}$"]},{"id":"KINE-038","q":"State the gradient of a displacement-time graph.","m":1,"ans":["Velocity","Velocity"]},{"id":"KINE-039","q":"What happens to the acceleration of a ball thrown upwards while it is in the air?","m":1,"ans":["It remains constant (9.8 m/s² downwards).","Constant g."]},{"id":"KINE-040","q":"A car covers 200 m in 20 s. It starts from rest and accelerates uniformly. Find the acceleration.","m":3,"ans":["1.0 m/s²","$s = 0.5 a t^2 \rightarrow 200 = 0.5 * a * 400$"]},{"id":"KINE-041","q":"On a velocity-time graph, how can you tell if an object is moving backwards?","m":1,"ans":["The velocity values become negative (below the x-axis).","Graph goes below the origin line."]},{"id":"KINE-042","q":"What is the difference between 'uniform' and 'non-uniform' acceleration?","m":2,"ans":["Uniform is constant rate of change of velocity; non-uniform is changing rate.","Constant vs changing a."]},{"id":"KINE-043","q":"An object moves at 5 m/s. After 10 s, it is moving at 5 m/s in the opposite direction. Find the change in velocity.","m":2,"ans":["10 m/s","5 - (-5)"]},{"id":"KINE-044","q":"Describe the motion of an object when the distance-time graph is a curve with a decreasing gradient.","m":2,"ans":["The object is slowing down / decelerating.","Decreasing speed."]},{"id":"KINE-045","q":"A car accelerates from rest at 2 m/s². How far does it travel in 10 s?","m":2,"ans":["100 m","0.5 * 2 * 10^2"]},{"id":"KINE-046","q":"What is the gradient of a horizontal line on a velocity-time graph?","m":1,"ans":["0","Zero acceleration"]},{"id":"KINE-047","q":"Identify the scalar: velocity, displacement, acceleration, speed.","m":1,"ans":["speed","speed"]},{"id":"KINE-048","q":"A stone is thrown downwards with an initial velocity of 5 m/s. What is its velocity after 2 s? ($g=10$ m/s²)","m":2,"ans":["25 m/s","5 + (10 * 2)"]},{"id":"KINE-049","q":"How can you find the instantaneous speed from a curved distance-time graph?","m":2,"ans":["Draw a tangent to the curve at that point and calculate its gradient.","Slope of tangent."]},{"id":"KINE-050","q":"If two objects of different masses are dropped in a vacuum, which hits the ground first?","m":1,"ans":["Both hit at the same time.","Same time."]},{"id":"KINE-051","q":"A velocity-time graph shows a trapezoid. Parallel sides are 10 s and 15 s, height is 20 m/s. Calculate distance.","m":3,"ans":["250 m","0.5 * (10 + 15) * 20"]},{"id":"KINE-052","q":"What is meant by 'instantaneous velocity'?","m":1,"ans":["The velocity of an object at a specific point in time.","Velocity at a moment."]},{"id":"KINE-053","q":"An object is moving at constant velocity. What is the gradient of its distance-time graph?","m":1,"ans":["Constant","A straight line slope."]},{"id":"KINE-054","q":"Calculate the time taken for a car to travel 1.5 km at 25 m/s.","m":2,"ans":["60 s","1500 / 25"]},{"id":"KINE-055","q":"State the two components required to describe a vector completely.","m":1,"ans":["Magnitude and Direction.","Size and direction."]},{"id":"KINE-056","q":"Does a negative acceleration always mean an object is slowing down?","m":2,"ans":["No, it could mean it is speeding up in the negative direction.","Speeding up backwards."]},{"id":"KINE-057","q":"A ball bounces. Describe the change in velocity at the moment of impact.","m":2,"ans":["Velocity changes direction rapidly (from downwards to upwards).","Sudden reversal of velocity."]},{"id":"KINE-058","q":"What is the initial velocity of an object that starts 'from rest'?","m":1,"ans":["0 m/s","Zero"]},{"id":"KINE-059","q":"On a distance-time graph, a steeper slope means...","m":1,"ans":["Higher speed","Faster motion"]},{"id":"KINE-060","q":"A car travels 60 m while accelerating from 10 m/s to 20 m/s. Find the time taken.","m":3,"ans":["4 s","$s = 0.5(u+v)t \rightarrow 60 = 0.5(30)t$"]},{"id":"KINE-061","q":"What does it mean if the area under a v-t graph is zero?","m":2,"ans":["The object has returned to its starting position (displacement is zero).","Net displacement = 0."]},{"id":"KINE-062","q":"Define 'speed'.","m":1,"ans":["Distance traveled per unit time.","$s = d/t$"]},{"id":"KINE-063","q":"How does air resistance change as the speed of a falling object increases?","m":1,"ans":["It increases.","Increases."]},{"id":"KINE-064","q":"An object moves 5 m East, 5 m North, 5 m West. State the final displacement from start.","m":1,"ans":["5 m North","5 m N"]},{"id":"KINE-065","q":"If acceleration is constant, the velocity-time graph is a...","m":1,"ans":["Straight line","Linear slope"]},{"id":"KINE-066","q":"A car travels 500 m in 25 s. Calculate its speed.","m":1,"ans":["20 m/s","500 / 25"]},{"id":"KINE-067","q":"Describe the motion of a body falling with constant acceleration.","m":1,"ans":["Velocity increases uniformly.","Constant rate of speed increase."]},{"id":"KINE-068","q":"What is the acceleration of an object at terminal velocity?","m":1,"ans":["0 m/s²","Zero"]},{"id":"KINE-069","q":"A bullet is fired horizontally. Neglecting air resistance, what is its vertical acceleration?","m":1,"ans":["9.8 m/s² (downwards)","g"]},{"id":"KINE-070","q":"Can an object have a constant speed and a changing velocity?","m":1,"ans":["Yes (e.g. circular motion).","Yes"]},{"id":"KINE-071","q":"Calculate the deceleration of a car that stops from 15 m/s in 30 m.","m":3,"ans":["3.75 m/s²","$v^2 = u^2 + 2as \rightarrow 0 = 15^2 + 2a(30)$"]},{"id":"KINE-072","q":"How do you calculate the gradient of a straight line graph?","m":1,"ans":["Rise over run / $(y_2 - y_1) / (x_2 - x_1)$","Change in y / change in x"]},{"id":"KINE-073","q":"On a v-t graph, a line sloping downwards towards the x-axis represents...","m":1,"ans":["Deceleration","Slowing down"]},{"id":"KINE-074","q":"A cyclist moves at 4 m/s for 10 s and then 8 m/s for 5 s. Find total distance.","m":2,"ans":["80 m","(4 * 10) + (8 * 5)"]},{"id":"KINE-075","q":"What is the unit for speed?","m":1,"ans":["m/s","meters per second"]},{"id":"KINE-076","q":"State the difference between distance and displacement.","m":2,"ans":["Distance is total path length (scalar); displacement is change in position (vector).","Scalar vs vector distance."]},{"id":"KINE-077","q":"If velocity is 0, must acceleration be 0?","m":2,"ans":["No (e.g. at the top of a ball's flight).","No"]},{"id":"KINE-078","q":"A car travels 100 km in 2 hours. Calculate speed in km/h.","m":1,"ans":["50 km/h","100 / 2"]},{"id":"KINE-079","q":"Describe the velocity-time graph for an object at rest.","m":1,"ans":["A horizontal line on the x-axis ($v=0$).","Line at y=0."]},{"id":"KINE-080","q":"What is the weight of a 5 kg mass? ($g=10$ N/kg)","m":1,"ans":["50 N","5 * 10"]},{"id":"KINE-081","q":"Define 'uniform velocity'.","m":1,"ans":["Constant speed in a constant direction.","No change in velocity."]},{"id":"KINE-082","q":"How many meters are in 5 kilometers?","m":1,"ans":["5000 m","5000"]},{"id":"KINE-083","q":"On a distance-time graph, what does a flat line mean?","m":1,"ans":["The object is not moving.","Stationary."]},{"id":"KINE-084","q":"An object falls 20 m in 2 s starting from rest. Calculate its acceleration.","m":3,"ans":["10 m/s²","$s = 0.5 a t^2 \rightarrow 20 = 0.5 * a * 4$"]},{"id":"KINE-085","q":"Which of these is a vector: mass, temperature, force, time?","m":1,"ans":["force","force"]},{"id":"KINE-086","q":"Calculate the speed of a sound wave that travels 1500 m in 5 s.","m":1,"ans":["300 m/s","1500 / 5"]},{"id":"KINE-087","q":"In a d-t graph, a steeper line means the object is moving...","m":1,"ans":["Faster","At a higher speed"]},{"id":"KINE-088","q":"What is the acceleration of a car moving at constant velocity of 100 km/h?","m":1,"ans":["0 m/s²","Zero"]},{"id":"KINE-089","q":"A stone is dropped. What is its velocity after 1 s? ($g=10$ m/s²)","m":1,"ans":["10 m/s","v = gt"]},{"id":"KINE-090","q":"Convert 10 m/s to km/h.","m":2,"ans":["36 km/h","10 * 3600 / 1000"]},{"id":"KINE-091","q":"What does a line with a decreasing gradient on a v-t graph show?","m":1,"ans":["Decreasing acceleration","Non-uniform acceleration"]},{"id":"KINE-092","q":"An object moves 3 m North and 4 m West. What is the total distance?","m":1,"ans":["7 m","3 + 4"]},{"id":"KINE-093","q":"What is the name for the 'change in velocity divided by time'?","m":1,"ans":["Acceleration","Acceleration"]},{"id":"KINE-094","q":"If a graph of distance against time is a curve, the speed is...","m":1,"ans":["Changing","Not constant"]},{"id":"KINE-095","q":"A car travels at 20 m/s for 1 minute. Find the distance.","m":1,"ans":["1200 m","20 * 60"]},{"id":"KINE-096","q":"State the direction of acceleration for an object slowing down while moving North.","m":1,"ans":["South","Opposite to motion"]},{"id":"KINE-097","q":"What is the average velocity for a round trip?","m":1,"ans":["0 m/s","Zero (displacement is zero)"]},{"id":"KINE-098","q":"On a v-t graph, what indicates an object is at rest?","m":1,"ans":["The line is on the time axis ($v=0$).","v = 0 line."]},{"id":"KINE-099","q":"Does air resistance act on a stationary object?","m":1,"ans":["No","No (v must be > 0)"]},{"id":"KINE-100","q":"A ball is thrown up and caught. Compare the time taken to go up and the time to come down (no air resistance).","m":1,"ans":["They are equal.","Time up = Time down"]}]},{"chapter":"Magnetism Electricity ElectroMagnetism","questions":[{"id":"Static_Electricity_1","q":"Explain, in terms of electron transfer, how a plastic rod becomes negatively charged when rubbed with a cloth.","m":3,"ans":["Friction between the rod and cloth causes electrons to move.","Electrons are transferred from the cloth to the rod.","The rod now has an excess of electrons."]},{"id":"Electric_Fields_2","q":"Define an electric field.","m":1,"ans":["A region in which an electric charge experiences a force."]},{"id":"Current_&_Charge_3","q":"A current of 0.5 A flows through a lamp for 2 minutes. Calculate the total charge that passes through.","m":1,"ans":["Q = It = 0.5 * 120 = 60 C."]},{"id":"Potential_Difference_4","q":"Define potential difference (p.d.) in terms of energy and charge.","m":1,"ans":["The work done per unit charge in driving charges through a component."]},{"id":"Resistance_5","q":"A wire has a resistance of 10 Ω. What is the resistance of a wire of the same material that is twice as long and has half the cross-sectional area?","m":2,"ans":["R is proportional to L/A.","New R = 10 * (2 / 0.5) = 40 Ω."]},{"id":"Ohm's_Law_6","q":"Sketch the I-V characteristic for a filament lamp and explain its shape.","m":2,"ans":["Graph: A curve with decreasing gradient.","Explanation: As current increases, temperature increases, causing resistance to increase."]},{"id":"Series_Circuits_7","q":"Three 6 Ω resistors are connected in series to a 12V battery. Calculate the current in the circuit.","m":2,"ans":["Total R = 6 + 6 + 6 = 18 Ω.","I = V/R = 12 / 18 = 0.67 A."]},{"id":"Parallel_Circuits_8","q":"Calculate the combined resistance of two resistors, 4 Ω and 12 Ω, connected in parallel.","m":2,"ans":["1/Rt = 1/4 + 1/12 = 3/12 + 1/12 = 4/12.","Rt = 12 / 4 = 3 Ω."]},{"id":"Electrical_Power_9","q":"An electric kettle is rated at 2.3 kW, 230 V. Calculate the correct fuse rating (3A, 5A, or 13A) to use.","m":2,"ans":["I = P/V = 2300 / 230 = 10 A.","The 13A fuse is the most appropriate."]},{"id":"Energy_Calculation_10","q":"Calculate the energy dissipated by a 100 Ω resistor when a current of 2 A flows through it for 30 seconds.","m":1,"ans":["E = I²Rt = (2²) * 100 * 30 = 4 * 100 * 30 = 12,000 J."]},{"id":"Magnetism_11","q":"How can a permanent magnet be demagnetized using alternating current (a.c.)?","m":2,"ans":["Place the magnet inside a solenoid connected to an a.c. supply.","Slowly withdraw the magnet far away from the solenoid while the current is still on."]},{"id":"Magnetic_Fields_12","q":"Draw the magnetic field pattern around a straight current-carrying wire.","m":2,"ans":["Concentric circles around the wire.","Spacing between circles increases as distance from wire increases."]},{"id":"Electromagnets_13","q":"List two ways to increase the strength of an electromagnet.","m":2,"ans":["Increase the current flowing through the coil.","Increase the number of turns in the solenoid (or add a soft iron core)."]},{"id":"Motor_Effect_14","q":"A wire carrying current into the page is placed in a magnetic field acting from left to right. Determine the direction of the force.","m":1,"ans":["Using Fleming’s Left-Hand Rule: Force is acting downwards."]},{"id":"D.C._Motor_15","q":"What is the function of the split-ring commutator in a d.c. motor?","m":2,"ans":["It reverses the direction of the current in the coil every half-turn.","This ensures the torque remains in the same direction so the coil continues to rotate."]},{"id":"Electromagnetic_Induction_16","q":"State Faraday’s Law of electromagnetic induction.","m":1,"ans":["The magnitude of the induced e.m.f. is directly proportional to the rate of change of magnetic flux linkage."]},{"id":"Lenz's_Law_17","q":"The North pole of a magnet is pushed into a solenoid. Describe the polarity of the end of the solenoid facing the magnet.","m":2,"ans":["It becomes a North pole.","This creates a repulsive force to oppose the motion of the magnet (Lenz's Law)."]},{"id":"Transformers_18","q":"A transformer has 200 turns on the primary coil and 1000 turns on the secondary. If 240 V a.c. is applied to the primary, find the output voltage.","m":2,"ans":["Vs/Vp = Ns/Np => Vs/240 = 1000/200.","Vs = 240 * 5 = 1200 V."]},{"id":"Transformer_Efficiency_19","q":"Why is a transformer's core made of laminated soft iron?","m":2,"ans":["Soft iron: Easily magnetized and demagnetized to reduce hysteresis loss.","Laminated: To reduce energy loss from eddy currents."]},{"id":"Power_Transmission_20","q":"Explain why electricity is transmitted at very high voltages over long distances.","m":2,"ans":["High voltage means low current for the same power ($P=VI$).","Lower current reduces energy lost as heat in cables ($P=I^2R$)."]},{"id":"Potential_Dividers_21","q":"In a potential divider circuit, an LDR is used. What happens to the output voltage across the LDR as light intensity increases?","m":2,"ans":["Resistance of LDR decreases.","The share of voltage across the LDR decreases, so V-out decreases."]},{"id":"Logic_Gates_22","q":"A logic circuit has two inputs, A and B. The output is 1 only when both A and B are 1. Name the gate.","m":1,"ans":["AND gate."]},{"id":"Cathode_Rays_23","q":"In a Cathode Ray Oscilloscope (CRO), what is the function of the hot filament?","m":1,"ans":["To emit electrons via thermionic emission."]},{"id":"Magnetism_24","q":"Explain why steel is used for permanent magnets while soft iron is used for electromagnets.","m":2,"ans":["Steel is a hard magnetic material (retains magnetism).","Soft iron is a soft magnetic material (loses magnetism easily)."]},{"id":"Induction_25","q":"An a.c. generator's coil is rotated twice as fast. State two changes to the output voltage.","m":2,"ans":["The peak voltage doubles.","The frequency of the a.c. doubles."]},{"id":"Electric_Fields_26","q":"Draw the field lines between two oppositely charged parallel plates.","m":2,"ans":["Parallel, equally spaced straight lines from positive to negative.","Edges show slight 'fringing' (curving outwards)."]},{"id":"Circuit_Components_27","q":"What is the purpose of a diode in a circuit?","m":1,"ans":["To allow current to flow in one direction only."]},{"id":"Safety_28","q":"Why is the fuse always placed in the live wire rather than the neutral wire?","m":1,"ans":["If a fault occurs, the fuse blows and disconnects the high-voltage live wire from the appliance, making it safe."]},{"id":"Electrostatics_29","q":"A gold-leaf electroscope is charged. What happens to the leaf when a rod with the same charge is brought near the cap?","m":1,"ans":["The divergence of the gold leaf increases due to increased repulsion."]},{"id":"Resistance_30","q":"Define 1 Ohm.","m":1,"ans":["The resistance of a conductor such that a potential difference of 1 Volt across it produces a current of 1 Ampere."]},{"id":"Internal_Resistance_31","q":"Explain why the terminal p.d. of a battery drops when it starts supplying a high current.","m":1,"ans":["Some voltage is 'lost' across the internal resistance of the battery ($V = E - Ir$)."]},{"id":"Magnetism_32","q":"Which direction do magnetic field lines point outside a magnet?","m":1,"ans":["From North pole to South pole."]},{"id":"The_Motor_Rule_33","q":"In Fleming’s Left-Hand Rule, what do the thumb and first finger represent?","m":2,"ans":["Thumb: Direction of Motion/Force.","First Finger: Direction of Magnetic Field."]},{"id":"Transformer_Power_34","q":"An ideal transformer has $P_{in} = 100W$. If $V_s = 20V$, find $I_s$.","m":2,"ans":["For ideal transformer, $P_{out} = P_{in} = 100W$.","$I_s = P/V = 100/20 = 5A$."]},{"id":"Thermistor_35","q":"How does the resistance of an NTC thermistor change as temperature increases?","m":1,"ans":["Resistance decreases."]},{"id":"Logic_Gates_36","q":"Draw the truth table for a NOT gate.","m":1,"ans":["Input 0 -> Output 1; Input 1 -> Output 0."]},{"id":"Induction_37","q":"State the energy conversion in an a.c. generator.","m":1,"ans":["Mechanical (Kinetic) energy to Electrical energy."]},{"id":"Relays_38","q":"What is the primary use of a relay in a circuit?","m":1,"ans":["To use a small current circuit to switch on/off a circuit carrying a much larger current."]},{"id":"D.C._Motor_39","q":"List three ways to increase the speed of rotation of a d.c. motor.","m":3,"ans":["Increase the current.","Use a stronger magnetic field.","Increase the number of turns in the coil."]},{"id":"Charge_40","q":"How many electrons make up 1 Coulomb of charge? ($e = 1.6 \times 10^{-19} C$)","m":1,"ans":["$n = 1 / (1.6 \times 10^{-19}) = 6.25 \times 10^{18}$ electrons."]},{"id":"Resistance_41","q":"A wire is stretched to double its length. Assuming density and volume remain constant, what happens to resistance?","m":2,"ans":["Length doubles and Area halves.","Resistance increases by 4 times."]},{"id":"Circuit_Analysis_42","q":"Two resistors in parallel (R and R) are in series with a third resistor R. Find total resistance.","m":2,"ans":["Parallel part = 0.5R.","Total = 0.5R + R = 1.5R."]},{"id":"Earthing_43","q":"What is the purpose of the earth wire in a three-pin plug?","m":1,"ans":["Provides a low-resistance path to the ground if the live wire touches the metal casing, preventing electric shock."]},{"id":"Magnetic_Effect_44","q":"State the rule used to find the direction of magnetic field around a solenoid.","m":1,"ans":["Right-Hand Grip Rule."]},{"id":"Induction_45","q":"Does a transformer work with a d.c. input? Explain.","m":1,"ans":["No. Induction requires a *changing* magnetic field, which is only provided by a.c."]},{"id":"Digital_Electronics_46","q":"Explain the difference between an analogue and a digital signal.","m":2,"ans":["Analogue: Continuously variable voltage.","Digital: Only two discrete states (High/1 or Low/0)."]},{"id":"Potential_Divider_47","q":"A 10V battery is across two resistors, 2kΩ and 8kΩ. Find the voltage across the 8kΩ resistor.","m":1,"ans":["$V = (8 / (2+8)) * 10 = 8V$."]},{"id":"Magnetism_48","q":"Where is the magnetic field strongest in a bar magnet?","m":1,"ans":["At the poles."]},{"id":"Energy_49","q":"A 12V car battery stores 50Ah of charge. Calculate energy in Joules.","m":2,"ans":["$Q = 50 \times 3600 = 180,000 C$.","$E = QV = 180,000 \times 12 = 2.16 \times 10^6 J$."]},{"id":"Lenz's_Law_50","q":"Lenz's Law is a consequence of which conservation law?","m":1,"ans":["Law of Conservation of Energy."]}]},{"chapter":"Measurements Kinematics Basics","questions":[{"id":"C1-001","q":"State the SI base units of density.","m":1,"ans":["kg/m³","kg m⁻³"]},{"id":"C1-002","q":"A micrometer screw gauge has 50 divisions on its thimble and a pitch of 0.5 mm. Calculate the distance moved by the spindle when the thimble is rotated through one division.","m":2,"ans":["0.01 mm","0.5 / 50"]},{"id":"C1-003","q":"Distinguish between a vector and a scalar quantity.","m":2,"ans":["A vector has both magnitude and direction, whereas a scalar has only magnitude.","Vector: magnitude + direction; Scalar: magnitude only."]},{"id":"C1-004","q":"Identify which of the following is a base quantity: velocity, acceleration, electric current, force.","m":1,"ans":["Electric current","Current"]},{"id":"C1-005","q":"A student measures the thickness of 50 sheets of paper as 0.50 cm. Calculate the thickness of one sheet in micrometers (µm).","m":3,"ans":["100 µm","1. 0.50/50 = 0.01 cm. 2. 0.01 cm = 0.1 mm. 3. 0.1 mm = 100 µm."]},{"id":"C1-006","q":"State the purpose of the ratchet in a micrometer screw gauge.","m":1,"ans":["To apply a constant/uniform pressure on the object and prevent over-tightening.","Uniform pressure/prevent damage."]},{"id":"C1-007","q":"A stopwatch has a zero error where it starts at 0.15 s. The recorded time for an event is 12.45 s. Calculate the actual time.","m":1,"ans":["12.30 s","12.45 - 0.15"]},{"id":"C1-008","q":"Describe how to check for zero error in Vernier callipers.","m":2,"ans":["Close the jaws fully and check if the zero on the vernier scale coincides with the zero on the main scale.","Check alignment of zeros when jaws are closed."]},{"id":"C1-009","q":"Calculate the volume of a sphere of radius 3.0 cm in m³. (Take $\\pi = 3.14$)","m":3,"ans":["$1.13 \times 10^{-4}$ m³","$V = \frac{4}{3} \\pi r^3$ and conversion to m³."]},{"id":"C1-010","q":"Why is a pendulum's period measured over 20 oscillations instead of one?","m":2,"ans":["To reduce the effect of human reaction time on the measurement and improve reliability.","Minimize reaction time error."]},{"id":"C1-011","q":"A force of 3.0 N acts due North and a force of 4.0 N acts due East. Determine the magnitude of the resultant force.","m":2,"ans":["5.0 N","$\\sqrt{3^2 + 4^2}$"]},{"id":"C1-012","q":"State the prefix and power of ten for 'mega'.","m":1,"ans":["M and $10^6$","M, 1,000,000"]},{"id":"C1-013","q":"Which instrument is best for measuring the internal diameter of a small glass ring?","m":1,"ans":["Vernier callipers","Inside jaws of vernier callipers"]},{"id":"C1-014","q":"Express 0.000045 N in standard form.","m":1,"ans":["$4.5 \times 10^{-5}$ N","4.5e-5"]},{"id":"C1-015","q":"Convert 72 km/h to m/s.","m":2,"ans":["20 m/s","72000 / 3600"]},{"id":"C1-016","q":"What is the sensitivity of a ruler with 1 mm divisions?","m":1,"ans":["1 mm","0.1 cm"]},{"id":"C1-017","q":"State the SI base unit for thermodynamic temperature.","m":1,"ans":["Kelvin","K"]},{"id":"C1-018","q":"A displacement-time graph is used to find velocity. Is velocity a scalar or a vector?","m":1,"ans":["Vector","Vector quantity"]},{"id":"C1-019","q":"Describe how to avoid parallax error when reading a measuring cylinder.","m":2,"ans":["Position the eye level with the bottom of the meniscus.","Read at eye level/meniscus bottom."]},{"id":"C1-020","q":"A vector diagram shows an arrow of 4 cm representing 20 N. What is the scale of the diagram?","m":1,"ans":["1 cm = 5 N","5 N/cm"]},{"id":"C1-021","q":"Name the base quantity associated with the unit 'mole'.","m":1,"ans":["Amount of substance","Amount"]},{"id":"C1-022","q":"Calculate the area of a circle with a diameter of 10 cm in m².","m":3,"ans":["$7.85 \times 10^{-3}$ m²","$\\pi \times 0.05^2$"]},{"id":"C1-023","q":"Define the 'period' of a pendulum.","m":1,"ans":["The time taken for one complete oscillation.","Time for one full swing"]},{"id":"C1-024","q":"A micrometer reads 3.50 mm on the main scale and 12 on the thimble. There is no zero error. State the reading.","m":1,"ans":["3.62 mm","3.5 + 0.12"]},{"id":"C1-025","q":"What is the symbol and value for the prefix 'pico'?","m":1,"ans":["p and $10^{-12}$","p, 10^-12"]},{"id":"C1-026","q":"How many significant figures are in 0.05020?","m":1,"ans":["4","four"]},{"id":"C1-027","q":"Convert 1.5 g/cm³ into kg/m³.","m":1,"ans":["1500 kg/m³","1.5 * 1000"]},{"id":"C1-028","q":"If the length of a pendulum is quadrupled, what happens to its period?","m":2,"ans":["The period doubles.","$T \\propto \\sqrt{L}$"]},{"id":"C1-029","q":"Identify the scalar from this list: Weight, Momentum, Energy, Acceleration.","m":1,"ans":["Energy","Energy"]},{"id":"C1-030","q":"What is the SI unit for the 'moment of a force'?","m":1,"ans":["Newton-metre","N m"]},{"id":"C1-031","q":"A stone is dropped into a measuring cylinder containing 20 cm³ of water. The level rises to 34 cm³. If the mass is 42 g, find the density.","m":2,"ans":["3.0 g/cm³","42 / (34-20)"]},{"id":"C1-032","q":"How do you calculate the resultant of two vectors acting in opposite directions?","m":1,"ans":["Subtract the smaller magnitude from the larger magnitude.","Find the difference."]},{"id":"C1-033","q":"Express 5.2 GPa in Pascals.","m":1,"ans":["$5.2 \times 10^9$ Pa","5,200,000,000 Pa"]},{"id":"C1-034","q":"What is the precision of a standard vernier calliper?","m":1,"ans":["0.1 mm","0.01 cm"]},{"id":"C1-035","q":"Define 'mass'.","m":1,"ans":["The amount of matter in a body.","Measure of inertia"]},{"id":"C1-036","q":"Why is it important to use a fiduciary marker when timing a pendulum?","m":2,"ans":["To provide a clear reference point to accurately count oscillations at the equilibrium position.","Reference point for counting."]},{"id":"C1-037","q":"Convert 0.5 mm² to m².","m":2,"ans":["$5.0 \times 10^{-7}$ m²","0.5 / 1,000,000"]},{"id":"C1-038","q":"State the number of base quantities in the SI system.","m":1,"ans":["7","seven"]},{"id":"C1-039","q":"Calculate the displacement of a person walking 10 m North and 10 m South.","m":1,"ans":["0 m","zero"]},{"id":"C1-040","q":"Which prefix represents $10^{-3}$?","m":1,"ans":["milli","m"]},{"id":"C1-041","q":"A measuring cylinder has a scale where the smallest division is 2 cm³. State the uncertainty in a single reading.","m":1,"ans":["$\\pm 1$ cm³","1 cm³"]},{"id":"C1-042","q":"Describe the difference between mass and weight.","m":2,"ans":["Mass is a scalar/constant amount of matter; weight is a vector/force due to gravity.","Mass is constant; weight changes with gravity."]},{"id":"C1-043","q":"Calculate the magnitude of the resultant of two 10 N forces acting at $90^\\circ$ to each other.","m":2,"ans":["14.1 N","$\\sqrt{10^2 + 10^2}$"]},{"id":"C1-044","q":"State the symbol for the prefix 'nano'.","m":1,"ans":["n","n"]},{"id":"C1-045","q":"If a force is 50 N and the scale is 1 cm = 10 N, how long is the vector arrow?","m":1,"ans":["5 cm","50 / 10"]},{"id":"C1-046","q":"Which instrument is most precise for measuring the diameter of a wire?","m":1,"ans":["Micrometer screw gauge","Micrometer"]},{"id":"C1-047","q":"Convert 1 hour into seconds.","m":1,"ans":["3600 s","60 * 60"]},{"id":"C1-048","q":"Define 'weight'.","m":1,"ans":["The gravitational force acting on an object.","$W = mg$"]},{"id":"C1-049","q":"A student measures the diameter of a ball using two wooden blocks and a ruler. Why?","m":2,"ans":["To ensure the diameter is measured between two parallel surfaces and avoid parallax.","Accuracy/align with ruler scale."]},{"id":"C1-050","q":"State the prefix for $10^{-2}$.","m":1,"ans":["centi","c"]},{"id":"C1-051","q":"What is the volume of a cube of side 2 cm in m³?","m":2,"ans":["$8 \times 10^{-6}$ m³","$0.02^3$"]},{"id":"C1-052","q":"Identify the base unit for luminous intensity.","m":1,"ans":["candela","cd"]},{"id":"C1-053","q":"State one advantage of a digital stopwatch over an analog one.","m":1,"ans":["Higher precision/easier to read.","Reads to 0.01 s."]},{"id":"C1-054","q":"Is 'Pressure' a scalar or a vector in the O Level syllabus?","m":1,"ans":["Scalar","Scalar"]},{"id":"C1-055","q":"What happens to the density of a gas when it is compressed at constant temperature?","m":1,"ans":["Increases","Density increases"]},{"id":"C1-056","q":"Calculate the time in seconds for 2.5 minutes.","m":1,"ans":["150 s","2.5 * 60"]},{"id":"C1-057","q":"A vernier calliper shows the 4th mark on the vernier scale coinciding with a main scale mark. The main scale reading is 2.1 cm. State the total reading.","m":1,"ans":["2.14 cm","2.1 + 0.04"]},{"id":"C1-058","q":"What is the value of the prefix 'deci'?","m":1,"ans":["$10^{-1}$","0.1"]},{"id":"C1-059","q":"Which physical quantity has the same unit as 'work'?","m":1,"ans":["Energy","Joules"]},{"id":"C1-060","q":"Define 'significant figures'.","m":1,"ans":["The digits in a measurement that carry meaning contributing to its precision.","Meaningful digits"]},{"id":"C1-061","q":"A micrometer has a zero error of -0.03 mm. The reading is 5.25 mm. What is the correct reading?","m":1,"ans":["5.28 mm","5.25 - (-0.03)"]},{"id":"C1-062","q":"Convert 1000 cm³ to m³.","m":1,"ans":["$10^{-3}$ m³","0.001 m³"]},{"id":"C1-063","q":"Which quantity is a vector: Distance, Speed, Displacement, Time?","m":1,"ans":["Displacement","Displacement"]},{"id":"C1-064","q":"State the power of ten for 'micro'.","m":1,"ans":["$10^{-6}$","10^-6"]},{"id":"C1-065","q":"Calculate the density of a 100 g object with a volume of 50 cm³.","m":1,"ans":["2.0 g/cm³","100 / 50"]},{"id":"C1-066","q":"What is the SI unit for electric charge?","m":1,"ans":["Coulomb","C"]},{"id":"C1-067","q":"Why is 'length' a base quantity but 'area' is a derived quantity?","m":2,"ans":["Length is fundamental; area is derived from the product of two lengths.","Area = L x L"]},{"id":"C1-068","q":"A student measures the length of a desk as 1.2 m. How many millimeters is this?","m":1,"ans":["1200 mm","1.2 * 1000"]},{"id":"C1-069","q":"What does the gradient of a displacement-time graph represent?","m":1,"ans":["Velocity","Velocity"]},{"id":"C1-070","q":"Identify the prefix for $10^{12}$.","m":1,"ans":["tera","T"]},{"id":"C1-071","q":"How do you find the volume of a small stone that floats?","m":2,"ans":["Use a sinker to submerge it in a measuring cylinder and find the change in volume.","Use a sinker/weighted object."]},{"id":"C1-072","q":"State the SI unit for frequency.","m":1,"ans":["Hertz","Hz"]},{"id":"C1-073","q":"What is a 'zero error'?","m":1,"ans":["A systematic error where an instrument shows a reading when the true value is zero.","Systematic offset at zero."]},{"id":"C1-074","q":"Calculate the resultant of 8 N North and 6 N South.","m":1,"ans":["2 N North","8 - 6 North"]},{"id":"C1-075","q":"Which prefix is larger: kilo or mega?","m":1,"ans":["mega","Mega"]},{"id":"C1-076","q":"Convert 500 nm to meters in standard form.","m":2,"ans":["$5.0 \times 10^{-7}$ m","$500 \times 10^{-9}$"]},{"id":"C1-077","q":"Define 'acceleration'.","m":1,"ans":["The rate of change of velocity.","a = (v-u)/t"]},{"id":"C1-078","q":"What instrument is used to measure the mass of a beaker?","m":1,"ans":["Electronic balance","Beam balance"]},{"id":"C1-079","q":"If a vector arrow is 10 cm and represents 50 km/h, what is the scale?","m":1,"ans":["1 cm = 5 km/h","5 km/h per cm"]},{"id":"C1-080","q":"What is the SI unit for power?","m":1,"ans":["Watt","W"]},{"id":"C1-081","q":"State the prefix for $10^{-15}$.","m":1,"ans":["femto","f"]},{"id":"C1-082","q":"A pendulum bob is released from point A. It takes 0.5 s to reach the opposite point B. What is the period?","m":1,"ans":["1.0 s","0.5 * 2"]},{"id":"C1-083","q":"Is 'Volume' a base quantity?","m":1,"ans":["No","Derived quantity"]},{"id":"C1-084","q":"How many seconds are in a microsecond?","m":1,"ans":["$10^{-6}$ s","one millionth"]},{"id":"C1-085","q":"What is the difference between distance and displacement?","m":2,"ans":["Distance is a scalar (total path); displacement is a vector (change in position).","Scalar vs Vector"]},{"id":"C1-086","q":"Convert 250 mg into grams.","m":1,"ans":["0.25 g","250 / 1000"]},{"id":"C1-087","q":"What represents the direction of a vector in a drawing?","m":1,"ans":["The arrowhead.","Direction of the arrow"]},{"id":"C1-088","q":"State the prefix for $10^9$.","m":1,"ans":["giga","G"]},{"id":"C1-089","q":"Calculate the area of a rectangle 5 mm by 10 mm in cm².","m":2,"ans":["0.5 cm²","0.5 * 1.0"]},{"id":"C1-090","q":"Which quantity is a vector: Mass, Force, Energy, Temperature?","m":1,"ans":["Force","Force"]},{"id":"C1-091","q":"What is the precision of a stopwatch that reads '00:12:45'?","m":1,"ans":["0.01 s","centisecond"]},{"id":"C1-092","q":"Define 'displacement'.","m":1,"ans":["Distance moved in a specified direction.","Vector distance"]},{"id":"C1-093","q":"A micrometer thimble has 50 divisions. If it is turned two full rotations, how far does it move? (Pitch = 0.5 mm)","m":1,"ans":["1.0 mm","0.5 * 2"]},{"id":"C1-094","q":"Is 'velocity' a derived quantity?","m":1,"ans":["Yes","Derived from L/T"]},{"id":"C1-095","q":"How can you check if a ruler is horizontal?","m":1,"ans":["Use a spirit level.","Spirit level"]},{"id":"C1-096","q":"State the symbol for the prefix 'milli'.","m":1,"ans":["m","m"]},{"id":"C1-097","q":"What is the volume of 1 kg of water in liters?","m":1,"ans":["1 L","1.0 liter"]},{"id":"C1-098","q":"Why should you repeat measurements?","m":1,"ans":["To identify and reduce the effect of random errors.","Improve reliability"]},{"id":"C1-099","q":"Identify the base quantity associated with 'kilogram'.","m":1,"ans":["Mass","Mass"]},{"id":"C1-100","q":"Calculate the resultant of two forces, 5 N North and 5 N East.","m":2,"ans":["7.07 N North-East","$\\sqrt{5^2+5^2}$ at 45 degrees"]}]},{"chapter":"Measurements Kinematics","questions":[{"id":"Measurement_1","q":"A micrometer has a negative zero error of 0.04mm. The sleeve reads 7.5mm and thimble 32. Calculate actual diameter.","m":2,"ans":["7.5 + 0.32 = 7.82mm","7.82 - (-0.04) = 7.86mm"]},{"id":"Vectors_2","q":"A plane flies North at 150m/s with a West wind of 40m/s. Find resultant velocity and bearing.","m":3,"ans":["mag = sqrt(150^2 + 40^2) = 155.2m/s","angle = invTan(40/150) = 14.9 deg","Bearing = 345.1 deg"]},{"id":"Kinematics_3","q":"A car accelerates from 10m/s to 30m/s over 200m. Calculate constant acceleration.","m":3,"ans":["v^2 = u^2 + 2as","900 = 100 + 400a","a = 2.0m/s^2"]},{"id":"Density_4","q":"A 200g alloy consists of 60% Metal A (8g/cm3) and 40% Metal B (10g/cm3) by mass. Find alloy density.","m":4,"ans":["Vol A = 120/8 = 15cm3","Vol B = 80/10 = 8cm3","Total Vol = 23cm3","Density = 200/23 = 8.7g/cm3"]},{"id":"Graphs_5","q":"On a displacement-time graph, a curve gets steeper. Describe the motion.","m":2,"ans":["Increasing gradient means increasing velocity","Object is accelerating"]},{"id":"Free_Fall_6","q":"An object falls 45m from rest. Calculate its impact velocity (g=10).","m":2,"ans":["v^2 = 2gs = 2*10*45 = 900","v = 30m/s"]},{"id":"Measurement_7","q":"A pendulum takes 32.4s for 20 swings. Calculate period T to 3 sig figs.","m":1,"ans":["T = 32.4 / 20 = 1.62s"]},{"id":"Dynamics_8","q":"Explain why a falling raindrop stops accelerating.","m":3,"ans":["Air resistance increases with speed","Weight = Air resistance","Resultant force = 0"]},{"id":"Vernier_9","q":"Vernier 0 is left of main scale 0 by 0.03cm. Reading is 2.55cm. Correct it.","m":2,"ans":["Negative error = -0.03cm","2.55 - (-0.03) = 2.58cm"]},{"id":"Speed_10","q":"A runner does 400m in 50s, but starts 2s late. Calculate intended vs actual speed.","m":2,"ans":["Actual = 400/48 = 8.33m/s","Intended = 400/50 = 8.0m/s"]},{"id":"Vectors_11","q":"Resolve a 100N force acting at 30 deg to the horizontal into components.","m":2,"ans":["Fx = 100 cos 30 = 86.6N","Fy = 100 sin 30 = 50N"]},{"id":"Kinematics_12","q":"A ball thrown up at 20m/s. Calculate max height reached.","m":3,"ans":["v^2 = u^2 + 2as","0 = 400 - 20h","h = 20m"]},{"id":"Graphs_13","q":"Area under a velocity-time graph represents what?","m":1,"ans":["Displacement / Distance traveled"]},{"id":"Density_14","q":"A block 2cm x 3cm x 5cm has mass 150g. Find density in kg/m3.","m":3,"ans":["Vol = 30cm3 = 3e-5 m3","Mass = 0.15kg","Density = 5000 kg/m3"]},{"id":"Acceleration_15","q":"Define acceleration in terms of velocity.","m":1,"ans":["Rate of change of velocity"]},{"id":"Measurement_16","q":"Why is a micrometer better than a ruler for wire diameter?","m":1,"ans":["Higher precision / smaller least count (0.01mm vs 1mm)"]},{"id":"Free_Fall_17","q":"Time taken for object to fall 20m in vacuum vs air. Compare.","m":1,"ans":["In air: longer time due to air resistance opposing weight"]},{"id":"Graphs_18","q":"Gradient of a velocity-time graph represents?","m":1,"ans":["Acceleration"]},{"id":"Vectors_19","q":"Can two vectors of 3N and 4N result in 1N? How?","m":1,"ans":["Yes, if they act in opposite directions (180 deg)"]},{"id":"Kinematics_20","q":"Object moves 5m East then 12m North. Total distance vs displacement?","m":2,"ans":["Dist = 17m","Disp = sqrt(5^2+12^2) = 13m"]},{"id":"Mass/Weight_21","q":"Mass is 60kg on Earth. Find weight on Moon (g=1.6).","m":1,"ans":["W = mg = 60 * 1.6 = 96N"]},{"id":"Density_22","q":"Liquid volume 50ml, mass 40g. Find density.","m":1,"ans":["40/50 = 0.8 g/cm3"]},{"id":"Acceleration_23","q":"Train slows from 40m/s to 10m/s in 15s. Find deceleration.","m":2,"ans":["(10-40)/15 = -2m/s^2","Decel = 2m/s^2"]},{"id":"Measurement_24","q":"Which instrument measures internal diameter of a pipe?","m":1,"ans":["Vernier callipers (internal jaws)"]},{"id":"Motion_25","q":"Difference between speed and velocity.","m":1,"ans":["Velocity has direction (vector), speed does not (scalar)"]},{"id":"Free_Fall_26","q":"A stone is dropped. What is its speed after 3.5s?","m":1,"ans":["v = gt = 10 * 3.5 = 35m/s"]},{"id":"Graphs_27","q":"Constant negative gradient on a v-t graph means?","m":1,"ans":["Uniform deceleration"]},{"id":"Density_28","q":"Object A (density 2) and B (density 4) have same mass. Ratio of Vol A : Vol B?","m":2,"ans":["Vol = m/d","Ratio = (1/2) / (1/4) = 2:1"]},{"id":"Measurement_29","q":"Human reaction time effect on stopwatch measurement.","m":1,"ans":["Introduces systematic/random error; use multiple oscillations to minimize"]},{"id":"Vectors_30","q":"Resultant of 5N and 12N at 90 degrees.","m":1,"ans":["sqrt(25 + 144) = 13N"]},{"id":"Kinematics_31","q":"Distance traveled at 15m/s in 2 minutes.","m":1,"ans":["15 * 120 = 1800m"]},{"id":"Precision_32","q":"Reading 0.045m in mm.","m":1,"ans":["45mm"]},{"id":"Force/Motion_33","q":"If net force is zero, what happens to velocity?","m":1,"ans":["Remains constant (Newton's 1st Law)"]},{"id":"Mass_34","q":"Does mass change on Jupiter?","m":1,"ans":["No, mass is constant (amount of matter)"]},{"id":"Graphs_35","q":"Find displacement from v-t graph: triangle base 5s, height 20m/s.","m":1,"ans":["0.5 * 5 * 20 = 50m"]},{"id":"Density_36","q":"Ice (0.92g/cm3) floats in water. Why?","m":1,"ans":["Density is less than water (1.0g/cm3)"]},{"id":"Scalars_37","q":"Identify scalar: Displacement, Velocity, Acceleration, Time.","m":1,"ans":["Time"]},{"id":"Kinematics_38","q":"Car travels 1/3 dist at 10m/s and 2/3 at 20m/s. Average speed?","m":1,"ans":["Total Dist D. Time = (D/3)/10 + (2D/3)/20 = D/30 + D/30 = D/15. Avg = D / (D/15) = 15m/s"]},{"id":"Measurement_39","q":"Smallest division on a standard metric ruler.","m":1,"ans":["1mm or 0.1cm"]},{"id":"Free_Fall_40","q":"Weight of 2kg mass during free fall (vacuum).","m":1,"ans":["20N (Weight does not change during fall)"]},{"id":"Density_41","q":"Density of air is 1.2kg/m3. Mass of air in 10m3 room?","m":1,"ans":["1.2 * 10 = 12kg"]},{"id":"Vectors_42","q":"Subtract vector 5N North from 5N North.","m":1,"ans":["0N"]},{"id":"Graphs_43","q":"A d-t graph is a horizontal line. Velocity is?","m":1,"ans":["Zero (at rest)"]},{"id":"Kinematics_44","q":"Initial speed 5m/s, accel 2m/s2, time 3s. Final speed?","m":1,"ans":["v = u + at = 5 + 6 = 11m/s"]},{"id":"Pendulum_45","q":"If length increases, what happens to Period T?","m":1,"ans":["T increases"]},{"id":"Measurement_46","q":"Range of a micrometer usually is?","m":1,"ans":["25mm"]},{"id":"Density_47","q":"Density unit in SI.","m":1,"ans":["kg/m3"]},{"id":"Motion_48","q":"Can an object have zero velocity but non-zero acceleration?","m":1,"ans":["Yes, at the peak of a vertical toss"]},{"id":"Vectors_49","q":"Angle between components of a vector.","m":1,"ans":["90 degrees"]},{"id":"Kinematics_50","q":"Stop distance if react time is 0.5s at 20m/s and decel is 5m/s2.","m":3,"ans":["Think dist = 20*0.5 = 10m","Brake dist = v^2/2a = 400/10 = 40m","Total = 50m"]}]},{"chapter":"Misc","questions":[{"id":"Measurement_1","q":"Describe how to use a micrometer screw gauge to measure the diameter of a thin wire accurately.","m":3,"ans":["Close jaws on wire and take reading from sleeve and thimble.","Repeat at different positions and orientations; calculate average.","Check and subtract zero error."]},{"id":"Precision_2","q":"State the precision of a meter rule, vernier caliper, and micrometer.","m":3,"ans":["Meter rule: 1mm.","Vernier: 0.1mm.","Micrometer: 0.01mm."]},{"id":"Graphing_3","q":"State three requirements for a line of best fit.","m":3,"ans":["Thin and smooth.","Passes through most points.","Balanced distribution of points above/below."]},{"id":"Gradient_4","q":"How do you choose points for a gradient calculation?","m":2,"ans":["Points on the line of best fit, not raw data points.","Points far apart (at least half the line length)."]},{"id":"Errors_5","q":"Distinguish between systematic and random errors.","m":2,"ans":["Systematic: Constant bias (e.g., zero error).","Random: Unpredictable (reduced by averaging)."]},{"id":"Parallax_6","q":"How do you avoid parallax error with a measuring cylinder?","m":2,"ans":["View at eye level.","Read from the bottom of the meniscus."]},{"id":"Oscillations_7","q":"Why time 20 oscillations of a pendulum instead of 1?","m":1,"ans":["Reduces effect of human reaction time error."]},{"id":"Circuits_8","q":"Why open the switch between readings in an electrical experiment?","m":1,"ans":["Prevents heating of components (which changes resistance)."]},{"id":"Variables_9","q":"Define independent and dependent variables.","m":2,"ans":["Independent: The one you change (x-axis).","Dependent: The one you measure (y-axis)."]},{"id":"Equilibrium_10","q":"Why must a lamina swing freely to find its center of mass?","m":1,"ans":["So gravity aligns the CM vertically below the pivot."]},{"id":"Proportionality_11","q":"What graph feature shows direct proportionality?","m":1,"ans":["A straight line passing through the origin."]},{"id":"Zero_Error_12","q":"A micrometer shows -0.02mm when closed. A reading is 0.54mm. Correct it.","m":1,"ans":["0.54 - (-0.02) = 0.56mm."]},{"id":"Sig_Figs_13","q":"Rule for significant figures in final answers?","m":1,"ans":["Give to the same number of s.f. as the least precise raw data."]},{"id":"Tables_14","q":"Requirement for table column headings?","m":1,"ans":["Quantity name/symbol and unit (e.g., V / Volts)."]},{"id":"Thermal_Safety_15","q":"One precaution for heat experiments?","m":1,"ans":["Use heat-proof mats / insulation to prevent burns."]},{"id":"Anomalies_16","q":"How to handle a point far from the best-fit line?","m":1,"ans":["Identify as anomalous; ignore in line of best fit or repeat."]},{"id":"Optics_17","q":"Difficulty in measuring refraction angles?","m":1,"ans":["Ray thickness / difficulty placing pins accurately."]},{"id":"Graph_Scale_18","q":"Minimum coverage for a graph plot?","m":1,"ans":["Points must occupy more than half the grid."]},{"id":"Volume_Units_19","q":"Convert 100cm³ to m³.","m":1,"ans":["$100 \times 10^{-6} = 0.0001 m^3$."]},{"id":"Timing_20","q":"Advantage of light gates over stopwatches?","m":1,"ans":["Eliminates human reaction time."]},{"id":"Mass_21","q":"Best instrument for mass of 10g salt?","m":1,"ans":["Electronic balance (precision 0.1g or 0.01g)."]},{"id":"Constants_22","q":"What is a control variable?","m":1,"ans":["A factor kept constant to ensure a fair test."]},{"id":"Volume_23","q":"How to measure volume of an irregular stone?","m":1,"ans":["Displacement method using a measuring cylinder."]},{"id":"Springs_24","q":"One precaution for Hooke's Law experiment?","m":1,"ans":["Do not exceed the limit of proportionality."]},{"id":"Reflections_25","q":"Purpose of using thin pins in optics?","m":1,"ans":["Increases accuracy of the ray path."]},{"id":"Sensitivity_26","q":"How to make a thermometer more sensitive?","m":1,"ans":["Narrower capillary tube / larger bulb."]},{"id":"Reliability_27","q":"How to improve reliability of results?","m":1,"ans":["Repeat measurements and take an average."]},{"id":"Magnetism_28","q":"Precaution for magnetic field plotting?","m":1,"ans":["Keep away from other magnets/metal objects."]},{"id":"Density_29","q":"Required measurements for density of a liquid?","m":1,"ans":["Mass of cylinder, mass of cylinder+liquid, volume."]},{"id":"Electric_safety_30","q":"Why avoid wet hands in the lab?","m":1,"ans":["Water lowers skin resistance, increasing shock risk."]},{"id":"Gradient_Units_31","q":"Unit of gradient for a Distance-Time graph?","m":1,"ans":["m/s (represents speed)."]},{"id":"Scale_Errors_32","q":"What causes a 'kink' in a graph?","m":1,"ans":["Inconsistent scale on an axis."]},{"id":"Lenses_33","q":"Difficulty in finding the sharpest image?","m":1,"ans":["Subjectivity of human eye / depth of focus."]},{"id":"Area_34","q":"How to measure area of irregular leaf?","m":1,"ans":["Trace on grid paper; count squares > half-filled."]},{"id":"Speed_35","q":"Why use a ticker timer?","m":1,"ans":["Measures constant small intervals of time (0.02s)."]},{"id":"Expansion_36","q":"Why heat water slowly in expansion tests?","m":1,"ans":["Ensures uniform temperature throughout."]},{"id":"Ammeter_37","q":"Effect of ammeter resistance on a circuit?","m":1,"ans":["Slightly reduces total current; ideally zero Ω."]},{"id":"Voltmeter_38","q":"Ideal resistance of a voltmeter?","m":1,"ans":["Infinite (to prevent current bypass)."]},{"id":"Momentum_39","q":"Why use friction-compensated ramps?","m":1,"ans":["So gravity balances friction; net force is zero."]},{"id":"Gas_pressure_40","q":"Safety for pressure law experiments?","m":1,"ans":["Wear safety goggles; do not overheat trapped air."]},{"id":"Oscillatory_41","q":"Define 'fiducial marker'.","m":1,"ans":["A reference point (e.g., pin) to aid timing."]},{"id":"Data_Logging_42","q":"Advantage of sensors over human observation?","m":1,"ans":["Higher sampling rate / long-term monitoring."]},{"id":"Units_43","q":"Value of 1mA in Amperes?","m":1,"ans":["0.001 A."]},{"id":"Density_Calc_44","q":"Sig figs for mass 5.0g and volume 2.00cm³?","m":1,"ans":["2 sig figs (2.5 g/cm³)."]},{"id":"Center_of_Mass_45","q":"Why use a plumb line?","m":1,"ans":["To ensure a perfectly vertical reference line."]},{"id":"Cooling_46","q":"Why stir liquid during cooling?","m":1,"ans":["To maintain uniform temperature."]},{"id":"Protractor_47","q":"Precision of a standard protractor?","m":1,"ans":["1 degree."]},{"id":"Graphing_48","q":"Can a best-fit line be a curve?","m":1,"ans":["Yes, if the trend of data is clearly non-linear."]},{"id":"Safety_Symbols_49","q":"Meaning of a skull and crossbones symbol?","m":1,"ans":["Toxic/Poisonous substance."]},{"id":"Conclusion_50","q":"What is meant by 'justifying' a relationship?","m":1,"ans":["Using data pairs to show a constant ratio or product."]}]},{"chapter":"Moments Basics","questions":[{"id":"MOM-001","q":"Define the 'moment of a force'.","m":2,"ans":["The product of the force and the perpendicular distance from the pivot to the line of action of the force.","Moment = F x d (perpendicular)."]},{"id":"MOM-002","q":"State the SI unit for the moment of a force.","m":1,"ans":["Newton-metre (Nm)","Nm"]},{"id":"MOM-003","q":"State the 'Principle of Moments'.","m":2,"ans":["For an object in equilibrium, the sum of clockwise moments about a pivot equals the sum of anticlockwise moments about the same pivot.","Total CW Moments = Total ACW Moments."]},{"id":"MOM-004","q":"Define 'Center of Mass'.","m":2,"ans":["The point through which the whole weight of an object appears to act.","Balance point of an object."]},{"id":"MOM-005","q":"What two conditions must be met for an object to be in equilibrium?","m":2,"ans":["1. Resultant force is zero. 2. Resultant moment is zero.","No net force; no net rotation."]},{"id":"MOM-006","q":"How does increasing the length of a wrench affect the moment produced by the same force?","m":1,"ans":["It increases the moment.","Larger turning effect."]},{"id":"MOM-007","q":"A force of 10 N is applied 0.5 m from a pivot. Calculate the moment.","m":1,"ans":["5 Nm","10 * 0.5"]},{"id":"MOM-008","q":"Explain why it is easier to open a door by pushing at the handle rather than near the hinges.","m":2,"ans":["The distance from the pivot (hinge) is greater, so a smaller force is needed to produce the same turning moment.","Greater d = less F."]},{"id":"MOM-009","q":"Describe how to find the center of mass of an irregularly shaped thin sheet (lamina).","m":3,"ans":["1. Hang the lamina from a hole and use a plumb line to draw a vertical line. 2. Repeat from a second hole. 3. The intersection is the center of mass.","Plumb line method/intersection."]},{"id":"MOM-010","q":"What is the moment of a force if the line of action of the force passes through the pivot?","m":1,"ans":["Zero","0 Nm (d = 0)"]},{"id":"MOM-011","q":"Define 'Stability'.","m":1,"ans":["The ability of an object to return to its original position after being tilted.","Resistance to toppling."]},{"id":"MOM-012","q":"State two ways to increase the stability of an object.","m":2,"ans":["1. Lower the center of mass. 2. Widen the base area.","Low CoM and wide base."]},{"id":"MOM-013","q":"A uniform beam is 2.0 m long and weighs 100 N. Where is its center of mass located?","m":1,"ans":["At its geometric center (1.0 m from either end).","Midpoint."]},{"id":"MOM-014","q":"A 50 N child sits 2 m from the pivot of a seesaw. How far must a 100 N child sit on the other side to balance it?","m":2,"ans":["1 m","50 * 2 = 100 * d"]},{"id":"MOM-015","q":"When does an object topple over?","m":2,"ans":["When the vertical line through its center of mass falls outside its base.","Line of weight outside base."]},{"id":"MOM-016","q":"Is the moment of a force a scalar or a vector?","m":1,"ans":["Vector","Vector (has direction: CW or ACW)"]},{"id":"MOM-017","q":"Explain why a racing car has a very low chassis.","m":2,"ans":["To lower the center of mass and increase stability during fast turns/prevent toppling.","Lower CoM = more stable."]},{"id":"MOM-018","q":"A uniform meter rule is balanced at the 50 cm mark. A 2 N weight is hung at the 10 cm mark. Where should a 4 N weight be placed to balance it?","m":3,"ans":["70 cm mark","2 * 40 = 4 * d -> d = 20 cm from pivot. 50 + 20 = 70."]},{"id":"MOM-019","q":"What is meant by a 'uniform' object in physics?","m":1,"ans":["An object whose mass is evenly distributed so its center of mass is at its geometric center.","Even mass distribution."]},{"id":"MOM-020","q":"What is the direction of the moment if it causes a rotation in the same direction as a clock's hands?","m":1,"ans":["Clockwise","CW"]},{"id":"MOM-021","q":"In a balanced lever, if the load is 400 N and the effort is 100 N, what is the ratio of effort distance to load distance?","m":2,"ans":["4:1","100 * d1 = 400 * d2"]},{"id":"MOM-022","q":"Describe 'stable equilibrium'.","m":2,"ans":["When tilted, the center of mass rises and the weight creates a moment that returns the object to its original position.","Returns when tilted."]},{"id":"MOM-023","q":"Describe 'unstable equilibrium'.","m":2,"ans":["When tilted, the center of mass falls and the weight creates a moment that pulls the object further from its original position.","Topples when tilted."]},{"id":"MOM-024","q":"Describe 'neutral equilibrium'.","m":2,"ans":["When moved, the height of the center of mass remains constant (e.g., a rolling ball).","CoM height stays same."]},{"id":"MOM-025","q":"A force is applied at an angle. Which component of the force is used to calculate the moment?","m":1,"ans":["The component perpendicular to the distance from the pivot.","Perpendicular component."]},{"id":"MOM-026","q":"Why do double-decker buses have heavy engines placed very low?","m":1,"ans":["To lower the center of mass for better stability.","Lower CoM."]},{"id":"MOM-027","q":"A 10 N force creates a 20 Nm moment. Calculate the distance from the pivot.","m":1,"ans":["2 m","20 / 10"]},{"id":"MOM-028","q":"Can the center of mass of an object be outside the physical body of the object?","m":2,"ans":["Yes (e.g., a ring or a doughnut).","Yes, in the center of the hole."]},{"id":"MOM-029","q":"How does a wide base help an object stay upright?","m":2,"ans":["It requires the object to be tilted through a larger angle before the line of weight falls outside the base.","Larger tilt angle to topple."]},{"id":"MOM-030","q":"If an object is in equilibrium, what is the net moment about ANY point?","m":1,"ans":["Zero","0"]},{"id":"MOM-031","q":"A nut requires 30 Nm to loosen. If you can only exert 50 N, what is the minimum length of the wrench needed?","m":2,"ans":["0.6 m","30 / 50"]},{"id":"MOM-032","q":"State the formula for a moment.","m":1,"ans":["$M = F \times d$","Force x perpendicular distance"]},{"id":"MOM-033","q":"A plumber uses a pipe to extend the handle of a wrench. Why?","m":1,"ans":["To increase the distance from the pivot and produce a larger moment with the same force.","Increase leverage."]},{"id":"MOM-034","q":"Where is the center of mass of a circular hoop?","m":1,"ans":["In the center of the hoop (empty space).","The geometric center."]},{"id":"MOM-035","q":"A non-uniform rod is 1 m long. Its center of mass is 0.3 m from one end. It weighs 20 N. Calculate the moment about the near end.","m":2,"ans":["6 Nm","20 * 0.3"]},{"id":"MOM-036","q":"What happens to the moment of a force as the angle of application changes from 90 to 0 degrees relative to the beam?","m":1,"ans":["It decreases to zero.","Reduces."]},{"id":"MOM-037","q":"Is the weight of an object a force that can produce a moment?","m":1,"ans":["Yes","Acts through the center of mass."]},{"id":"MOM-038","q":"A bridge is supported at two ends. A car drives across. Which support experiences more force as the car moves towards it?","m":1,"ans":["The support closer to the car.","The nearer one."]},{"id":"MOM-039","q":"If an object is balanced, the anticlockwise moments are ______ to the clockwise moments.","m":1,"ans":["equal","equal"]},{"id":"MOM-040","q":"Calculate the force needed 10 cm from a pivot to balance 5 N at 20 cm.","m":2,"ans":["10 N","5 * 20 = F * 10"]},{"id":"MOM-041","q":"What is a 'pivot'?","m":1,"ans":["The fixed point about which an object rotates.","Fulcrum."]},{"id":"MOM-042","q":"Define 'Lamina'.","m":1,"ans":["A flat, two-dimensional shape.","Thin flat sheet."]},{"id":"MOM-043","q":"A uniform beam is supported at its center. If you move a weight further from the center, what happens to its moment?","m":1,"ans":["It increases.","Increases."]},{"id":"MOM-044","q":"What is the weight of a uniform meter rule if it balances at 50 cm with 2 N at 0 cm and a pivot at 25 cm?","m":3,"ans":["2 N","2 * 25 = W * 25"]},{"id":"MOM-045","q":"Does the center of mass change if you cut the object in half?","m":1,"ans":["Yes","It shifts within the new pieces."]},{"id":"MOM-046","q":"Why do heavy-bottomed glasses prevent spills?","m":1,"ans":["They have a low center of mass, making them more stable.","Stability."]},{"id":"MOM-047","q":"In a diagram of a crowbar, identify the pivot.","m":1,"ans":["The point where the bar touches the ground/surface.","The fulcrum."]},{"id":"MOM-048","q":"What is a 'force couple' (extension topic)?","m":2,"ans":["Two equal and opposite forces acting along different lines of action, causing rotation only.","Pure turning effect."]},{"id":"MOM-049","q":"Calculate the total moment: 5 Nm (CW) and 2 Nm (ACW).","m":1,"ans":["3 Nm Clockwise","5 - 2 CW"]},{"id":"MOM-050","q":"Can an object be in equilibrium if it is moving?","m":2,"ans":["Yes, if it is moving at a constant velocity (translational) and constant angular velocity (rotational).","Yes, zero net force/moment."]},{"id":"MOM-051","q":"If you push a wall, do you create a moment?","m":1,"ans":["No (unless the wall can rotate about a pivot).","No."]},{"id":"MOM-052","q":"What is the perpendicular distance in a moment calculation?","m":1,"ans":["The shortest distance from the pivot to the line of action of the force.","Shortest distance."]},{"id":"MOM-053","q":"A car is tilted on a slope. At what angle will it topple?","m":2,"ans":["When the center of mass is vertically above the lower wheels.","Line of weight at edge of base."]},{"id":"MOM-054","q":"Define 'Equilibrium'.","m":1,"ans":["A state where there is no resultant force and no resultant moment.","State of balance."]},{"id":"MOM-055","q":"Is the center of mass and center of gravity the same thing on Earth?","m":1,"ans":["Yes (for O Level purposes).","Yes."]},{"id":"MOM-056","q":"A door is 0.8 m wide. A force of 20 N is applied at the edge. Find the moment.","m":1,"ans":["16 Nm","20 * 0.8"]},{"id":"MOM-057","q":"How does 'weight' act on a beam in moment problems?","m":1,"ans":["As a downward force acting through the center of mass.","Force at CoM."]},{"id":"MOM-058","q":"A wheelbarrow is a type of lever. Where is the pivot?","m":1,"ans":["The wheel (axle).","The wheel."]},{"id":"MOM-059","q":"State the units of distance used in Nm.","m":1,"ans":["metres (m)","meters"]},{"id":"MOM-060","q":"If a force of 5 N is 20 cm from a pivot, what is the moment in SI units?","m":2,"ans":["1.0 Nm","5 * 0.2"]},{"id":"MOM-061","q":"Why is it harder to balance on one foot than two?","m":1,"ans":["The base area is smaller, making it easier for the CoM to fall outside the base.","Smaller base."]},{"id":"MOM-062","q":"Where is the center of mass of a uniform triangle?","m":1,"ans":["At the intersection of its medians (1/3 of the height from the base).","One-third up."]},{"id":"MOM-063","q":"A 100 N weight is 0.4 m from a pivot. What anticlockwise moment is needed for balance?","m":1,"ans":["40 Nm","100 * 0.4"]},{"id":"MOM-064","q":"What is the moment of a 2 kg mass hung 30 cm from a pivot? ($g=10$)","m":2,"ans":["6 Nm","20 * 0.3"]},{"id":"MOM-065","q":"If a beam is balanced, the resultant moment is...","m":1,"ans":["Zero","0"]},{"id":"MOM-066","q":"What instrument can be used to ensure a pivot is level?","m":1,"ans":["Spirit level.","Spirit level."]},{"id":"MOM-067","q":"A force of 6 N North and 6 N South act 1 m apart. What is the resultant force?","m":1,"ans":["0 N","Zero."]},{"id":"MOM-068","q":"Referencing question 67, what is the resultant moment?","m":1,"ans":["6 Nm","6 * 1 (Couple)."]},{"id":"MOM-069","q":"How do you label the turning effect in a diagram?","m":1,"ans":["With an arrow showing the direction of rotation (CW or ACW).","Rotation arrow."]},{"id":"MOM-070","q":"A boy stands on a plank. As he walks towards the end, what happens to the force on the far support?","m":1,"ans":["It increases.","Increases."]},{"id":"MOM-071","q":"What is the moment of a 50 N force acting parallel to a beam?","m":1,"ans":["Zero","0 (d = 0)"]},{"id":"MOM-072","q":"Why are table lamps designed with heavy bases?","m":1,"ans":["To lower the center of mass and increase stability.","Stability."]},{"id":"MOM-073","q":"Does the Principle of Moments apply to non-uniform beams?","m":1,"ans":["Yes","As long as it's in equilibrium."]},{"id":"MOM-074","q":"Calculate the effort needed to lift a 600 N load if effort distance is 3 m and load distance is 0.5 m.","m":2,"ans":["100 N","600 * 0.5 = E * 3"]},{"id":"MOM-075","q":"Where is the center of mass of a person standing upright?","m":1,"ans":["Approximately in the pelvic region.","Lower torso."]},{"id":"MOM-076","q":"Why does a tightrope walker carry a long pole?","m":2,"ans":["To increase their rotational inertia and lower their center of mass.","Stability/Balance."]},{"id":"MOM-077","q":"A uniform 4 m beam weighs 200 N. It is supported 1 m from the left end. What force at the left end is needed to balance it?","m":3,"ans":["200 N","200 * 1 = F * 1 (Pivot is 1m in; CoM is 2m in)."]},{"id":"MOM-078","q":"What happens to stability as an object's center of mass rises?","m":1,"ans":["It decreases.","Less stable."]},{"id":"MOM-079","q":"Is 'pivot' the same as 'fulcrum'?","m":1,"ans":["Yes","Yes."]},{"id":"MOM-080","q":"Can you use cm instead of m in moment equations?","m":1,"ans":["Yes, as long as you are consistent on both sides.","Consistency is key."]},{"id":"MOM-081","q":"What is the moment of a force of 0 N?","m":1,"ans":["Zero","0"]},{"id":"MOM-082","q":"Where should the pivot be placed to balance a uniform beam with no extra weights?","m":1,"ans":["At its center of mass.","The midpoint."]},{"id":"MOM-083","q":"Explain why a tall vase is less stable than a short, wide one.","m":2,"ans":["It has a higher center of mass and a smaller base area.","Higher CoM and smaller base."]},{"id":"MOM-084","q":"A crane lifts a load. How is it balanced to prevent toppling?","m":1,"ans":["By using heavy counterweights on the opposite side of the pivot.","Counterweights."]},{"id":"MOM-085","q":"Define 'perpendicular'.","m":1,"ans":["At an angle of 90 degrees.","Right-angled."]},{"id":"MOM-086","q":"If an object is in neutral equilibrium, does its center of mass rise when moved?","m":1,"ans":["No","Stays at the same height."]},{"id":"MOM-087","q":"A 5 N force is 0.1 m from a pivot. What is the moment in Ncm?","m":1,"ans":["50 Ncm","5 * 10"]},{"id":"MOM-088","q":"What is the effect of a resultant moment on an object?","m":1,"ans":["It causes the object to rotate / change its angular velocity.","Rotation."]},{"id":"MOM-089","q":"Does the center of mass move if the object is rotated?","m":1,"ans":["No, its position relative to the object stays the same.","Stays fixed relative to object."]},{"id":"MOM-090","q":"In a balanced meter rule, if Weight A > Weight B, which weight is closer to the pivot?","m":1,"ans":["Weight A","Heavier weight is closer."]},{"id":"MOM-091","q":"State the condition for an object to be 'stably balanced'.","m":2,"ans":["The vertical line from the CoM must fall within the base.","CoM within base."]},{"id":"MOM-092","q":"How does a wide-based chair improve stability?","m":1,"ans":["By increasing the area over which the CoM can move before toppling.","Larger base area."]},{"id":"MOM-093","q":"A 12 N force is applied 25 cm from a hinge. Calculate the moment in Nm.","m":2,"ans":["3 Nm","12 * 0.25"]},{"id":"MOM-094","q":"Why do some rulers have a hole at one end?","m":1,"ans":["To allow them to be hung (useful for finding CoM/pivot experiments).","Hanging/Pivot point."]},{"id":"MOM-095","q":"What is the net force on a balanced seesaw?","m":1,"ans":["Zero","0 N"]},{"id":"MOM-096","q":"What happens if you sit on a chair and lean too far back?","m":2,"ans":["Your center of mass moves past the rear legs (pivot), creating a toppling moment.","Topples back."]},{"id":"MOM-097","q":"If $F = 20$ N and $M = 4$ Nm, what is $d$?","m":1,"ans":["0.2 m","4 / 20"]},{"id":"MOM-098","q":"Is the center of mass always at the center of the object?","m":1,"ans":["No, only for uniform objects.","Depends on mass distribution."]},{"id":"MOM-099","q":"What type of equilibrium is a cone resting on its base?","m":1,"ans":["Stable equilibrium.","Stable."]},{"id":"MOM-100","q":"What type of equilibrium is a cone resting on its tip?","m":1,"ans":["Unstable equilibrium.","Unstable."]}]},{"chapter":"Pressure Basics Basics","questions":[{"id":"PRE-001","q":"Define 'Pressure'.","m":2,"ans":["The force acting per unit area.","$P = F/A$"]},{"id":"PRE-002","q":"State the SI unit of pressure.","m":1,"ans":["Pascal (Pa)","Pa"]},{"id":"PRE-003","q":"Give the equivalent of 1 Pascal in terms of Newtons and Metres.","m":1,"ans":["1 N/m²","Newton per square meter"]},{"id":"PRE-004","q":"Explain why a sharp knife cuts more easily than a blunt one.","m":2,"ans":["A sharp knife has a smaller surface area, so for the same force, it exerts a much higher pressure.","Smaller area = higher pressure."]},{"id":"PRE-005","q":"A force of 100 N acts on an area of 2 m². Calculate the pressure.","m":1,"ans":["50 Pa","100 / 2"]},{"id":"PRE-006","q":"State the formula for pressure in a liquid.","m":1,"ans":["$P = \rho gh$","density * gravity * depth"]},{"id":"PRE-007","q":"What happens to the pressure in a liquid as depth increases?","m":1,"ans":["It increases.","Directly proportional to depth."]},{"id":"PRE-008","q":"Does the shape of a container affect the pressure at the bottom of a liquid?","m":1,"ans":["No","Only depends on vertical depth, density, and g."]},{"id":"PRE-009","q":"Calculate the pressure at a depth of 10 m in water ($\rho = 1000$ kg/m³, $g=10$).","m":2,"ans":["100,000 Pa","1000 * 10 * 10"]},{"id":"PRE-010","q":"What is the approximate value of standard atmospheric pressure?","m":1,"ans":["1.01 \times 10^5 Pa (or 760 mmHg / 1 atm)","101,000 Pa"]},{"id":"PRE-011","q":"Name the instrument used to measure atmospheric pressure.","m":1,"ans":["Mercury barometer.","Barometer."]},{"id":"PRE-012","q":"Name the instrument used to measure the pressure of a gas supply.","m":1,"ans":["Manometer.","U-tube manometer."]},{"id":"PRE-013","q":"In a mercury barometer, what exists in the space above the mercury column?","m":1,"ans":["A vacuum.","Torricellian vacuum."]},{"id":"PRE-014","q":"How does increasing the density of a liquid affect the pressure at a fixed depth?","m":1,"ans":["Pressure increases.","$P \\propto \rho$"]},{"id":"PRE-015","q":"A block weighs 200 N and has a base area of 0.5 m². Find the pressure it exerts on the floor.","m":1,"ans":["400 Pa","200 / 0.5"]},{"id":"PRE-016","q":"State Pascal's Principle.","m":2,"ans":["Pressure applied to an enclosed fluid is transmitted equally in all directions throughout the fluid.","Pressure in = Pressure out."]},{"id":"PRE-017","q":"In a hydraulic lift, the small piston has area 0.01 m² and the large 0.1 m². If 50 N is applied to the small, what is the output force?","m":2,"ans":["500 N","(50 / 0.01) * 0.1"]},{"id":"PRE-018","q":"Why is a gas easily compressed while a liquid is not?","m":2,"ans":["Gas particles are far apart with large empty spaces; liquid particles are close together.","Particles spacing."]},{"id":"PRE-019","q":"What happens to atmospheric pressure as you go higher up a mountain?","m":1,"ans":["It decreases.","Less air above to exert weight."]},{"id":"PRE-020","q":"Explain why a dam is built thicker at the bottom than at the top.","m":2,"ans":["Water pressure increases with depth; a thicker wall is needed at the bottom to withstand the higher pressure.","Withstand depth-pressure."]},{"id":"PRE-021","q":"A man stands on one foot. Does the pressure on the floor increase or decrease compared to standing on two feet?","m":1,"ans":["Increases.","Area is halved, so pressure doubles."]},{"id":"PRE-022","q":"Convert 2 N/cm² into Pascals.","m":2,"ans":["20,000 Pa","2 * 10,000"]},{"id":"PRE-023","q":"What is the vertical height of a mercury barometer at sea level?","m":1,"ans":["760 mm (or 76 cm)","760 mmHg"]},{"id":"PRE-024","q":"If the glass tube of a barometer is tilted, what happens to the vertical height of the mercury?","m":1,"ans":["It remains the same.","Vertical height is constant."]},{"id":"PRE-025","q":"A U-tube manometer shows a height difference of 10 cm of water. Calculate the excess gas pressure ($\rho_{water} = 1000$).","m":2,"ans":["1000 Pa","1000 * 10 * 0.1"]},{"id":"PRE-026","q":"Why is mercury used in barometers instead of water?","m":2,"ans":["Mercury is very dense, so the tube can be of a manageable height (~76cm). A water barometer would need to be ~10m tall.","High density."]},{"id":"PRE-027","q":"Define 'Absolute Pressure'.","m":2,"ans":["The total pressure, equal to atmospheric pressure plus gauge pressure.","$P_{abs} = P_{atm} + P_{gauge}$"]},{"id":"PRE-028","q":"What is the pressure exerted by a 10 kg box with base 10 cm by 10 cm? ($g=10$)","m":3,"ans":["10,000 Pa","100 N / 0.01 m²"]},{"id":"PRE-029","q":"Describe the movement of gas molecules that creates pressure on the walls of a container.","m":2,"ans":["Constant random motion; molecules collide with the walls, exerting a force upon impact.","Collisions with walls."]},{"id":"PRE-030","q":"State one application of a hydraulic system.","m":1,"ans":["Car brakes / hydraulic jacks / aircraft control surfaces.","Brakes."]},{"id":"PRE-031","q":"If you blow air into a balloon, why does it expand?","m":2,"ans":["The number of air molecules increases, leading to more frequent collisions with the walls, increasing internal pressure.","Higher collision rate."]},{"id":"PRE-032","q":"What property of a liquid makes it suitable for use in hydraulics?","m":1,"ans":["It is incompressible.","Incompressibility."]},{"id":"PRE-033","q":"A diver descends in the ocean. If the pressure increases by 100,000 Pa, how deep did they go? ($\rho=1000, g=10$)","m":2,"ans":["10 m","$h = P / (\rho g)$"]},{"id":"PRE-034","q":"Explain why your ears might 'pop' when a plane takes off.","m":2,"ans":["External atmospheric pressure drops faster than the pressure inside your middle ear, causing the eardrum to bulge.","Pressure imbalance."]},{"id":"PRE-035","q":"State the relationship between Pressure and Area for a constant force.","m":1,"ans":["Pressure is inversely proportional to Area.","$P \\propto 1/A$"]},{"id":"PRE-036","q":"Why do tractors have very wide tires?","m":2,"ans":["To increase the surface area and reduce the pressure on the soft ground, preventing the tractor from sinking.","Spread weight/reduce pressure."]},{"id":"PRE-037","q":"How does temperature affect gas pressure in a fixed volume?","m":1,"ans":["Pressure increases.","Molecules move faster/hit harder."]},{"id":"PRE-038","q":"A cylinder has a cross-section of 0.05 m². What force is needed to produce 2000 Pa of pressure?","m":2,"ans":["100 N","2000 * 0.05"]},{"id":"PRE-039","q":"Can a liquid be used in a manometer to measure very small pressure differences?","m":2,"ans":["Yes, a low-density liquid (like oil) is used to give a larger height difference.","Low density liquid."]},{"id":"PRE-040","q":"What happens to the pressure of a gas if its volume is halved at constant temperature?","m":1,"ans":["Pressure doubles.","Boyle's Law."]},{"id":"PRE-041","q":"Calculate the area required for a 600 N person to exert a pressure of 12,000 Pa.","m":2,"ans":["0.05 m²","600 / 12,000"]},{"id":"PRE-042","q":"If a hole is poked in a water tank, which stream goes further: the one from the top or the bottom?","m":1,"ans":["The one from the bottom.","Higher pressure at the bottom."]},{"id":"PRE-043","q":"Why is the base of a light bulb made of thin glass but the bottom of a scuba tank made of thick steel?","m":2,"ans":["The scuba tank must withstand very high internal pressure; the bulb only withstands 1 atm.","Withstand pressure difference."]},{"id":"PRE-044","q":"Define 'Gauge Pressure'.","m":1,"ans":["The difference between the absolute pressure and the atmospheric pressure.","$P_{abs} - P_{atm}$"]},{"id":"PRE-045","q":"State the unit 'mmHg'.","m":1,"ans":["Millimetres of mercury.","Unit of pressure height."]},{"id":"PRE-046","q":"A hydraulic system has an input force of 10 N and an output of 100 N. What is the 'force multiplier'?","m":1,"ans":["10","100 / 10"]},{"id":"PRE-047","q":"How does the width of a barometer tube affect the height of the mercury?","m":1,"ans":["It doesn't.","Independent of width."]},{"id":"PRE-048","q":"Why is it dangerous to stand on a frozen lake if the ice is thin?","m":2,"ans":["Your weight acts on a small area (feet), creating high pressure that can crack the ice. Laying flat reduces pressure.","High pressure vs surface area."]},{"id":"PRE-049","q":"Calculate the pressure exerted by water at a depth of 50 cm. ($\rho=1000, g=10$)","m":2,"ans":["5,000 Pa","1000 * 10 * 0.5"]},{"id":"PRE-050","q":"What is the direction of pressure in a fluid?","m":1,"ans":["In all directions.","Equally in all directions."]},{"id":"PRE-051","q":"If atmospheric pressure is 76 cmHg and a gas pressure is 80 cmHg, what is the height difference in a mercury manometer?","m":1,"ans":["4 cm","80 - 76"]},{"id":"PRE-052","q":"State the effect of air bubbles in a hydraulic system.","m":2,"ans":["It makes the system 'spongy' and less efficient because air is compressible.","Reduces force transmission."]},{"id":"PRE-053","q":"A thumb tack has a head area of 1 cm² and a point area of 0.01 mm². Why?","m":2,"ans":["Low pressure on the thumb (large area) and high pressure on the board (tiny area).","Pressure concentration."]},{"id":"PRE-054","q":"A submarine is at a depth of 200 m. Calculate the pressure from the sea water. ($\rho=1025$ kg/m³)","m":2,"ans":["2,050,000 Pa","1025 * 10 * 200"]},{"id":"PRE-055","q":"What happens to the mercury level in a barometer if air is leaked into the vacuum space?","m":2,"ans":["The level falls.","Air pressure pushes the mercury down."]},{"id":"PRE-056","q":"Define 'Incompressible'.","m":1,"ans":["A substance whose volume does not change significantly under pressure.","Constant volume."]},{"id":"PRE-057","q":"Why do camels have wide feet?","m":2,"ans":["To reduce the pressure on the sand so they don't sink.","Adaptation for soft surfaces."]},{"id":"PRE-058","q":"A force of 50 N is applied to a piston of area 5 cm². Find the pressure in N/cm².","m":1,"ans":["10 N/cm²","50 / 5"]},{"id":"PRE-059","q":"Referencing Q58, what is that pressure in Pascals?","m":2,"ans":["100,000 Pa","10 * 10,000"]},{"id":"PRE-060","q":"What does a manometer measure if both ends are open to the atmosphere?","m":1,"ans":["Nothing / zero difference.","No height difference."]},{"id":"PRE-061","q":"State the equation for Boyle's Law.","m":1,"ans":["$P_1V_1 = P_2V_2$","Pressure * Volume = Constant"]},{"id":"PRE-062","q":"Calculate the pressure at the bottom of a 2 m deep pool of mercury. ($\rho=13600$)","m":2,"ans":["272,000 Pa","13600 * 10 * 2"]},{"id":"PRE-063","q":"Explain why suction cups stick to a wall.","m":2,"ans":["Air is pushed out, creating a low-pressure area inside; higher atmospheric pressure outside pushes the cup against the wall.","Atmospheric pressure force."]},{"id":"PRE-064","q":"If the area of a hydraulic output piston is 50 times the input, what is the force gain?","m":1,"ans":["50","Multiplier = Area ratio."]},{"id":"PRE-065","q":"Is 'Pressure' a scalar or a vector?","m":1,"ans":["Scalar","Scalar"]},{"id":"PRE-066","q":"Why is the top of a barometer tube closed?","m":1,"ans":["To prevent atmospheric pressure from acting on the top of the mercury column.","Maintain vacuum."]},{"id":"PRE-067","q":"A brick of dimensions 5x10x20 cm weighs 20 N. What is the maximum pressure it can exert?","m":3,"ans":["4000 Pa","20 N / (0.05 * 0.10)"]},{"id":"PRE-068","q":"What is the minimum pressure the brick in Q67 can exert?","m":3,"ans":["1000 Pa","20 N / (0.10 * 0.20)"]},{"id":"PRE-069","q":"How does a straw work?","m":2,"ans":["You reduce the air pressure in your mouth; the higher atmospheric pressure on the liquid surface pushes the liquid up the straw.","Pressure difference."]},{"id":"PRE-070","q":"What happens to the density of air as you go higher in the atmosphere?","m":1,"ans":["It decreases.","Molecules further apart."]},{"id":"PRE-071","q":"A manometer uses oil ($\rho=800$). A gas causes a 20 cm height difference. Find the pressure.","m":2,"ans":["1600 Pa","800 * 10 * 0.2"]},{"id":"PRE-072","q":"Does a mercury barometer measure the pressure of the mercury?","m":1,"ans":["No, it measures atmospheric pressure.","External pressure."]},{"id":"PRE-073","q":"What is the pressure at the surface of a lake?","m":1,"ans":["Atmospheric pressure.","101 kPa"]},{"id":"PRE-074","q":"Why do deep-sea fish die if brought to the surface too quickly?","m":2,"ans":["The high internal pressure in their bodies is no longer balanced by the high external water pressure, causing damage.","Internal pressure expansion."]},{"id":"PRE-075","q":"How many Newtons are in a pressure of 500 Pa acting on 0.1 m²?","m":1,"ans":["50 N","500 * 0.1"]},{"id":"PRE-076","q":"Define 'Fluids'.","m":1,"ans":["Substances that can flow (liquids and gases).","Liquids/Gases."]},{"id":"PRE-077","q":"What is the force on a 2 m² window if the pressure is 100,000 Pa?","m":1,"ans":["200,000 N","100,000 * 2"]},{"id":"PRE-078","q":"How do you convert cmHg to Pascals? (Formula)","m":1,"ans":["$P = \rho_{Hg} \times g \times h$","Use liquid pressure formula."]},{"id":"PRE-079","q":"Why are elephant feet large and flat?","m":1,"ans":["To spread their massive weight and reduce pressure on the ground.","Reduced pressure."]},{"id":"PRE-080","q":"State the effect of increasing altitude on the boiling point of water.","m":2,"ans":["Boiling point decreases because the atmospheric pressure is lower.","Lower pressure = lower B.P."]},{"id":"PRE-081","q":"Calculate the weight of a person who exerts 15,000 Pa with shoes of total area 0.04 m².","m":2,"ans":["600 N","15,000 * 0.04"]},{"id":"PRE-082","q":"Is the pressure at the same horizontal level in a continuous liquid always equal?","m":1,"ans":["Yes","Hydrostatic balance."]},{"id":"PRE-083","q":"Explain why a hydraulic jack cannot create more energy than is put into it.","m":2,"ans":["Conservation of energy: the small piston must move a much larger distance than the large piston moves.","Work in = Work out."]},{"id":"PRE-084","q":"What is the pressure of a 5 m column of oil? ($\rho=900$)","m":1,"ans":["45,000 Pa","900 * 10 * 5"]},{"id":"PRE-085","q":"Why is a syringe used to measure small gas pressures?","m":1,"ans":["The small area allows for high sensitivity to small forces.","Sensitivity."]},{"id":"PRE-086","q":"What happens to the pressure in a syringe if you pull the plunger out while blocking the tip?","m":1,"ans":["Pressure decreases.","Volume increases."]},{"id":"PRE-087","q":"If $P = 10$ Pa and $A = 0.5$ m², find $F$.","m":1,"ans":["5 N","10 * 0.5"]},{"id":"PRE-088","q":"Does a barometer work on the moon?","m":2,"ans":["No, because there is no atmosphere to push the mercury up.","Requires atmosphere."]},{"id":"PRE-089","q":"Which is greater: 100 kPa or 1 atm?","m":1,"ans":["1 atm (101.3 kPa)","1 atm."]},{"id":"PRE-090","q":"How do you calculate the pressure difference between two points in a liquid?","m":1,"ans":["$\\Delta P = \rho g \\Delta h$","Density * g * height diff."]},{"id":"PRE-091","q":"State one use of a siphon.","m":1,"ans":["Moving liquid from a higher container to a lower one over an obstacle.","Liquid transfer."]},{"id":"PRE-092","q":"Why do high-altitude balloons expand as they rise?","m":2,"ans":["External atmospheric pressure decreases, allowing the internal gas to expand the balloon.","Pressure drops outside."]},{"id":"PRE-093","q":"What is the force exerted by the atmosphere on a 1 cm² area?","m":2,"ans":["10 N","100,000 * 0.0001"]},{"id":"PRE-094","q":"Why are hydraulic fluids usually oils rather than water?","m":2,"ans":["Oil lubricates the system and prevents rust.","Lubrication and protection."]},{"id":"PRE-095","q":"A manometer contains mercury. One end is connected to a gas of 800 mmHg. Atmospheric pressure is 760 mmHg. State the height difference.","m":1,"ans":["40 mm","800 - 760"]},{"id":"PRE-096","q":"Is gas pressure constant in all parts of a small container?","m":1,"ans":["Yes","Negligible weight of gas molecules."]},{"id":"PRE-097","q":"Calculate the pressure of a 100 N force on a 5 cm by 5 cm area.","m":2,"ans":["40,000 Pa","100 / 0.0025"]},{"id":"PRE-098","q":"State the standard unit for volume in the SI system.","m":1,"ans":["m³","cubic meters"]},{"id":"PRE-099","q":"How does the area of the reservoir in a barometer affect the reading?","m":1,"ans":["It doesn't.","Independent of reservoir size."]},{"id":"PRE-100","q":"If the pressure on a gas is tripled at constant temperature, what is the new volume?","m":1,"ans":["One-third of the original volume.","1/3 V"]}]},{"chapter":"Radioactivity Basics","questions":[{"id":"NUC-001","q":"Describe the structure of an atom.","m":2,"ans":["A central nucleus containing protons and neutrons, surrounded by electrons in shells.","Nucleus + orbiting electrons."]},{"id":"NUC-002","q":"State the relative charge of a proton, neutron, and electron.","m":2,"ans":["Proton: +1, Neutron: 0, Electron: -1.","P(+1), N(0), E(-1)"]},{"id":"NUC-003","q":"State the relative mass of a proton, neutron, and electron.","m":2,"ans":["Proton: 1, Neutron: 1, Electron: 1/1840 (negligible).","P(1), N(1), E(0)"]},{"id":"NUC-004","q":"What is the 'Proton Number' ($Z$)?","m":1,"ans":["The number of protons in the nucleus of an atom.","Atomic number."]},{"id":"NUC-005","q":"What is the 'Nucleon Number' ($A$)?","m":1,"ans":["The total number of protons and neutrons in the nucleus.","Mass number."]},{"id":"NUC-006","q":"Define 'Isotopes'.","m":2,"ans":["Atoms of the same element with the same number of protons but different number of neutrons.","Same Z, different A."]},{"id":"NUC-007","q":"Why is an atom electrically neutral?","m":1,"ans":["The number of positive protons equals the number of negative electrons.","Protons = Electrons."]},{"id":"NUC-008","q":"What is 'Radioactivity'?","m":2,"ans":["The spontaneous and random decay of an unstable nucleus to become more stable, emitting radiation.","Spontaneous nuclear decay."]},{"id":"NUC-009","q":"What does 'Random' mean in the context of radioactive decay?","m":1,"ans":["It is impossible to predict which specific nucleus will decay next.","Unpredictable timing."]},{"id":"NUC-010","q":"What does 'Spontaneous' mean in the context of radioactive decay?","m":1,"ans":["The decay is not affected by external factors like temperature or pressure.","Unaffected by environment."]},{"id":"NUC-011","q":"Name the three types of nuclear radiation.","m":1,"ans":["Alpha ($\\alpha$), Beta ($\beta$), and Gamma ($\\gamma$).","Alpha, Beta, Gamma."]},{"id":"NUC-012","q":"Describe an Alpha ($\\alpha$) particle.","m":2,"ans":["A helium nucleus consisting of 2 protons and 2 neutrons.","Helium nucleus / $^4_2He$"]},{"id":"NUC-013","q":"Describe a Beta ($\beta$) particle.","m":1,"ans":["A fast-moving electron emitted from the nucleus.","Electron / $^0_{-1}e$"]},{"id":"NUC-014","q":"Describe Gamma ($\\gamma$) radiation.","m":1,"ans":["High-frequency electromagnetic radiation.","EM wave."]},{"id":"NUC-015","q":"Rank the three types of radiation by ionizing power (highest to lowest).","m":1,"ans":["Alpha > Beta > Gamma.","$\\alpha$ is strongest."]},{"id":"NUC-016","q":"Rank the three types of radiation by penetrating power (highest to lowest).","m":1,"ans":["Gamma > Beta > Alpha.","$\\gamma$ is strongest."]},{"id":"NUC-017","q":"What can stop an Alpha particle?","m":1,"ans":["A few centimeters of air or a sheet of paper.","Paper."]},{"id":"NUC-018","q":"What can stop a Beta particle?","m":1,"ans":["A few millimeters of aluminum.","Aluminum foil."]},{"id":"NUC-019","q":"What can stop/reduce Gamma radiation?","m":1,"ans":["Several centimeters of lead or meters of concrete.","Lead/Concrete."]},{"id":"NUC-020","q":"Which types of radiation are deflected by magnetic/electric fields?","m":1,"ans":["Alpha and Beta (because they are charged).","$\\alpha$ and $\beta$."]},{"id":"NUC-021","q":"Why is Gamma radiation not deflected by electric fields?","m":1,"ans":["It has no charge.","Neutral."]},{"id":"NUC-022","q":"What is 'Background Radiation'?","m":2,"ans":["Radiation from natural and man-made sources in the environment that is always present.","Natural radiation."]},{"id":"NUC-023","q":"State two sources of background radiation.","m":1,"ans":["Cosmic rays, Radon gas, rocks/soil, medical X-rays, food (bananas).","Radon and Cosmic rays."]},{"id":"NUC-024","q":"Define 'Half-life' ($t_{1/2}$).","m":2,"ans":["The time taken for half the nuclei in a sample to decay (or for the activity to halve).","Time to halve activity."]},{"id":"NUC-025","q":"A sample has 800 counts/s. After 3 half-lives, what is its activity?","m":2,"ans":["100 counts/s","800 -> 400 -> 200 -> 100"]},{"id":"NUC-026","q":"How does Alpha decay affect $A$ and $Z$?","m":2,"ans":["$A$ decreases by 4, $Z$ decreases by 2.","Mass -4, Proton -2."]},{"id":"NUC-027","q":"How does Beta decay affect $A$ and $Z$?","m":2,"ans":["$A$ remains the same, $Z$ increases by 1.","Mass same, Proton +1."]},{"id":"NUC-028","q":"How does Gamma decay affect $A$ and $Z$?","m":1,"ans":["Neither $A$ nor $Z$ changes.","No change."]},{"id":"NUC-029","q":"What is 'Nuclear Fission'?","m":2,"ans":["The splitting of a large, unstable nucleus into two smaller nuclei, releasing energy.","Splitting heavy nuclei."]},{"id":"NUC-030","q":"What is 'Nuclear Fusion'?","m":2,"ans":["The joining of two light nuclei to form a heavier nucleus, releasing energy.","Joining light nuclei."]},{"id":"NUC-031","q":"Which process powers the Sun?","m":1,"ans":["Nuclear Fusion.","Fusion."]},{"id":"NUC-032","q":"Which process is used in current nuclear power stations?","m":1,"ans":["Nuclear Fission.","Fission."]},{"id":"NUC-033","q":"State one danger of ionizing radiation to living cells.","m":1,"ans":["Can cause mutations, cancer, or cell death.","DNA damage."]},{"id":"NUC-034","q":"State one safety precaution when handling radioactive sources.","m":1,"ans":["Use long-handled tongs / keep in lead-lined boxes / minimize exposure time.","Tongs/Shielding."]},{"id":"NUC-035","q":"What is a 'Geiger-Müller (GM) Tube' used for?","m":1,"ans":["To detect and count radioactive particles/pulses.","Radiation detector."]},{"id":"NUC-036","q":"Define 'Ionization'.","m":2,"ans":["The process of removing or adding electrons to an atom to form an ion.","Creating charged atoms."]},{"id":"NUC-037","q":"Why are Alpha particles highly ionizing?","m":1,"ans":["They have a large mass and a $+2$ charge.","Large and charged."]},{"id":"NUC-038","q":"If a sample takes 12 hours to drop from 100% to 12.5%, what is its half-life?","m":3,"ans":["4 hours","3 half-lives = 12 hours ($100 \rightarrow 50 \rightarrow 25 \rightarrow 12.5$)."]},{"id":"NUC-039","q":"What is 'Carbon Dating'?","m":2,"ans":["Using the ratio of Carbon-14 to Carbon-12 to estimate the age of once-living materials.","Ageing fossils."]},{"id":"NUC-040","q":"What is a 'Cloud Chamber' used for?","m":1,"ans":["To visualize the paths of ionizing radiation.","Seeing particle tracks."]},{"id":"NUC-041","q":"What is the typical diameter of an atom?","m":1,"ans":["$10^{-10}$ m","0.1 nanometers."]},{"id":"NUC-042","q":"What is the typical diameter of a nucleus?","m":1,"ans":["$10^{-15}$ m","Much smaller than the atom."]},{"id":"NUC-043","q":"In Rutherford's alpha-scattering experiment, why did most particles go straight through?","m":2,"ans":["Because most of the atom is empty space.","Empty space."]},{"id":"NUC-044","q":"In Rutherford's experiment, why were some particles deflected at large angles?","m":2,"ans":["They were repelled by a tiny, dense, positively charged nucleus.","Positive nucleus."]},{"id":"NUC-045","q":"What is the unit for the activity of a radioactive source?","m":1,"ans":["Becquerel (Bq)","Bq"]},{"id":"NUC-046","q":"State one medical use of Gamma rays.","m":1,"ans":["Radiotherapy / Sterilizing equipment / Tracers.","Radiotherapy."]},{"id":"NUC-047","q":"Why must a medical tracer have a short half-life?","m":2,"ans":["To minimize the radiation dose to the patient after the procedure is finished.","Safety/Reduce exposure."]},{"id":"NUC-048","q":"Which type of radiation is used in smoke detectors?","m":1,"ans":["Alpha radiation.","Alpha."]},{"id":"NUC-049","q":"Which type of radiation is used to monitor the thickness of paper in a factory?","m":1,"ans":["Beta radiation.","Beta."]},{"id":"NUC-050","q":"What happens to a neutron during Beta decay?","m":2,"ans":["A neutron turns into a proton and an electron (the electron is emitted).","N $\rightarrow$ P + E."]},{"id":"NUC-051","q":"What is 'Nuclear Fusion' harder to achieve than Fission?","m":2,"ans":["It requires extremely high temperatures and pressures to overcome the electrostatic repulsion between nuclei.","Repulsion of positive nuclei."]},{"id":"NUC-052","q":"Identify the element: $^1_1H$.","m":1,"ans":["Hydrogen-1 (Protium).","Hydrogen."]},{"id":"NUC-053","q":"What is 'Deuterium'?","m":1,"ans":["An isotope of hydrogen with 1 proton and 1 neutron ($^2_1H$).","Hydrogen-2."]},{"id":"NUC-054","q":"What is 'Tritium'?","m":1,"ans":["An isotope of hydrogen with 1 proton and 2 neutrons ($^3_1H$).","Hydrogen-3."]},{"id":"NUC-055","q":"What is a 'Chain Reaction'?","m":2,"ans":["A reaction where the neutrons released by one fission event trigger further fission events.","Self-sustaining fission."]},{"id":"NUC-056","q":"What is the role of 'Control Rods' in a nuclear reactor?","m":2,"ans":["They absorb neutrons to control the rate of the chain reaction.","Absorb neutrons."]},{"id":"NUC-057","q":"What is the role of the 'Moderator' in a nuclear reactor?","m":2,"ans":["To slow down fast-moving neutrons so they can be absorbed by the fuel.","Slow down neutrons."]},{"id":"NUC-058","q":"What is a 'Coolant' used for in a reactor?","m":1,"ans":["To remove thermal energy from the reactor core to produce steam.","Heat transfer."]},{"id":"NUC-059","q":"Which radiation is the most dangerous outside the body?","m":1,"ans":["Gamma and Beta (can penetrate skin).","Gamma/Beta."]},{"id":"NUC-060","q":"Which radiation is the most dangerous inside the body?","m":1,"ans":["Alpha (highly ionizing and cannot escape).","Alpha."]},{"id":"NUC-061","q":"Define 'Irradiation'.","m":2,"ans":["Exposing an object to radiation (the object does not become radioactive).","Exposed to rays."]},{"id":"NUC-062","q":"Define 'Contamination'.","m":2,"ans":["The unwanted presence of radioactive atoms on or inside an object.","Radioactive material on object."]},{"id":"NUC-063","q":"Does 1 Bq represent 1 decay per second or 1 decay per minute?","m":1,"ans":["1 decay per second.","1 s^-1"]},{"id":"NUC-064","q":"A nucleus has $A=238$ and $Z=92$. It emits an alpha particle. State the new $A$ and $Z$.","m":2,"ans":["$A=234, Z=90$.","Uranium to Thorium."]},{"id":"NUC-065","q":"A nucleus has $A=14$ and $Z=6$. It emits a beta particle. State the new $A$ and $Z$.","m":2,"ans":["$A=14, Z=7$.","Carbon to Nitrogen."]},{"id":"NUC-066","q":"What is 'Mass Defect'?","m":2,"ans":["The difference between the mass of a nucleus and the sum of the masses of its individual nucleons.","Missing mass."]},{"id":"NUC-067","q":"Which equation relates mass and energy?","m":1,"ans":["$E = mc^2$","Einstein's equation."]},{"id":"NUC-068","q":"Why are radioactive sources kept in lead containers?","m":1,"ans":["Lead is dense enough to absorb most radiation, protecting people.","Shielding."]},{"id":"NUC-069","q":"Can human senses detect radioactivity?","m":1,"ans":["No","Invisible, odorless, tasteless."]},{"id":"NUC-070","q":"What is the charge of an alpha particle?","m":1,"ans":["+2","Two protons."]},{"id":"NUC-071","q":"What is the charge of a beta particle?","m":1,"ans":["-1","One electron."]},{"id":"NUC-072","q":"What is the charge of gamma radiation?","m":1,"ans":["0","Neutral."]},{"id":"NUC-073","q":"Explain why the mass of an atom is concentrated in the nucleus.","m":2,"ans":["Because protons and neutrons are much heavier than electrons.","Protons/Neutrons weigh more."]},{"id":"NUC-074","q":"What force holds the nucleus together?","m":1,"ans":["Strong Nuclear Force.","Strong force."]},{"id":"NUC-075","q":"Why do isotopes of an element have the same chemical properties?","m":2,"ans":["They have the same number and arrangement of electrons.","Same electron structure."]},{"id":"NUC-076","q":"What is 'Radon-222'?","m":1,"ans":["A radioactive gas that contributes significantly to background radiation.","Natural gas source."]},{"id":"NUC-077","q":"How do you calculate corrected count rate?","m":1,"ans":["Measured count rate - Background count rate.","Subtract background."]},{"id":"NUC-078","q":"Is radioactive decay a chemical or nuclear process?","m":1,"ans":["Nuclear process.","Involves the nucleus."]},{"id":"NUC-079","q":"What is 'Nuclear Waste'?","m":2,"ans":["The radioactive materials left over from nuclear reactors or medical use.","Used radioactive material."]},{"id":"NUC-080","q":"How is high-level nuclear waste stored?","m":2,"ans":["Sealed in glass/steel and buried deep underground in stable rock.","Deep burial."]},{"id":"NUC-081","q":"State one advantage of nuclear power.","m":1,"ans":["No CO2 emissions / High energy density.","Clean/Powerful."]},{"id":"NUC-082","q":"State one disadvantage of nuclear power.","m":1,"ans":["Risk of accidents / Difficulty in disposing of waste.","Waste/Accident risk."]},{"id":"NUC-083","q":"What is the daughter nucleus?","m":1,"ans":["The nucleus formed after a radioactive decay event.","Product nucleus."]},{"id":"NUC-084","q":"What is the parent nucleus?","m":1,"ans":["The original unstable nucleus before decay.","Original nucleus."]},{"id":"NUC-085","q":"If a sample has 1/16th of its original activity, how many half-lives have passed?","m":2,"ans":["4","1/2, 1/4, 1/8, 1/16."]},{"id":"NUC-086","q":"Why is gamma used for medical tracers instead of alpha?","m":2,"ans":["Gamma can pass out of the body to be detected; alpha would be absorbed and cause damage.","Penetrating power."]},{"id":"NUC-087","q":"What is 'Ionizing Radiation'?","m":2,"ans":["Radiation that has enough energy to knock electrons off atoms.","Makes ions."]},{"id":"NUC-088","q":"Why is fusion fuel (hydrogen) easier to find than fission fuel (uranium)?","m":1,"ans":["Hydrogen is abundant in water/oceans.","Abundance."]},{"id":"NUC-089","q":"What is 'Critical Mass'?","m":2,"ans":["The minimum amount of fissile material needed to maintain a chain reaction.","Minimum mass for fission."]},{"id":"NUC-090","q":"What is 'Thermal Neutron'?","m":1,"ans":["A slow-moving neutron that is more likely to cause fission.","Slow neutron."]},{"id":"NUC-091","q":"State the speed of gamma radiation.","m":1,"ans":["$3 \times 10^8$ m/s","Speed of light."]},{"id":"NUC-092","q":"How many protons are in $^{12}_6C$?","m":1,"ans":["6","Atomic number."]},{"id":"NUC-093","q":"How many neutrons are in $^{14}_6C$?","m":1,"ans":["8","14 - 6 = 8."]},{"id":"NUC-094","q":"Which type of radiation consists of electromagnetic waves?","m":1,"ans":["Gamma","Gamma"]},{"id":"NUC-095","q":"What happens to the activity of a source as it decays?","m":1,"ans":["It decreases exponentially.","Activity drops over time."]},{"id":"NUC-096","q":"Can you speed up a half-life by heating the sample?","m":1,"ans":["No","Half-life is constant."]},{"id":"NUC-097","q":"What is the mass of a beta particle compared to a proton?","m":1,"ans":["Approximately 1/1840th.","Negligible."]},{"id":"NUC-098","q":"What is an 'Alpha emitter'?","m":1,"ans":["A radioactive substance that primarily decays by emitting alpha particles.","Alpha source."]},{"id":"NUC-099","q":"Define 'Nucleon'.","m":1,"ans":["A particle found in the nucleus (either a proton or a neutron).","P or N."]},{"id":"NUC-100","q":"What is the net charge of an atom that has lost two electrons?","m":1,"ans":["+2","Positive ion."]}]},{"chapter":"Space Physics","questions":[{"id":"Solar_System_1","q":"Name the eight planets in order of increasing distance from the Sun.","m":1,"ans":["Mercury, Venus, Earth, Mars, Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus, Neptune."]},{"id":"Orbital_Speed_2","q":"Calculate the orbital speed of a planet if its orbital radius is $1.5 \times 10^{11}$ m and its orbital period is $3.15 \times 10^7$ s.","m":2,"ans":["$v = 2\\pi r / T$","$v = (2 * 3.14 * 1.5 \times 10^{11}) / 3.15 \times 10^7 = 29,900$ m/s."]},{"id":"Sun's_Energy_3","q":"Identify the process by which the Sun releases energy and the fuel it consumes.","m":2,"ans":["Nuclear Fusion.","Hydrogen nuclei fusing into Helium."]},{"id":"Light_Year_4","q":"Define a 'light-year'.","m":1,"ans":["The distance traveled by light in a vacuum in one year ($9.5 \times 10^{15}$ m)."]},{"id":"Star_Life_Cycle_5","q":"Describe the formation of a star from a nebula.","m":2,"ans":["Gravitational attraction pulls dust and gas together.","The cloud collapses, GPE is converted to thermal energy, and a protostar forms."]},{"id":"Stable_Stars_6","q":"Explain why a 'Main Sequence' star is stable.","m":1,"ans":["The inward force of gravity is balanced by the outward pressure from nuclear fusion."]},{"id":"Redshift_7","q":"What is meant by 'redshift'?","m":1,"ans":["The increase in the observed wavelength of light from distant galaxies as they move away from us."]},{"id":"Big_Bang_Theory_8","q":"State two pieces of evidence for the Big Bang Theory.","m":2,"ans":["Redshift of distant galaxies.","Cosmic Microwave Background Radiation (CMBR)."]},{"id":"Hubble's_Law_9","q":"State Hubble's Law and the formula associated with it.","m":2,"ans":["The recessional velocity of a galaxy is proportional to its distance from Earth.","$v = H_0 d$."]},{"id":"Age_of_Universe_10","q":"How can the age of the universe be estimated from the Hubble constant ($H_0$)?","m":1,"ans":["Age $T = 1 / H_0$."]},{"id":"Planetary_Characteristics_11","q":"Compare the composition of the inner four planets to the outer four planets.","m":2,"ans":["Inner: Rocky/Terrestrial.","Outer: Gaseous (Gas Giants)."]},{"id":"Gravity_12","q":"What provides the centripetal force required to keep a moon in orbit around a planet?","m":1,"ans":["Gravitational attraction."]},{"id":"Star_Death_13","q":"What determines whether a star becomes a White Dwarf or a Black Hole at the end of its life?","m":1,"ans":["The initial mass of the star."]},{"id":"Supernova_14","q":"Define a 'Supernova'.","m":1,"ans":["The explosive death of a high-mass star that ejects its outer layers into space."]},{"id":"Milky_Way_15","q":"Name the galaxy that our Solar System belongs to and describe its shape.","m":2,"ans":["The Milky Way.","Spiral galaxy."]},{"id":"Asteroids/Comets_16","q":"Where is the Asteroid Belt located?","m":1,"ans":["Between the orbits of Mars and Jupiter."]},{"id":"Comet_Orbits_17","q":"Describe the shape of a comet's orbit.","m":1,"ans":["Highly elliptical."]},{"id":"Orbital_Velocity_18","q":"If the orbital radius of a satellite decreases, what happens to its orbital speed?","m":1,"ans":["It must increase to maintain the orbit."]},{"id":"Star_Temperature_19","q":"How does the color of a star relate to its surface temperature?","m":1,"ans":["Blue stars are hotter; Red stars are cooler."]},{"id":"Cosmology_20","q":"What does the CMBR stand for?","m":1,"ans":["Cosmic Microwave Background Radiation."]},{"id":"Planetary_Motion_21","q":"State Kepler's third law in simple terms.","m":1,"ans":["The further a planet is from the Sun, the longer its orbital period."]},{"id":"Red_Giants_22","q":"What causes a star to leave the main sequence and become a Red Giant?","m":1,"ans":["The hydrogen in the core runs out; the core collapses and the outer layers expand and cool."]},{"id":"Neutron_Stars_23","q":"What is a neutron star?","m":1,"ans":["The incredibly dense remnant of a high-mass star consisting almost entirely of neutrons."]},{"id":"Hubble_Constant_24","q":"State the approximate value of the Hubble Constant (SI units).","m":1,"ans":["$2.2 \times 10^{-18}$ s⁻¹."]},{"id":"Black_Holes_25","q":"Why is a black hole 'black'?","m":1,"ans":["Its gravitational pull is so strong that even light cannot escape it."]},{"id":"Speed_of_Light_26","q":"Calculate the time taken for light from the Sun to reach Earth ($d = 1.5 \times 10^8$ km).","m":1,"ans":["$t = d/v = (1.5 \times 10^{11}) / (3 \times 10^8) = 500$ s (approx 8.3 minutes)."]},{"id":"Circular_Orbit_27","q":"Why does the speed of a planet in a circular orbit remain constant while its velocity changes?","m":1,"ans":["Velocity is a vector; the direction is constantly changing even if the magnitude (speed) is not."]},{"id":"Protoplanetary_Disks_28","q":"What formed the planets and moons in the early solar system?","m":1,"ans":["Accretion of dust and gas in the disk surrounding the young Sun."]},{"id":"Exoplanets_29","q":"What is an 'exoplanet'?","m":1,"ans":["A planet that orbits a star other than our Sun."]},{"id":"Galaxies_30","q":"Define a 'galaxy'.","m":1,"ans":["A massive collection of billions of stars, dust, and gas held together by gravity."]},{"id":"Solar_Energy_31","q":"Why is it impossible for humans to recreate the conditions for fusion on Earth easily?","m":1,"ans":["Extreme temperature and pressure are required to overcome the repulsion between nuclei."]},{"id":"Redshift_Distances_32","q":"A galaxy shows a very large redshift. What does this tell you about its distance and speed?","m":1,"ans":["It is very far away and moving away at a very high speed."]},{"id":"White_Dwarf_33","q":"Describe a white dwarf.","m":1,"ans":["The small, hot, dense remnant of a low-mass star."]},{"id":"Planetary_Mass_34","q":"Which planet in our solar system has the greatest mass?","m":1,"ans":["Jupiter."]},{"id":"Atmospheric_Refraction_35","q":"Why do stars 'twinkle' when viewed from Earth?","m":1,"ans":["Light is refracted as it passes through different layers of Earth's atmosphere."]},{"id":"Gravity_Variation_36","q":"How does gravitational field strength 'g' change as you move further from a planet?","m":1,"ans":["It decreases."]},{"id":"Black_Dwarf_37","q":"What is a black dwarf?","m":1,"ans":["A white dwarf that has cooled down completely and no longer emits light."]},{"id":"Planetary_Year_38","q":"Why is a year on Neptune much longer than a year on Earth?","m":1,"ans":["It is much further from the Sun, so it has a larger orbital path and travels slower."]},{"id":"Binary_Stars_39","q":"What is a binary star system?","m":1,"ans":["Two stars that orbit a common center of mass."]},{"id":"Singularity_40","q":"What is the center of a black hole called?","m":1,"ans":["A singularity."]},{"id":"Cosmic_Expansion_41","q":"If galaxies are moving away from each other, what can we conclude about the universe's size?","m":1,"ans":["The universe is expanding."]},{"id":"Star_Brightness_42","q":"Distinguish between apparent magnitude and absolute magnitude.","m":1,"ans":["Apparent: Brightness as seen from Earth. Absolute: Actual brightness at a standard distance."]},{"id":"Nuclear_Fuel_43","q":"What element is produced when Helium fuses in a Red Giant?","m":1,"ans":["Carbon."]},{"id":"Satellite_Motion_44","q":"Does a satellite in orbit need fuel to keep moving? Explain.","m":1,"ans":["No. Once in orbit, gravity provides the centripetal force; it is in a state of 'perpetual fall'."]},{"id":"Solar_System_Age_45","q":"What is the estimated age of the Solar System?","m":1,"ans":["4.6 billion years."]},{"id":"Galactic_Center_46","q":"What is believed to sit at the center of most large galaxies?","m":1,"ans":["A supermassive black hole."]},{"id":"Planetary_Heat_47","q":"Why is Venus hotter than Mercury despite being further from the Sun?","m":1,"ans":["It has a thick atmosphere of $CO_2$ that traps heat (Greenhouse effect)."]},{"id":"Hubble_Constant_48","q":"What is the unit for $H_0$ usually used by astronomers?","m":1,"ans":["km/s per Megaparsec (Mpc)."]},{"id":"Dark_Matter_49","q":"What is 'dark matter'?","m":1,"ans":["Unseen mass that does not emit light but exerts gravitational pull on visible matter."]},{"id":"Final_Outcome_50","q":"What will be the final stage of our Sun?","m":1,"ans":["A Black Dwarf (after passing through the Red Giant and White Dwarf stages)."]}]},{"chapter":"Thermal Physics Basics","questions":[{"id":"THM-001","q":"State the three states of matter.","m":1,"ans":["Solid, Liquid, and Gas.","Solid, Liquid, Gas."]},{"id":"THM-002","q":"Describe the arrangement of particles in a solid.","m":2,"ans":["Particles are closely packed in a regular lattice arrangement.","Regular, close-packed."]},{"id":"THM-003","q":"Describe the motion of particles in a solid.","m":1,"ans":["They vibrate about fixed positions.","Vibrate only."]},{"id":"THM-004","q":"Describe the arrangement of particles in a liquid.","m":2,"ans":["Particles are close together but in an irregular/random arrangement.","Random, close-packed."]},{"id":"THM-005","q":"Describe the motion of particles in a gas.","m":2,"ans":["They move randomly at high speeds in all directions.","Fast, random motion."]},{"id":"THM-006","q":"Define 'Brownian Motion'.","m":2,"ans":["The random movement of microscopic particles (like smoke or pollen) suspended in a fluid.","Random motion of suspended particles."]},{"id":"THM-007","q":"What causes Brownian motion?","m":2,"ans":["Collisions between the visible particles and much smaller, fast-moving invisible molecules of the fluid.","Collisions with fluid molecules."]},{"id":"THM-008","q":"What evidence does Brownian motion provide?","m":2,"ans":["Evidence for the kinetic theory of matter (that matter is made of moving particles).","Kinetic theory evidence."]},{"id":"THM-009","q":"Define 'Evaporation'.","m":2,"ans":["The escape of more energetic molecules from the surface of a liquid below its boiling point.","Surface escaping of molecules."]},{"id":"THM-010","q":"State three factors that increase the rate of evaporation.","m":3,"ans":["1. Increase in temperature. 2. Increase in surface area. 3. Increase in air movement (wind).","Temp, Area, Wind."]},{"id":"THM-011","q":"Explain why evaporation causes cooling.","m":2,"ans":["The fastest (most energetic) molecules leave the liquid, reducing the average kinetic energy of the remaining molecules.","Loss of high-energy particles."]},{"id":"THM-012","q":"Define 'Internal Energy'.","m":2,"ans":["The sum of the random distribution of kinetic and potential energies of particles in a system.","KE + PE of particles."]},{"id":"THM-013","q":"What physical property changes when an object's temperature increases?","m":1,"ans":["The average kinetic energy of its particles increases.","Higher KE."]},{"id":"THM-014","q":"Define 'Specific Heat Capacity' ($c$).","m":2,"ans":["The amount of thermal energy required to raise the temperature of 1 kg of a substance by 1°C.","$c = Q / (m\\Delta\theta)$"]},{"id":"THM-015","q":"State the formula for thermal energy transfer during temperature change.","m":1,"ans":["$Q = mc\\Delta\theta$","mass x SHC x temp change"]},{"id":"THM-016","q":"Define 'Heat Capacity' ($C$).","m":2,"ans":["The amount of thermal energy required to raise the temperature of the whole object by 1°C.","$C = mc$"]},{"id":"THM-017","q":"State the SI unit for Specific Heat Capacity.","m":1,"ans":["J/(kg·°C) or J/(kg·K)","J/kg°C"]},{"id":"THM-018","q":"Define 'Melting Point'.","m":1,"ans":["The constant temperature at which a solid turns into a liquid.","Solid to Liquid temp."]},{"id":"THM-019","q":"Define 'Boiling Point'.","m":1,"ans":["The constant temperature at which a liquid turns into a gas throughout its volume.","Liquid to Gas temp."]},{"id":"THM-020","q":"What happens to the temperature of a substance during a state change?","m":1,"ans":["It remains constant.","No temp change during melting/boiling."]},{"id":"THM-021","q":"Define 'Specific Latent Heat of Fusion' ($l_f$).","m":2,"ans":["The thermal energy required to change 1 kg of a solid to a liquid without a change in temperature.","$l_f = Q / m$"]},{"id":"THM-022","q":"Define 'Specific Latent Heat of Vaporization' ($l_v$).","m":2,"ans":["The thermal energy required to change 1 kg of a liquid to a gas without a change in temperature.","$l_v = Q / m$"]},{"id":"THM-023","q":"During melting, what happens to the energy supplied if the temperature doesn't rise?","m":2,"ans":["The energy is used to break the bonds/attractive forces between particles.","Breaking bonds."]},{"id":"THM-024","q":"Why is $l_v$ usually much larger than $l_f$ for the same substance?","m":2,"ans":["Much more energy is needed to completely separate particles (liquid to gas) than to just loosen them (solid to liquid).","Separation vs Loosening."]},{"id":"THM-025","q":"Calculate the energy needed to heat 2 kg of water from 20°C to 80°C ($c = 4200$ J/kg°C).","m":2,"ans":["504,000 J","2 * 4200 * 60"]},{"id":"THM-026","q":"Define 'Absolute Zero'.","m":2,"ans":["The theoretical temperature at which particles have minimum internal energy (-273°C or 0 K).","-273°C"]},{"id":"THM-027","q":"How do you convert Celsius to Kelvin?","m":1,"ans":["$T(K) = \theta(°C) + 273$","Add 273."]},{"id":"THM-028","q":"What happens to the pressure of a gas if the temperature increases at constant volume?","m":2,"ans":["Pressure increases because particles move faster and hit the walls more frequently and with more force.","Pressure increases."]},{"id":"THM-029","q":"State Boyle's Law.","m":2,"ans":["For a fixed mass of gas at constant temperature, pressure is inversely proportional to volume.","$PV = Constant$"]},{"id":"THM-030","q":"A gas has a volume of 2 m³ at 100 kPa. What is the volume at 200 kPa (temp constant)?","m":2,"ans":["1 m³","$100 * 2 = 200 * V_2$"]},{"id":"THM-031","q":"Define 'Thermal Expansion'.","m":1,"ans":["The increase in volume/size of a substance as its temperature increases.","Expanding when hot."]},{"id":"THM-032","q":"Which state of matter expands the most for the same temperature rise?","m":1,"ans":["Gas","Gas expands most."]},{"id":"THM-033","q":"State one practical application of thermal expansion.","m":1,"ans":["Bimetallic strips (thermostats) / Liquid-in-glass thermometers.","Thermostats."]},{"id":"THM-034","q":"Why are gaps left in railway tracks?","m":2,"ans":["To allow for thermal expansion during hot weather, preventing the rails from buckling.","Prevent buckling."]},{"id":"THM-035","q":"Define 'Conduction'.","m":2,"ans":["The transfer of thermal energy through a substance without overall movement of the substance itself.","Vibration transfer."]},{"id":"THM-036","q":"Why are metals good thermal conductors?","m":2,"ans":["They have free/delocalized electrons that can quickly transfer energy through collisions.","Free electrons."]},{"id":"THM-037","q":"Define 'Convection'.","m":2,"ans":["The transfer of thermal energy in a fluid by the movement of the fluid itself (convection currents).","Fluid movement transfer."]},{"id":"THM-038","q":"Why does convection not happen in solids?","m":1,"ans":["Solid particles are fixed in position and cannot flow.","Particles can't move."]},{"id":"THM-039","q":"Define 'Radiation' (thermal).","m":2,"ans":["The transfer of energy by electromagnetic waves (infrared), which does not require a medium.","Infrared waves."]},{"id":"THM-040","q":"Which surface is the best absorber of thermal radiation?","m":1,"ans":["Dull, black surfaces.","Dull black."]},{"id":"THM-041","q":"Which surface is the best emitter of thermal radiation?","m":1,"ans":["Dull, black surfaces.","Dull black."]},{"id":"THM-042","q":"Which surface is the best reflector of thermal radiation?","m":1,"ans":["Shiny, silver/white surfaces.","Shiny silver."]},{"id":"THM-043","q":"Explain how a vacuum flask reduces heat loss by conduction.","m":1,"ans":["The vacuum space between the walls contains no particles.","No medium for conduction."]},{"id":"THM-044","q":"Explain how a vacuum flask reduces heat loss by radiation.","m":1,"ans":["Silvered surfaces reflect radiation back into or out of the flask.","Silvering reflects IR."]},{"id":"THM-045","q":"Why are double-glazed windows effective?","m":2,"ans":["The air trapped between the panes is a poor conductor, and the gap is too narrow for convection currents.","Air is a good insulator."]},{"id":"THM-046","q":"Define 'Sensitivity' of a thermometer.","m":2,"ans":["The change in the physical property per unit change in temperature (e.g., mm per °C).","Large change for small temp."]},{"id":"THM-047","q":"Define 'Range' of a thermometer.","m":1,"ans":["The difference between the maximum and minimum temperatures it can measure.","Max to Min."]},{"id":"THM-048","q":"Define 'Linearity' in a thermometer.","m":2,"ans":["When the physical property changes by equal amounts for equal changes in temperature.","Equal spacing on scale."]},{"id":"THM-049","q":"How can you increase the sensitivity of a liquid-in-glass thermometer?","m":2,"ans":["Use a thinner capillary tube or a larger bulb.","Thin tube/Big bulb."]},{"id":"THM-050","q":"State the fixed points used for the Celsius scale.","m":2,"ans":["Ice point (0°C) and Steam point (100°C) at standard atmospheric pressure.","0 and 100."]},{"id":"THM-051","q":"What is a 'Thermocouple'?","m":2,"ans":["A thermometer made of two different wires joined at two junctions; a voltage is produced when the junctions are at different temperatures.","Two-wire voltage probe."]},{"id":"THM-052","q":"State one advantage of a thermocouple.","m":1,"ans":["Can measure very high temperatures / responds very quickly / small size.","Quick/High range."]},{"id":"THM-053","q":"Why is ice used to cool drinks instead of cold water at 0°C?","m":2,"ans":["Ice absorbs latent heat to melt, taking more energy from the drink than 0°C water would.","Latent heat absorption."]},{"id":"THM-054","q":"Describe the arrangement of molecules in a gas.","m":1,"ans":["Very far apart and randomly arranged.","Sparse, random."]},{"id":"THM-055","q":"What is 'Condensation'?","m":1,"ans":["The process where a gas turns into a liquid.","Gas to Liquid."]},{"id":"THM-056","q":"What is 'Solidification' (Freezing)?","m":1,"ans":["The process where a liquid turns into a solid.","Liquid to Solid."]},{"id":"THM-057","q":"Explain why metals feel colder than wood at the same room temperature.","m":2,"ans":["Metals are better conductors and carry heat away from your hand faster.","Better conductor."]},{"id":"THM-058","q":"State the relationship between Pressure and Volume in words.","m":1,"ans":["Pressure is inversely proportional to Volume.","V doubles, P halves."]},{"id":"THM-059","q":"What is 'Sublimation'?","m":1,"ans":["The process where a solid turns directly into a gas without becoming a liquid.","Solid to Gas."]},{"id":"THM-060","q":"Which state of matter has the strongest intermolecular forces?","m":1,"ans":["Solid","Solid"]},{"id":"THM-061","q":"Calculate the heat energy required to melt 500g of ice ($l_f = 334,000$ J/kg).","m":2,"ans":["167,000 J","0.5 * 334,000"]},{"id":"THM-062","q":"Why does a gas exert pressure?","m":2,"ans":["Molecules collide with container walls and exert a force over the area.","Wall collisions."]},{"id":"THM-063","q":"If you double the Kelvin temperature of a gas in a fixed volume, what happens to the pressure?","m":1,"ans":["It doubles.","$P \\propto T(K)$"]},{"id":"THM-064","q":"What is a 'Good Emitter' also good at?","m":1,"ans":["Absorbing (Good Absorber).","Black surfaces."]},{"id":"THM-065","q":"Why does hot air rise?","m":2,"ans":["It expands, becomes less dense than the surrounding cold air, and is pushed upwards.","Density decrease."]},{"id":"THM-066","q":"A 100W heater is used for 2 minutes to heat 1kg of metal by 10°C. Calculate the SHC.","m":3,"ans":["1200 J/kg°C","$(100 * 120) / (1 * 10)$"]},{"id":"THM-067","q":"Define 'Melting' in terms of kinetic theory.","m":2,"ans":["Molecules gain enough energy to overcome the regular lattice structure of a solid.","Overcoming lattice bonds."]},{"id":"THM-068","q":"State the unit for Latent Heat.","m":1,"ans":["J/kg","Joules per kilogram"]},{"id":"THM-069","q":"Why does a person sweat to stay cool?","m":2,"ans":["Sweat evaporates from the skin, taking latent heat from the body.","Evaporative cooling."]},{"id":"THM-070","q":"How is heat transferred from the Sun to Earth?","m":1,"ans":["Radiation.","IR Radiation."]},{"id":"THM-071","q":"What happens to the volume of water when it freezes?","m":1,"ans":["It increases (expands).","Becomes less dense."]},{"id":"THM-072","q":"Define 'Calibrating' a thermometer.","m":2,"ans":["Marking a scale on a thermometer by using fixed points.","Setting the scale."]},{"id":"THM-073","q":"Which state of matter is the most compressible?","m":1,"ans":["Gas","Gas"]},{"id":"THM-074","q":"What is a 'Thermometric Property'?","m":2,"ans":["A physical property that changes measurably and continuously with temperature.","Varying property (e.g. Volume)."]},{"id":"THM-075","q":"Give an example of a thermometric property.","m":1,"ans":["Volume of liquid / Resistance of a wire / Voltage of a thermocouple.","Liquid volume."]},{"id":"THM-076","q":"Why does a balloon pop if left in a hot car?","m":2,"ans":["Temperature increases, pressure increases until the material cannot withstand the force.","Pressure/Volume increase."]},{"id":"THM-077","q":"Describe a cooling curve for a substance being frozen.","m":2,"ans":["Temp drops, stays flat (horizontal) during freezing, then drops again.","Flat line at freezing."]},{"id":"THM-078","q":"What is the 'Ice Point'?","m":1,"ans":["The temperature of pure melting ice (0°C).","0°C"]},{"id":"THM-079","q":"What is the 'Steam Point'?","m":1,"ans":["The temperature of steam above pure boiling water at 1 atm (100°C).","100°C"]},{"id":"THM-080","q":"Why is the cooling fin of a motorcycle painted black?","m":1,"ans":["To emit thermal radiation more efficiently into the surroundings.","Better emission."]},{"id":"THM-081","q":"Explain 'Sea Breeze' in terms of convection.","m":3,"ans":["Land heats faster than sea; air above land rises; cooler air from sea moves in to replace it.","Convection current."]},{"id":"THM-082","q":"What happens to the potential energy of particles when a gas condenses?","m":1,"ans":["It decreases.","Particles get closer."]},{"id":"THM-083","q":"Calculate the power of a heater that boils away 100g of water in 5 minutes ($l_v = 2,260,000$ J/kg).","m":3,"ans":["753 W","$(0.1 * 2260000) / 300$"]},{"id":"THM-084","q":"Why is trapped air a good insulator?","m":1,"ans":["Air is a poor conductor and trapping it prevents convection.","Prevents conduction/convection."]},{"id":"THM-085","q":"Which state of matter has a fixed volume but no fixed shape?","m":1,"ans":["Liquid","Liquid"]},{"id":"THM-086","q":"What is the relation between pressure and volume if temperature is kept in Kelvin?","m":1,"ans":["$P \\propto 1/V$","Inversely proportional."]},{"id":"THM-087","q":"Why are some pots made of copper bottoms?","m":1,"ans":["Copper is an excellent thermal conductor.","Efficient heating."]},{"id":"THM-088","q":"What color would you paint a house in a very hot climate?","m":1,"ans":["White / Light colors.","Reflects radiation."]},{"id":"THM-089","q":"Define 'Vaporization'.","m":1,"ans":["The change of state from liquid to gas (includes boiling and evaporation).","Liquid to Gas."]},{"id":"THM-090","q":"Why is there no temperature change during boiling?","m":2,"ans":["Energy is used to overcome intermolecular forces rather than increasing KE.","Potential energy increase only."]},{"id":"THM-091","q":"How does a clinical thermometer differ from a laboratory thermometer?","m":2,"ans":["It has a small range (35-42°C) and a constriction to prevent liquid falling back.","Constriction/Short range."]},{"id":"THM-092","q":"What is the 'upper fixed point' of the Celsius scale?","m":1,"ans":["100°C","100°C"]},{"id":"THM-093","q":"What is the 'lower fixed point' of the Celsius scale?","m":1,"ans":["0°C","0°C"]},{"id":"THM-094","q":"Why does a bimetallic strip bend when heated?","m":2,"ans":["The two metals expand at different rates.","Unequal expansion."]},{"id":"THM-095","q":"Calculate the temperature in Kelvin of a room at 25°C.","m":1,"ans":["298 K","25 + 273"]},{"id":"THM-096","q":"Calculate the temperature in Celsius of a star at 6000 K.","m":1,"ans":["5727°C","6000 - 273"]},{"id":"THM-097","q":"State the effect of increasing surface area on evaporation.","m":1,"ans":["Evaporation rate increases.","Faster evaporation."]},{"id":"THM-098","q":"Does radiation require a medium?","m":1,"ans":["No","Works in a vacuum."]},{"id":"THM-099","q":"What is the boiling point of water in Kelvin?","m":1,"ans":["373 K","100 + 273"]},{"id":"THM-100","q":"What is the freezing point of water in Kelvin?","m":1,"ans":["273 K","0 + 273"]}]},{"chapter":"Thermal Physics","questions":[{"id":"Kinetic_Model_1","q":"Compare the motion and arrangement of molecules in a liquid and a gas.","m":2,"ans":["Arrangement: Liquid molecules are close together/touching but random; Gas molecules are far apart/spaced out.","Motion: Liquid molecules slide over each other; Gas molecules move rapidly and randomly in all directions."]},{"id":"Brownian_Motion_2","q":"In the smoke cell experiment, small bright specks are seen moving randomly. Explain this observation.","m":2,"ans":["Bright specks are smoke particles reflecting light.","Random motion is caused by uneven collisions with invisible, fast-moving air molecules."]},{"id":"Evaporation_3","q":"Explain, in terms of molecules, why evaporation causes cooling.","m":3,"ans":["The most energetic molecules escape from the surface of the liquid.","This lowers the average kinetic energy of the remaining molecules.","Since temperature is proportional to average KE, the temperature drops."]},{"id":"Pressure_and_Temperature_4","q":"Explain why the pressure of a gas in a fixed volume increases when heated.","m":3,"ans":["Molecules move faster/have more kinetic energy.","They collide with the walls more frequently.","They collide with greater force, increasing the total pressure."]},{"id":"Thermal_Expansion_5","q":"A bimetallic strip consists of brass and iron. Brass expands more than iron. Sketch the strip when heated.","m":1,"ans":["The strip curves with the brass on the outside of the arc."]},{"id":"Specific_Heat_Capacity_6","q":"Calculate the energy required to raise the temperature of 2.0 kg of copper (c = 400 J/kg°C) from 20°C to 100°C.","m":1,"ans":["Q = mcΔθ = 2.0 * 400 * 80 = 64,000 J."]},{"id":"Latent_Heat_7","q":"Define specific latent heat of fusion.","m":1,"ans":["The thermal energy required to change 1 kg of a substance from solid to liquid without a change in temperature."]},{"id":"Boiling_vs_Evaporation_8","q":"State two differences between boiling and evaporation.","m":2,"ans":["Boiling occurs throughout the liquid; evaporation only at the surface.","Boiling occurs at a fixed temperature; evaporation occurs at any temperature."]},{"id":"Conduction_9","q":"Explain why metals are better thermal conductors than plastics.","m":2,"ans":["Metals have free/delocalized electrons.","These electrons move quickly through the lattice, transferring energy via collisions with ions."]},{"id":"Convection_10","q":"Describe how a convection current is formed in a beaker of water being heated from below.","m":3,"ans":["Water at the bottom expands and becomes less dense.","The less dense water rises.","Cooler, denser water sinks to take its place, creating a cycle."]},{"id":"Radiation_11","q":"Which surface is the best absorber and best emitter of infra-red radiation?","m":1,"ans":["Dull/matte black surface."]},{"id":"Thermal_Capacity_12","q":"A 500W heater is used for 2 minutes to heat a block. The temperature rises by 10°C. Calculate the thermal capacity of the block.","m":2,"ans":["Energy = 500 * 120 = 60,000 J.","Thermal Capacity (C) = Q / Δθ = 60,000 / 10 = 6,000 J/°C."]},{"id":"Gas_Laws_13","q":"A gas occupies 2.0 m³ at 100 kPa. If the volume is reduced to 0.5 m³ at constant temperature, find the new pressure.","m":2,"ans":["P1V1 = P2V2 => 100 * 2.0 = P2 * 0.5.","P2 = 400 kPa."]},{"id":"Thermometers_14","q":"Name two physical properties that can be used to measure temperature.","m":2,"ans":["Expansion of a liquid (mercury/alcohol).","Voltage/EMF of a thermocouple."]},{"id":"Latent_Heat_Calculation_15","q":"How much energy is needed to melt 0.5 kg of ice at 0°C? (Lf = 334,000 J/kg).","m":1,"ans":["Q = mLf = 0.5 * 334,000 = 167,000 J."]},{"id":"States_of_Matter_16","q":"Why is the temperature constant during a change of state even though heat is being supplied?","m":1,"ans":["The energy is used to break the bonds/overcome forces between molecules rather than increasing their kinetic energy."]},{"id":"Radiation_17","q":"Why are houses in hot countries often painted white?","m":1,"ans":["White is a poor absorber/good reflector of infra-red radiation from the Sun, keeping the house cool."]},{"id":"Sensitivity_18","q":"How can the sensitivity of a liquid-in-glass thermometer be increased?","m":1,"ans":["Use a narrower capillary tube or a larger bulb."]},{"id":"Thermocouple_19","q":"State one advantage of a thermocouple over a liquid-in-glass thermometer.","m":1,"ans":["Can measure rapidly changing temperatures / can measure very high temperatures."]},{"id":"Fixed_Points_20","q":"What are the two fixed points on the Celsius scale?","m":1,"ans":["Melting point of pure ice (0°C) and boiling point of pure water at standard atmospheric pressure (100°C)."]},{"id":"Kinetic_Theory_21","q":"What happens to the internal energy of a gas when it is compressed rapidly?","m":1,"ans":["Internal energy increases because work is done on the gas, which increases the kinetic energy of the molecules."]},{"id":"Linear_Expansion_22","q":"Why are gaps left between rails on a railway track?","m":1,"ans":["To allow for expansion during hot weather, preventing the rails from buckling."]},{"id":"Thermal_Energy_Transfer_23","q":"Explain how a vacuum flask reduces heat loss by radiation.","m":1,"ans":["The silvered inner surfaces reflect infra-red radiation back into the flask (or prevent it from entering)."]},{"id":"Specific_Heat_24","q":"Why does a 1 kg block of iron heat up faster than 1 kg of water when supplied with the same power?","m":1,"ans":["Iron has a lower specific heat capacity than water."]},{"id":"Gas_Molecules_25","q":"Describe the change in the frequency of collisions when a gas is compressed into a smaller volume at constant temperature.","m":1,"ans":["Frequency of collisions increases because the molecules are closer together and have less distance to travel between the walls."]},{"id":"Evaporation_Rate_26","q":"List three factors that increase the rate of evaporation.","m":1,"ans":["Higher temperature, larger surface area, and draught/wind across the surface."]},{"id":"Heat_Transfer_27","q":"In which state(s) of matter does convection primarily occur? Why?","m":1,"ans":["Fluids (liquids and gases). Because the particles are free to move and change density."]},{"id":"Thermal_Equilibrium_28","q":"Define thermal equilibrium.","m":1,"ans":["A state where two objects in contact are at the same temperature and there is no net flow of thermal energy between them."]},{"id":"Melting_Point_29","q":"How does adding salt affect the melting point of ice?","m":1,"ans":["It lowers the melting point."]},{"id":"Pressure_Law_30","q":"State the relationship between pressure and Kelvin temperature for a fixed mass of gas at constant volume.","m":1,"ans":["Pressure is directly proportional to the absolute (Kelvin) temperature ($P \\propto T$)."]},{"id":"Range_31","q":"What is meant by the 'range' of a thermometer?","m":1,"ans":["The difference between the maximum and minimum temperatures it can measure."]},{"id":"Thermal_Conductivity_32","q":"Explain why a tile floor feels colder to bare feet than a carpeted floor at the same temperature.","m":1,"ans":["Tiles are better conductors; they conduct thermal energy away from the feet much faster than the carpet."]},{"id":"Specific_Heat_Capacity_33","q":"0.1 kg of water at 80°C is mixed with 0.1 kg of water at 20°C. Calculate the final temperature.","m":1,"ans":["Energy lost = Energy gained. Since masses and 'c' are equal, it is the average: (80 + 20) / 2 = 50°C."]},{"id":"Vacuum_Flask_34","q":"How does the vacuum in a flask reduce heat transfer?","m":1,"ans":["Prevents conduction and convection as these require a medium (particles) to transfer energy."]},{"id":"Absolute_Zero_35","q":"What is the value of absolute zero in Celsius?","m":1,"ans":["-273°C."]},{"id":"Boyle's_Law_36","q":"Sketch a graph of Pressure vs 1/Volume for a gas at constant temperature.","m":1,"ans":["A straight line passing through the origin."]},{"id":"Radiation_37","q":"Can radiation travel through a vacuum? Explain.","m":1,"ans":["Yes, it consists of electromagnetic waves (infra-red) which do not require a medium."]},{"id":"Linearity_38","q":"What is a 'linear' scale on a thermometer?","m":1,"ans":["A scale where equal changes in temperature produce equal changes in the physical property (e.g., length of liquid column)."]},{"id":"Condensation_39","q":"Explain condensation in terms of the kinetic theory.","m":1,"ans":["Gas molecules lose kinetic energy as they cool, move slower, and come close enough for attractive forces to pull them together into a liquid."]},{"id":"Latent_Heat_of_Vaporization_40","q":"Calculate the energy to turn 0.2 kg of water at 100°C into steam. (Lv = 2,260,000 J/kg).","m":1,"ans":["Q = mLv = 0.2 * 2,260,000 = 452,000 J."]},{"id":"Conduction_41","q":"Why is air a poor conductor of heat?","m":1,"ans":["Molecules are very far apart, so collisions (which transfer energy) are infrequent."]},{"id":"Experimental_Physics_42","q":"Why is it important to stir the liquid when determining its specific heat capacity?","m":1,"ans":["To ensure a uniform temperature throughout the liquid."]},{"id":"States_of_Matter_43","q":"Which state of matter is the least compressible? Why?","m":1,"ans":["Solid. Particles are already very close together with very strong repulsive forces."]},{"id":"Greenhouse_Effect_44","q":"How does the Earth's atmosphere act like a greenhouse?","m":1,"ans":["Short-wavelength radiation from the Sun passes through; the Earth emits long-wavelength IR which is absorbed/trapped by greenhouse gases."]},{"id":"Expansion_45","q":"Why do liquids generally expand more than solids for the same rise in temperature?","m":1,"ans":["Attractive forces between molecules in liquids are weaker than in solids."]},{"id":"Cooling_Curve_46","q":"In a cooling curve, what does a horizontal section represent?","m":1,"ans":["A change of state (e.g., freezing or condensing)."]},{"id":"Evaporation_47","q":"Why does a breeze speed up the cooling of a hot drink?","m":1,"ans":["It moves the vapor away from the surface, preventing it from re-entering the liquid, thus increasing the rate of evaporation."]},{"id":"Calculations_48","q":"A 2kg block of metal absorbs 10,000 J and its temperature rises by 5°C. Find its specific heat capacity.","m":1,"ans":["c = Q / (mΔθ) = 10,000 / (2 * 5) = 1,000 J/kg°C."]},{"id":"Safety_49","q":"Why can a steam burn be more severe than a boiling water burn at the same temperature?","m":1,"ans":["Steam contains extra energy (latent heat of vaporization) which is released when it condenses on the skin."]},{"id":"Thermometry_50","q":"Convert 300 K to Celsius.","m":1,"ans":["300 - 273 = 27°C."]}]},{"chapter":"Waves Basics","questions":[{"id":"WAV-001","q":"Define a 'Transverse Wave'.","m":2,"ans":["A wave where the oscillations are perpendicular to the direction of energy transfer.","Perpendicular oscillations."]},{"id":"WAV-002","q":"Define a 'Longitudinal Wave'.","m":2,"ans":["A wave where the oscillations are parallel to the direction of energy transfer.","Parallel oscillations."]},{"id":"WAV-003","q":"Give an example of a transverse wave.","m":1,"ans":["Light / Radio / Water ripples / S-waves.","Light."]},{"id":"WAV-004","q":"Give an example of a longitudinal wave.","m":1,"ans":["Sound / Ultrasound / P-waves.","Sound."]},{"id":"WAV-005","q":"Define 'Wavelength' ($\\lambda$).","m":2,"ans":["The distance between two successive identical points on a wave (e.g., crest to crest).","Distance for one full cycle."]},{"id":"WAV-006","q":"Define 'Frequency' ($f$).","m":2,"ans":["The number of complete waves passing a point per second.","Waves per second."]},{"id":"WAV-007","q":"State the SI unit for frequency.","m":1,"ans":["Hertz (Hz)","Hz"]},{"id":"WAV-008","q":"Define 'Amplitude'.","m":2,"ans":["The maximum displacement of a particle from its rest (equilibrium) position.","Height of wave from center."]},{"id":"WAV-009","q":"State the Wave Equation.","m":1,"ans":["$v = f \times \\lambda$","Speed = frequency x wavelength"]},{"id":"WAV-010","q":"Define 'Period' ($T$).","m":1,"ans":["The time taken for one complete wave to pass a point.","$T = 1/f$"]},{"id":"WAV-011","q":"A wave has a frequency of 50 Hz. Calculate its period.","m":2,"ans":["0.02 s","1 / 50"]},{"id":"WAV-012","q":"What is 'Wavefront'?","m":1,"ans":["An imaginary line joining all adjacent points that are in the same phase (e.g., all crests).","Line of crests."]},{"id":"WAV-013","q":"What happens to the speed of water waves when they move from deep to shallow water?","m":1,"ans":["Speed decreases.","Slows down."]},{"id":"WAV-014","q":"What happens to the wavelength of water waves in shallow water?","m":1,"ans":["Wavelength decreases.","Shortens."]},{"id":"WAV-015","q":"Does frequency change when a wave transitions between media?","m":1,"ans":["No","Frequency remains constant."]},{"id":"WAV-016","q":"Define 'Refraction'.","m":2,"ans":["The change in direction of a wave as it passes from one medium to another due to a change in speed.","Bending due to speed change."]},{"id":"WAV-017","q":"State the Law of Reflection.","m":1,"ans":["Angle of incidence ($i$) = Angle of reflection ($r$).","$i = r$"]},{"id":"WAV-018","q":"What is the 'Normal' in a ray diagram?","m":1,"ans":["An imaginary line perpendicular (90°) to the surface at the point of incidence.","Perpendicular line."]},{"id":"WAV-019","q":"State the characteristics of an image formed by a plane mirror.","m":3,"ans":["Virtual, upright, same size, same distance behind mirror, laterally inverted.","Virtual, same size, upright."]},{"id":"WAV-020","q":"Define 'Refractive Index' ($n$).","m":2,"ans":["The ratio of the speed of light in vacuum to the speed of light in the medium.","$n = c/v$ or $n = \\sin(i)/\\sin(r)$"]},{"id":"WAV-021","q":"Light travels from air into glass ($n=1.5$). If $i = 30°$, calculate $r$.","m":2,"ans":["19.5°","$\\sin(r) = \\sin(30)/1.5$"]},{"id":"WAV-022","q":"Define 'Critical Angle' ($c$).","m":2,"ans":["The angle of incidence in the denser medium for which the angle of refraction in the rarer medium is 90°.","Boundary angle for TIR."]},{"id":"WAV-023","q":"State the conditions for 'Total Internal Reflection' (TIR).","m":2,"ans":["1. Light travels from denser to rarer medium. 2. Angle of incidence > Critical angle.","Denser to rarer and $i > c$."]},{"id":"WAV-024","q":"State the formula relating refractive index and critical angle.","m":1,"ans":["$n = 1 / \\sin(c)$","Inverse sine of n."]},{"id":"WAV-025","q":"Calculate the critical angle for glass with $n=1.5$.","m":2,"ans":["41.8°","$\\arcsin(1/1.5)$"]},{"id":"WAV-026","q":"Name one application of optical fibers.","m":1,"ans":["Telecommunications / Endoscopy.","Data transfer."]},{"id":"WAV-027","q":"What is a 'Converging Lens'?","m":1,"ans":["A lens that is thicker in the middle and causes parallel rays to meet at a point.","Convex lens."]},{"id":"WAV-028","q":"Define 'Principal Focus' ($F$).","m":1,"ans":["The point where rays parallel to the principal axis converge after passing through the lens.","Focal point."]},{"id":"WAV-029","q":"Define 'Focal Length' ($f$).","m":1,"ans":["The distance from the center of the lens to the principal focus.","Distance to F."]},{"id":"WAV-030","q":"When is a real image formed by a convex lens?","m":2,"ans":["When the object is placed at a distance greater than the focal length ($u > f$).","Object outside F."]},{"id":"WAV-031","q":"When is a virtual image formed by a convex lens?","m":2,"ans":["When the object is placed between the lens and the principal focus ($u < f$).","Magnifying glass position."]},{"id":"WAV-032","q":"State the colors of the visible spectrum in order.","m":1,"ans":["Red, Orange, Yellow, Green, Blue, Indigo, Violet (ROYGBIV).","Rainbow colors."]},{"id":"WAV-033","q":"Which color of light is refracted most by a prism?","m":1,"ans":["Violet","Violet (shortest wavelength)"]},{"id":"WAV-034","q":"State the speed of all electromagnetic (EM) waves in a vacuum.","m":1,"ans":["$3 \times 10^8$ m/s","Speed of light."]},{"id":"WAV-035","q":"List the EM spectrum in order of increasing frequency.","m":2,"ans":["Radio, Micro, IR, Visible, UV, X-ray, Gamma.","Radio to Gamma."]},{"id":"WAV-036","q":"State one use and one danger of UV radiation.","m":2,"ans":["Use: Sunbeds/Sterilization. Danger: Skin cancer/Eye damage.","Tanning vs Cancer."]},{"id":"WAV-037","q":"State one use of X-rays.","m":1,"ans":["Medical imaging (bones) / Airport security.","Scanning bones."]},{"id":"WAV-038","q":"State the range of human hearing.","m":1,"ans":["20 Hz to 20,000 Hz (20 kHz).","20-20,000 Hz"]},{"id":"WAV-039","q":"Define 'Ultrasound'.","m":1,"ans":["Sound waves with frequencies above 20,000 Hz.","Higher than human limit."]},{"id":"WAV-040","q":"State one use of ultrasound in medicine.","m":1,"ans":["Prenatal scanning (foetus) / breaking kidney stones.","Scanning babies."]},{"id":"WAV-041","q":"How does the speed of sound compare in solids, liquids, and gases?","m":2,"ans":["Fastest in solids, slowest in gases.","$V_{solid} > V_{liquid} > V_{gas}$"]},{"id":"WAV-042","q":"What determines the 'Pitch' of a sound?","m":1,"ans":["Frequency","Frequency"]},{"id":"WAV-043","q":"What determines the 'Loudness' of a sound?","m":1,"ans":["Amplitude","Amplitude"]},{"id":"WAV-044","q":"Define 'Echo'.","m":1,"ans":["The reflection of a sound wave off a surface.","Reflected sound."]},{"id":"WAV-045","q":"A sound takes 2 s to return from a cliff 330 m away. Calculate speed.","m":2,"ans":["330 m/s","$2 \times 330 / 2$ (Distance is doubled)"]},{"id":"WAV-046","q":"What is 'Monochromatic' light?","m":1,"ans":["Light of a single frequency/color.","Single color."]},{"id":"WAV-047","q":"Explain 'Dispersion'.","m":2,"ans":["The splitting of white light into its component colors by a prism.","Splitting white light."]},{"id":"WAV-048","q":"What are 'Optical Center' and 'Principal Axis' of a lens?","m":2,"ans":["Optical Center: The center point of the lens. Principal Axis: Line passing through the center perpendicular to the lens faces.","Lens center and horizontal line."]},{"id":"WAV-049","q":"Calculate the frequency of a radio wave with $\\lambda = 1500$ m.","m":2,"ans":["200,000 Hz (200 kHz)","$3 \times 10^8 / 1500$"]},{"id":"WAV-050","q":"Are EM waves longitudinal or transverse?","m":1,"ans":["Transverse","Transverse"]},{"id":"WAV-051","q":"Which EM wave has the longest wavelength?","m":1,"ans":["Radio waves.","Radio."]},{"id":"WAV-052","q":"Which EM wave has the highest energy?","m":1,"ans":["Gamma rays.","Gamma."]},{"id":"WAV-053","q":"State one use of Microwaves.","m":1,"ans":["Cooking / Satellite communication / Mobile phones.","Satellite/Cooking."]},{"id":"WAV-054","q":"State one use of Infrared (IR).","m":1,"ans":["Remote controls / Night vision / Radiant heaters.","Remotes."]},{"id":"WAV-055","q":"Why can sound not travel through a vacuum?","m":1,"ans":["It requires a medium (particles) to vibrate and transmit energy.","Needs particles."]},{"id":"WAV-056","q":"Define 'Compression' in a sound wave.","m":1,"ans":["A region where particles are pushed close together (high pressure).","High density area."]},{"id":"WAV-057","q":"Define 'Rarefaction' in a sound wave.","m":1,"ans":["A region where particles are spread far apart (low pressure).","Low density area."]},{"id":"WAV-058","q":"Define 'Diffraction'.","m":2,"ans":["The spreading of waves as they pass through a gap or around an obstacle.","Spreading through gaps."]},{"id":"WAV-059","q":"When is diffraction most significant?","m":1,"ans":["When the size of the gap is similar to the wavelength of the wave.","Gap $\\approx \\lambda$."]},{"id":"WAV-060","q":"What is the speed of sound in air?","m":1,"ans":["Approximately 330–340 m/s.","340 m/s"]},{"id":"WAV-061","q":"If a lens has a magnification of 2, what does this mean?","m":1,"ans":["The image is twice the size of the object.","Image = 2 * Object."]},{"id":"WAV-062","q":"Draw the ray diagram for an object at $2F$. What is the image like?","m":3,"ans":["Real, inverted, same size, at $2F$ on the other side.","Inverted, same size."]},{"id":"WAV-063","q":"What is the 'focal plane'?","m":1,"ans":["The plane perpendicular to the principal axis where the focus lies.","Vertical plane at F."]},{"id":"WAV-064","q":"What is 'Lateral Inversion'?","m":1,"ans":["The effect where the left and right sides of an image are swapped (as in a plane mirror).","Left becomes right."]},{"id":"WAV-065","q":"State the formula for Magnification.","m":1,"ans":["$M = \text{Image height} / \text{Object height}$ or $v/u$.","Height ratio."]},{"id":"WAV-066","q":"Does light speed up or slow down when entering glass from air?","m":1,"ans":["Slows down.","Air to Glass = Slower."]},{"id":"WAV-067","q":"What is 'Normal incidence'?","m":1,"ans":["When light strikes a surface at 90° (along the normal).","$i = 0°$"]},{"id":"WAV-068","q":"Does light bend during normal incidence?","m":1,"ans":["No","Goes straight through."]},{"id":"WAV-069","q":"A wave has 10 crests in 2 seconds. Find its frequency.","m":2,"ans":["5 Hz","10 / 2"]},{"id":"WAV-070","q":"Which EM wave is used in remote controls?","m":1,"ans":["Infrared.","IR."]},{"id":"WAV-071","q":"Why do we see lightning before we hear thunder?","m":1,"ans":["Light travels much faster than sound.","Light vs Sound speed."]},{"id":"WAV-072","q":"State one use of Gamma rays.","m":1,"ans":["Sterilizing medical equipment / Killing cancer cells (Radiotherapy).","Killing bacteria/cancer."]},{"id":"WAV-073","q":"What is 'Sonar'?","m":2,"ans":["Sound Navigation and Ranging; uses ultrasound echoes to measure depth or detect objects underwater.","Underwater echo location."]},{"id":"WAV-074","q":"Define 'Virtual Image'.","m":2,"ans":["An image where light rays do not actually meet; it cannot be projected onto a screen.","Cannot be on screen."]},{"id":"WAV-075","q":"Define 'Real Image'.","m":2,"ans":["An image where light rays actually converge; it can be projected onto a screen.","Can be on screen."]},{"id":"WAV-076","q":"What is the purpose of the 'triangular prism' in dispersion?","m":1,"ans":["To refract different colors by different amounts due to their different speeds in glass.","Separate colors."]},{"id":"WAV-077","q":"If a wave speed is 300 m/s and $\\lambda = 2$ m, find $f$.","m":1,"ans":["150 Hz","300 / 2"]},{"id":"WAV-078","q":"State the effect of loudness on a sound wave's appearance on an oscilloscope.","m":1,"ans":["The amplitude of the trace increases.","Higher waves."]},{"id":"WAV-079","q":"State the effect of pitch on a sound wave's appearance on an oscilloscope.","m":1,"ans":["The number of waves on the screen increases (frequency increases).","More waves."]},{"id":"WAV-080","q":"Why is the image in a plane mirror called 'Virtual'?","m":1,"ans":["Because the rays appear to come from behind the mirror but do not actually pass through the image.","Apparent origin."]},{"id":"WAV-081","q":"Which travels faster: AM radio or FM radio?","m":1,"ans":["They travel at the same speed (speed of light).","EM waves same speed."]},{"id":"WAV-082","q":"State the relationship between density and refractive index.","m":1,"ans":["Generally, denser materials have a higher refractive index.","Higher density $\\approx$ higher $n$."]},{"id":"WAV-083","q":"What happens to a wave's speed if its frequency doubles (wavelength constant)?","m":1,"ans":["Speed doubles.","$v = f\\lambda$"]},{"id":"WAV-084","q":"Define 'Total Internal Reflection'.","m":2,"ans":["Complete reflection of a light ray back into its original denser medium when $i > c$.","100% reflection."]},{"id":"WAV-085","q":"What is the 'Angle of Deviation'?","m":1,"ans":["The angle between the incident ray and the final emergent ray.","Turn angle."]},{"id":"WAV-086","q":"What is the speed of sound in water?","m":1,"ans":["Approximately 1500 m/s.","1500 m/s"]},{"id":"WAV-087","q":"Calculate the wavelength of a 100 MHz radio wave.","m":2,"ans":["3 m","$3 \times 10^8 / 10^8$"]},{"id":"WAV-088","q":"Why are X-rays dangerous?","m":1,"ans":["They are ionizing radiation and can damage DNA/cells.","Ionizing radiation."]},{"id":"WAV-089","q":"Which EM waves are used for satellite TV?","m":1,"ans":["Microwaves.","Microwaves."]},{"id":"WAV-090","q":"What is 'Reflection'?","m":1,"ans":["The bouncing back of a wave when it hits a surface.","Bouncing."]},{"id":"WAV-091","q":"A lens has $f = 10$ cm. Where should an object be placed to act as a magnifying glass?","m":1,"ans":["Less than 10 cm from the lens.","$u < f$"]},{"id":"WAV-092","q":"Does a sound wave travel faster on a hot day or a cold day?","m":1,"ans":["Hot day.","Faster in warm air."]},{"id":"WAV-093","q":"What is 'Refractive Index' of air?","m":1,"ans":["Approximately 1.0","1.0"]},{"id":"WAV-094","q":"State the formula for speed using echo data.","m":1,"ans":["$v = 2d / t$","Double distance / time"]},{"id":"WAV-095","q":"What is the 'Visible Spectrum'?","m":1,"ans":["The part of the EM spectrum that the human eye can detect.","Light."]},{"id":"WAV-096","q":"Is the speed of light constant in all media?","m":1,"ans":["No","Changes based on $n$."]},{"id":"WAV-097","q":"What is 'Wavelength' in a longitudinal wave?","m":1,"ans":["Distance between two successive compressions.","Comp to Comp."]},{"id":"WAV-098","q":"Define 'Phase'.","m":2,"ans":["The position of a specific point on a wave cycle compared to a reference point.","Point in cycle."]},{"id":"WAV-099","q":"Name the EM wave used in burglar alarms (motion sensors).","m":1,"ans":["Infrared (Passive Infrared / PIR).","IR."]},{"id":"WAV-100","q":"State the wave equation in terms of Period.","m":1,"ans":["$v = \\lambda / T$","Wavelength / Period"]}]},{"chapter":"Waves","questions":[{"id":"General_Waves_1","q":"Define 'longitudinal wave' and give one example.","m":2,"ans":["A wave where the particles vibrate parallel to the direction of energy travel.","Example: Sound waves."]},{"id":"Wave_Equation_2","q":"A water wave has a wavelength of 0.5m and a frequency of 4Hz. Calculate the speed of the wave.","m":1,"ans":["v = fλ = 4 * 0.5 = 2.0 m/s."]},{"id":"Reflection_3","q":"State the laws of reflection.","m":2,"ans":["The angle of incidence is equal to the angle of reflection (i=r).","The incident ray, reflected ray, and normal all lie in the same plane."]},{"id":"Refraction_4","q":"Light travels from air into glass. If the angle of incidence is 45° and the refractive index is 1.5, calculate the angle of refraction.","m":3,"ans":["n = sin(i) / sin(r) => 1.5 = sin(45) / sin(r).","sin(r) = 0.707 / 1.5 = 0.471.","r = 28.1°."]},{"id":"Total_Internal_Reflection_5","q":"Define the 'critical angle'.","m":1,"ans":["The angle of incidence in the denser medium for which the angle of refraction in the less dense medium is 90°."]},{"id":"Lenses_6","q":"A converging lens has a focal length of 10cm. Describe the image formed when an object is placed 5cm from the lens.","m":3,"ans":["Virtual.","Upright.","Magnified."]},{"id":"Sound_7","q":"Explain how the pitch and loudness of a sound are related to its wave properties.","m":2,"ans":["Pitch is determined by frequency (higher f = higher pitch).","Loudness is determined by amplitude (higher A = louder sound)."]},{"id":"Diffraction_8","q":"What happens to the amount of diffraction of a wave as it passes through a gap that is much wider than its wavelength?","m":1,"ans":["The amount of diffraction decreases (less spreading of the wave)."]},{"id":"Electromagnetic_Spectrum_9","q":"List the EM waves in order of increasing frequency.","m":1,"ans":["Radio, Micro, Infra-red, Visible, UV, X-ray, Gamma."]},{"id":"Refractive_Index_10","q":"Calculate the speed of light in water (n=1.33) if the speed of light in vacuum is 3e8 m/s.","m":1,"ans":["n = c / v => v = 3e8 / 1.33 = 2.26e8 m/s."]},{"id":"Mirror_Images_11","q":"List three characteristics of an image formed in a plane mirror.","m":3,"ans":["Same size as object.","Laterally inverted.","Virtual (or same distance behind mirror as object is in front)."]},{"id":"TIR_Calculation_12","q":"Calculate the critical angle for a diamond with a refractive index of 2.4.","m":2,"ans":["sin(c) = 1/n = 1 / 2.4 = 0.417.","c = 24.6°."]},{"id":"Wavefronts_13","q":"Define a 'wavefront'.","m":1,"ans":["A line joining all adjacent points on a wave that are in the same phase (e.g., all crests)."]},{"id":"Sound_Speed_14","q":"A clap is heard 2 seconds after the lightning is seen. If sound travels at 330 m/s, how far is the storm?","m":1,"ans":["Distance = speed * time = 330 * 2 = 660 m."]},{"id":"Lenses_15","q":"Define 'focal length'.","m":1,"ans":["The distance between the optical center of the lens and the principal focus."]},{"id":"Dispersion_16","q":"Why is white light dispersed into a spectrum when it passes through a prism?","m":2,"ans":["Different colors (wavelengths) of light travel at different speeds in glass.","This causes them to refract by different amounts (Red least, Violet most)."]},{"id":"Echo_17","q":"A ship uses sonar to measure depth. The pulse takes 0.8s to return. (Speed in water = 1500 m/s). Find depth.","m":2,"ans":["Distance = v * t / 2 (round trip).","Depth = 1500 * 0.8 / 2 = 600 m."]},{"id":"UV_Radiation_18","q":"State one danger and one use of Ultraviolet radiation.","m":2,"ans":["Danger: Skin cancer / cataracts.","Use: Sterilizing medical equipment / sunbeds."]},{"id":"Sound_Range_19","q":"State the approximate range of audible frequencies for a healthy human ear.","m":1,"ans":["20 Hz to 20,000 Hz (20 kHz)."]},{"id":"Lenses_20","q":"Calculate the magnification if an object 2cm high produces an image 6cm high.","m":1,"ans":["Magnification = Height of image / Height of object = 6 / 2 = 3."]},{"id":"Refraction_21","q":"What happens to the frequency of a wave when it enters a denser medium?","m":1,"ans":["Frequency remains constant."]},{"id":"EM_Waves_22","q":"Which property is common to all electromagnetic waves in a vacuum?","m":1,"ans":["They all travel at the same speed ($3 \times 10^8$ m/s)."]},{"id":"Sound_Media_23","q":"Why can sound not travel through a vacuum?","m":1,"ans":["Sound is a mechanical wave; it requires a medium (particles) to transmit vibrations."]},{"id":"X-Rays_24","q":"Explain why X-rays are used for bone imaging.","m":1,"ans":["They are highly penetrating and can pass through soft tissue but are absorbed by dense materials like bone."]},{"id":"Wave_Properties_25","q":"Identify the part of a longitudinal wave where particles are furthest apart.","m":1,"ans":["Rarefaction."]},{"id":"Refractive_Index_26","q":"State the formula for 'n' using real and apparent depth.","m":1,"ans":["n = Real Depth / Apparent Depth."]},{"id":"TIR_Use_27","q":"Give one application of optical fibers in medicine.","m":1,"ans":["Endoscopy (viewing internal organs)."]},{"id":"General_Waves_28","q":"Define 'period' of a wave.","m":1,"ans":["The time taken for one complete wave to pass a point."]},{"id":"Diffraction_29","q":"Sound can be heard around a corner, but light cannot. Why?","m":2,"ans":["Sound has a much longer wavelength than light.","Longer wavelengths diffract (spread) more significantly around large obstacles like corners."]},{"id":"Microwaves_30","q":"State one use of microwaves in communication.","m":1,"ans":["Satellite television / Mobile phone signals."]},{"id":"Sound_Speed_31","q":"In which state of matter does sound travel fastest? Why?","m":1,"ans":["Solids. Particles are closest together, allowing vibrations to transfer more rapidly."]},{"id":"Infra-red_32","q":"Name a device used to detect infra-red radiation.","m":1,"ans":["Thermopile / Blackened thermometer / IR camera."]},{"id":"Refraction_Graph_33","q":"What would a graph of sin(i) against sin(r) look like?","m":1,"ans":["A straight line passing through the origin (the gradient is the refractive index)."]},{"id":"Wavefronts_34","q":"When waves enter shallow water, do the wavefronts get closer or further apart?","m":1,"ans":["Closer together (because speed decreases and frequency stays same, so wavelength decreases)."]},{"id":"Gamma_Rays_35","q":"State a medical use for Gamma radiation.","m":1,"ans":["Radiotherapy (killing cancer cells) / Tracers."]},{"id":"Visible_Light_36","q":"Which color of visible light has the longest wavelength?","m":1,"ans":["Red."]},{"id":"Lenses_37","q":"What type of lens is used to correct short-sightedness?","m":1,"ans":["Diverging (concave) lens."]},{"id":"Echo_38","q":"State one condition necessary for a distinct echo to be heard.","m":1,"ans":["The reflecting surface must be at least 17m away (so the time delay is > 0.1s)."]},{"id":"Refraction_39","q":"A ray enters a glass block along the normal. What is the angle of refraction?","m":1,"ans":["0 degrees."]},{"id":"Sound_40","q":"What is an 'ultrasound'?","m":1,"ans":["Sound with a frequency higher than the upper limit of human hearing (> 20,000 Hz)."]},{"id":"Calculations_41","q":"Frequency is 500 Hz. Calculate the period.","m":1,"ans":["T = 1/f = 1/500 = 0.002 s."]},{"id":"Radio_Waves_42","q":"Why are radio waves used for broadcasting?","m":1,"ans":["They have long wavelengths that can reflect off the ionosphere or diffract over hills."]},{"id":"Refraction_43","q":"If n=1.5, calculate the ratio of the speed of light in vacuum to speed in medium.","m":1,"ans":["Ratio = 1.5 (definition of refractive index)."]},{"id":"Lenses_44","q":"Where must an object be placed in front of a converging lens to act as a magnifying glass?","m":1,"ans":["Between the optical center and the principal focus (u < f)."]},{"id":"General_Waves_45","q":"Is a water wave longitudinal or transverse?","m":1,"ans":["Transverse."]},{"id":"Sound_46","q":"How does increasing the temperature of air affect the speed of sound?","m":1,"ans":["Speed of sound increases."]},{"id":"TIR_47","q":"Calculate 'n' if the critical angle is 30 degrees.","m":1,"ans":["n = 1 / sin(30) = 1 / 0.5 = 2.0."]},{"id":"EM_Waves_48","q":"Which EM wave has the highest energy per photon?","m":1,"ans":["Gamma rays."]},{"id":"Wave_Behavior_49","q":"Define 'reflection'.","m":1,"ans":["The bouncing back of a wave when it hits a boundary/surface."]},{"id":"Optics_50","q":"What is meant by a 'virtual image'?","m":1,"ans":["An image formed where light rays appear to diverge from, but do not actually pass through."]}]},{"chapter":"Work Energy and Power","questions":[{"id":"Work_Done_1","q":"A man pushes a 50 kg crate 10 m across a floor with a horizontal force of 200 N. The frictional force is 150 N. Calculate the work done by the man and the work done against friction.","m":2,"ans":["Work by man = F * d = 200 * 10 = 2000 J.","Work against friction = 150 * 10 = 1500 J."]},{"id":"Kinetic_Energy_2","q":"An object of mass 2 kg has 100 J of kinetic energy. Calculate its speed.","m":2,"ans":["KE = 0.5 * m * v² => 100 = 0.5 * 2 * v².","v² = 100, so v = 10 m/s."]},{"id":"GPE_3","q":"A crane lifts a 2000 kg load through a vertical height of 30 m in 1 minute. Calculate the gain in GPE.","m":1,"ans":["GPE = mgh = 2000 * 10 * 30 = 600,000 J (or 600 kJ)."]},{"id":"Power_4","q":"Using the data from Q3, calculate the useful power output of the crane.","m":1,"ans":["Power = Work / time = 600,000 J / 60 s = 10,000 W (or 10 kW)."]},{"id":"Efficiency_5","q":"An electric motor is 75% efficient. If it consumes 4000 J of electrical energy, how much energy is 'wasted' as heat and sound?","m":2,"ans":["Useful energy = 0.75 * 4000 = 3000 J.","Wasted energy = 4000 - 3000 = 1000 J."]},{"id":"Conservation_of_Energy_6","q":"A ball is dropped from a height of 20 m. Calculate its speed just before it hits the ground, assuming no air resistance. (g=10)","m":3,"ans":["mgh = 0.5 * m * v².","10 * 20 = 0.5 * v² => v² = 400.","v = 20 m/s."]},{"id":"Work_Done_7","q":"Explain why no work is done by the Earth's gravity on a satellite in a perfectly circular orbit.","m":2,"ans":["The force of gravity is perpendicular to the direction of motion (displacement).","Work = Fd cos(90) = 0."]},{"id":"Energy_Sources_8","q":"State the difference between renewable and non-renewable energy sources.","m":2,"ans":["Renewable: Sources that are replenished naturally in a short time (e.g., solar, wind).","Non-renewable: Finite sources that take millions of years to form (e.g., coal, oil)."]},{"id":"Power_9","q":"A car engine provides a constant driving force of 500 N at a constant speed of 20 m/s. Calculate the power developed.","m":1,"ans":["Power = Force * velocity = 500 * 20 = 10,000 W."]},{"id":"Kinetic_Energy_10","q":"If the speed of a vehicle is tripled, by what factor does its kinetic energy increase?","m":2,"ans":["KE is proportional to v².","3² = 9. The KE increases by a factor of 9."]},{"id":"Work_Done_11","q":"A 5 kg block is pulled 4 m up a frictionless slope tilted at 30°. Calculate the work done.","m":2,"ans":["Vertical height h = 4 * sin(30) = 2 m.","Work = mgh = 5 * 10 * 2 = 100 J."]},{"id":"Nuclear_Energy_12","q":"Describe the energy change occurring in a nuclear power station.","m":1,"ans":["Nuclear energy -> Thermal energy -> Kinetic energy -> Electrical energy."]},{"id":"Efficiency_13","q":"A light bulb produces 10 J of light for every 100 J of electrical energy. Calculate efficiency.","m":1,"ans":["(10/100) * 100 = 10%."]},{"id":"Power_14","q":"A person of mass 60 kg runs up a flight of stairs 5 m high in 4 s. Calculate their power.","m":2,"ans":["Work = 60 * 10 * 5 = 3000 J.","Power = 3000 / 4 = 750 W."]},{"id":"Solar_Energy_15","q":"List one advantage and one disadvantage of solar cells.","m":2,"ans":["Advantage: No CO2 emissions during operation.","Disadvantage: Weather dependent / high initial cost."]},{"id":"Conservation_of_Energy_16","q":"A pendulum bob (mass 0.1 kg) is pulled aside to a height of 0.2 m. Calculate its maximum velocity.","m":2,"ans":["mgh = 0.5mv².","10 * 0.2 = 0.5 * v² => v = 2 m/s."]},{"id":"Work/Energy_17","q":"Define the Joule in terms of Newtons and Metres.","m":1,"ans":["The work done when a force of 1 Newton moves an object through a distance of 1 metre in the direction of the force."]},{"id":"Chemical_Energy_18","q":"Name two devices that convert chemical energy into electrical energy.","m":2,"ans":["Battery / Electric cell.","Hydrogen fuel cell."]},{"id":"Internal_Energy_19","q":"When a car brakes to a stop, where does its kinetic energy go?","m":1,"ans":["It is converted into thermal (internal) energy in the brake pads and discs due to friction."]},{"id":"Efficiency_20","q":"Why can an efficiency never be 100% or greater?","m":1,"ans":["Due to friction/air resistance, some energy is always dissipated as heat to the surroundings."]},{"id":"Hydroelectric_Power_21","q":"Identify the initial form of energy stored behind a dam.","m":1,"ans":["Gravitational Potential Energy."]},{"id":"Kinetic_Energy_22","q":"A 0.5 kg ball is kicked at 20 m/s. Calculate its KE.","m":1,"ans":["0.5 * 0.5 * 20² = 100 J."]},{"id":"Elastic_Potential_Energy_23","q":"What happens to the energy stored in a compressed spring when it is released?","m":1,"ans":["Elastic potential energy is converted into kinetic energy."]},{"id":"Work_Done_24","q":"A weightlifter holds a 100 kg barbell 2 m above the ground for 5 s. Calculate the work done during those 5 s.","m":1,"ans":["Zero (there is no displacement during the 5 seconds)."]},{"id":"Sankey_Diagrams_25","q":"What is represented by the width of the arrows in a Sankey diagram?","m":1,"ans":["The amount of energy or power."]},{"id":"Tidal_Energy_26","q":"What is the primary source of tidal energy?","m":1,"ans":["The gravitational pull of the Moon (and Sun)."]},{"id":"Geothermal_Energy_27","q":"Where does geothermal energy originate?","m":1,"ans":["Radioactive decay of elements inside the Earth's core."]},{"id":"Power_28","q":"How many Watts are in 1 Horsepower (approx)?","m":1,"ans":["746 W."]},{"id":"Conservation_of_Energy_29","q":"An object falls with air resistance. Compare the loss in GPE to the gain in KE.","m":1,"ans":["Loss in GPE > Gain in KE (Some energy is lost as heat due to work done against air resistance)."]},{"id":"Efficiency_30","q":"A machine does 200 J of useful work but requires 1000 J of input. Find efficiency.","m":1,"ans":["(200/1000) * 100 = 20%."]},{"id":"Wind_Energy_31","q":"Which energy transformation occurs in a wind turbine?","m":1,"ans":["Kinetic energy of wind -> Kinetic energy of blades -> Electrical energy."]},{"id":"Kinetic_Energy_32","q":"Two cars have the same KE. Car A has twice the mass of Car B. Find the ratio of their speeds.","m":2,"ans":["0.5 * m1 * v1² = 0.5 * m2 * v2².","2m * v1² = m * v2² => v2/v1 = sqrt(2)."]},{"id":"GPE_33","q":"Does GPE depend on the path taken to reach a height?","m":1,"ans":["No, it only depends on the vertical height h."]},{"id":"Work/Power_34","q":"Two people lift the same weight to the same height. Person A takes 10s, Person B takes 20s. Compare work and power.","m":2,"ans":["Work done is the same.","Person A has twice the power of Person B."]},{"id":"Fossil_Fuels_35","q":"Name the gas released by burning fossil fuels that contributes to global warming.","m":1,"ans":["Carbon dioxide (CO2)."]},{"id":"Efficiency_36","q":"State the formula for Efficiency in terms of Power.","m":1,"ans":["Efficiency = (Useful Power Output / Total Power Input) * 100%."]},{"id":"Work_Done_37","q":"Calculate the work done in lifting a 500g book 1.5m.","m":1,"ans":["0.5 kg * 10 * 1.5 = 7.5 J."]},{"id":"Sun_as_a_Source_38","q":"Which energy source does NOT originate from the Sun?","m":1,"ans":["Nuclear / Geothermal / Tidal."]},{"id":"Kinetic_Energy_39","q":"A 1000 kg car travels at 72 km/h. Calculate its KE.","m":2,"ans":["72 km/h = 20 m/s.","0.5 * 1000 * 20² = 200,000 J."]},{"id":"Internal_Energy_40","q":"Define internal energy.","m":1,"ans":["The sum of the random distribution of kinetic and potential energies of the molecules in a system."]},{"id":"Work_Done_41","q":"A force of 50N acts on a body at 60° to the horizontal. If the body moves 10m horizontally, find the work done.","m":1,"ans":["Work = Fd cosθ = 50 * 10 * cos(60) = 250 J."]},{"id":"Power_42","q":"Calculate the power of a pump that can move 10kg of water per second through 5m.","m":1,"ans":["Power = mgh/t = 10 * 10 * 5 = 500 W."]},{"id":"Conservation_of_Energy_43","q":"A 2kg mass falls 10m and its velocity is 12 m/s. Calculate energy lost to heat.","m":3,"ans":["Initial GPE = 2 * 10 * 10 = 200 J.","Final KE = 0.5 * 2 * 12² = 144 J.","Loss = 200 - 144 = 56 J."]},{"id":"Efficiency_44","q":"If an engine's efficiency is 30%, how much input energy is needed for 600 J of useful work?","m":1,"ans":["Input = Useful / 0.3 = 600 / 0.3 = 2000 J."]},{"id":"Sankey_Diagram_45","q":"Draw a Sankey diagram for a heater where 80% is useful heat and 20% is light.","m":1,"ans":["Input arrow splits into a thick 80% arrow (straight) and a thin 20% arrow (down)."]},{"id":"Potential_Energy_46","q":"A spring with k = 100 N/m is compressed by 0.1 m. Calculate energy stored.","m":1,"ans":["E = 0.5 * k * x² = 0.5 * 100 * (0.1)² = 0.5 J."]},{"id":"Energy_Dissipation_47","q":"What is meant by 'degraded' energy?","m":1,"ans":["Energy that is spread out to the surroundings (usually as heat) and is no longer useful for work."]},{"id":"Work_Done_48","q":"Calculate work done to accelerate a 5 kg object from 2 m/s to 6 m/s.","m":1,"ans":["Work = Change in KE = 0.5 * 5 * (6² - 2²) = 0.5 * 5 * 32 = 80 J."]},{"id":"Power_49","q":"A 1 kW heater runs for 1 hour. Calculate energy in Joules.","m":1,"ans":["Energy = 1000 W * 3600 s = 3.6 x 10⁶ J."]},{"id":"Efficiency_50","q":"List two ways to improve the efficiency of a mechanical system.","m":2,"ans":["Lubrication (to reduce friction).","Streamlining (to reduce air resistance)."]}]},{"chapter":"Work Energy Power Basics","questions":[{"id":"WEP-001","q":"Define 'Work Done'.","m":2,"ans":["The product of the force and the distance moved in the direction of the force.","$W = F \times d$"]},{"id":"WEP-002","q":"State the SI unit for Work and Energy.","m":1,"ans":["Joule (J)","J"]},{"id":"WEP-003","q":"Define 'Power'.","m":1,"ans":["The rate of doing work / rate of energy transfer.","$P = W/t$"]},{"id":"WEP-004","q":"State the SI unit for Power.","m":1,"ans":["Watt (W)","W"]},{"id":"WEP-005","q":"State the Principle of Conservation of Energy.","m":2,"ans":["Energy cannot be created or destroyed, only converted from one form to another.","Total energy remains constant."]},{"id":"WEP-006","q":"Write the formula for Kinetic Energy ($E_k$).","m":1,"ans":["$E_k = \frac{1}{2}mv^2$","0.5 * mass * velocity squared"]},{"id":"WEP-007","q":"Write the formula for Gravitational Potential Energy ($E_p$).","m":1,"ans":["$E_p = mgh$","mass * gravity * height"]},{"id":"WEP-008","q":"A 10 N force moves an object 5 m. Calculate the work done.","m":1,"ans":["50 J","10 * 5"]},{"id":"WEP-009","q":"Calculate the kinetic energy of a 2 kg mass moving at 4 m/s.","m":2,"ans":["16 J","0.5 * 2 * 4^2"]},{"id":"WEP-010","q":"Define 'Efficiency'.","m":2,"ans":["The ratio of useful energy output to total energy input (expressed as a percentage).","Useful output / Total input"]},{"id":"WEP-011","q":"Why is efficiency always less than 100% in real machines?","m":2,"ans":["Energy is lost to the surroundings, usually as heat due to friction.","Thermal energy losses."]},{"id":"WEP-012","q":"A motor does 1000 J of work in 20 s. Calculate its power.","m":2,"ans":["50 W","1000 / 20"]},{"id":"WEP-013","q":"If an object's velocity doubles, what happens to its Kinetic Energy?","m":2,"ans":["It quadruples ($4 \times$).","$E_k \\propto v^2$"]},{"id":"WEP-014","q":"State the energy change when a battery-powered torch is switched on.","m":2,"ans":["Chemical Energy $\rightarrow$ Electrical Energy $\rightarrow$ Light + Heat Energy.","Chemical to Light/Heat."]},{"id":"WEP-015","q":"An object of mass 5 kg is lifted 2 m. Calculate the gain in $E_p$ ($g=10$).","m":2,"ans":["100 J","5 * 10 * 2"]},{"id":"WEP-016","q":"Calculate the work done when a force of 50 N is applied but the object does not move.","m":1,"ans":["0 J","No displacement = No work."]},{"id":"WEP-017","q":"Name the primary energy source for most energy on Earth.","m":1,"ans":["The Sun / Solar Energy.","Sun"]},{"id":"WEP-018","q":"Define a 'Non-renewable' energy source.","m":1,"ans":["A source that is finite and cannot be replaced once used.","Will run out."]},{"id":"WEP-019","q":"State two examples of renewable energy sources.","m":1,"ans":["Solar, Wind, Hydroelectric, Geothermal, Tidal.","Wind and Solar."]},{"id":"WEP-020","q":"What is the gravitational potential energy of an object on the ground?","m":1,"ans":["0 J","Zero (h = 0)"]},{"id":"WEP-021","q":"A 60 W bulb is left on for 1 minute. Calculate the energy used.","m":2,"ans":["3600 J","60 * 60"]},{"id":"WEP-022","q":"State the energy conversion in a hydroelectric dam.","m":2,"ans":["G.P.E. $\rightarrow$ Kinetic Energy $\rightarrow$ Electrical Energy.","Water GPE to Electricity."]},{"id":"WEP-023","q":"If 200 J of energy is supplied to a machine and 160 J is useful, find the efficiency.","m":2,"ans":["80%","(160 / 200) * 100"]},{"id":"WEP-024","q":"Is 'Energy' a scalar or a vector?","m":1,"ans":["Scalar","Scalar"]},{"id":"WEP-025","q":"Calculate the mass of an object with 100 J of $E_p$ at a height of 5 m ($g=10$).","m":2,"ans":["2 kg","100 / (10 * 5)"]},{"id":"WEP-026","q":"What happens to the $E_p$ of a falling ball as it loses height?","m":1,"ans":["It decreases.","Converts to KE."]},{"id":"WEP-027","q":"What energy is stored in a stretched rubber band?","m":1,"ans":["Elastic Potential Energy.","Strain energy."]},{"id":"WEP-028","q":"Explain 'Sankey Diagrams'.","m":2,"ans":["A diagram showing energy transfers where the width of arrows represents the amount of energy.","Visual energy flow."]},{"id":"WEP-029","q":"A car engine is 25% efficient. If it produces 5000 J of useful work, how much total energy was input?","m":2,"ans":["20,000 J","5000 / 0.25"]},{"id":"WEP-030","q":"Which energy source uses uranium?","m":1,"ans":["Nuclear Fission / Nuclear Power.","Nuclear."]},{"id":"WEP-031","q":"What is the kinetic energy of a stationary object?","m":1,"ans":["0 J","Zero"]},{"id":"WEP-032","q":"Calculate the velocity of a 1 kg mass with 50 J of Kinetic Energy.","m":3,"ans":["10 m/s","$\\sqrt{50 \times 2 / 1}$"]},{"id":"WEP-033","q":"Does walking up stairs or running up stairs require more work?","m":2,"ans":["The work is the same (same force and distance), but the power is different.","Work is identical."]},{"id":"WEP-034","q":"Define 'Internal Energy'.","m":2,"ans":["The sum of the random distribution of kinetic and potential energies of the particles in a system.","Total particle energy."]},{"id":"WEP-035","q":"What is the power of a machine that does 120 J of work in 3 seconds?","m":1,"ans":["40 W","120 / 3"]},{"id":"WEP-036","q":"A ball is thrown up. At the highest point, what has the kinetic energy been converted into?","m":1,"ans":["Gravitational Potential Energy.","G.P.E."]},{"id":"WEP-037","q":"What is the energy conversion in a solar cell?","m":1,"ans":["Light Energy $\rightarrow$ Electrical Energy.","Light to Electric."]},{"id":"WEP-038","q":"State one disadvantage of wind energy.","m":1,"ans":["Unreliable (only works when windy) / Visual and noise pollution.","Intermittent."]},{"id":"WEP-039","q":"State the energy conversion in a microphone.","m":1,"ans":["Sound Energy $\rightarrow$ Electrical Energy.","Sound to Electric."]},{"id":"WEP-040","q":"Calculate the height a 10 kg object must be lifted to have 500 J of $E_p$.","m":2,"ans":["5 m","500 / (10 * 10)"]},{"id":"WEP-041","q":"Which uses more power: A 1000 W heater for 1 hour or a 2000 W heater for 30 minutes?","m":1,"ans":["The 2000 W heater has more power (but they use the same energy).","2000 W."]},{"id":"WEP-042","q":"What is 'waste energy'?","m":1,"ans":["Energy that is converted into forms that are not useful for the intended purpose.","Non-useful energy."]},{"id":"WEP-043","q":"Define 'Joule' in terms of Newtons and Metres.","m":1,"ans":["1 Joule = 1 Newton $\times$ 1 Metre.","1 J = 1 Nm"]},{"id":"WEP-044","q":"How does mass affect the kinetic energy of an object at a constant speed?","m":1,"ans":["Kinetic Energy is directly proportional to mass.","$E_k \\propto m$"]},{"id":"WEP-045","q":"Calculate the power of a heart that pumps 0.1 J of work every beat, with 60 beats per minute.","m":3,"ans":["0.1 W","(0.1 * 60) / 60"]},{"id":"WEP-046","q":"State the formula for Efficiency in terms of Power.","m":1,"ans":["(Useful Power Output / Total Power Input) * 100","P_out / P_in"]},{"id":"WEP-047","q":"Name the energy stored in food and fuel.","m":1,"ans":["Chemical Potential Energy.","Chemical Energy."]},{"id":"WEP-048","q":"A car travels at constant speed on a level road. Where does the chemical energy of the fuel go?","m":1,"ans":["It is converted to heat energy due to friction and air resistance.","Internal/Heat Energy."]},{"id":"WEP-049","q":"Why is G.P.E. called 'potential' energy?","m":1,"ans":["Because it is stored energy that has the potential to do work when the object's position changes.","Stored energy."]},{"id":"WEP-050","q":"A pendulum swings. At which point is the $E_k$ at its maximum?","m":1,"ans":["At the lowest point (equilibrium position).","Bottom of the swing."]},{"id":"WEP-051","q":"What is the energy conversion in a nuclear power station?","m":2,"ans":["Nuclear $\rightarrow$ Thermal $\rightarrow$ Kinetic $\rightarrow$ Electrical.","Nuclear to Heat to Electric."]},{"id":"WEP-052","q":"State the value of the 'watt' in terms of Joules and Seconds.","m":1,"ans":["1 Watt = 1 Joule per second.","1 W = 1 J/s"]},{"id":"WEP-053","q":"A 50 kg girl runs up a 4 m high flight of stairs in 5 s. Calculate her power.","m":3,"ans":["400 W","(50 * 10 * 4) / 5"]},{"id":"WEP-054","q":"If air resistance is present, how does it affect the final $E_k$ of a falling object?","m":1,"ans":["Final $E_k$ will be less than the initial $E_p$ (some energy is lost as heat).","Reduces KE."]},{"id":"WEP-055","q":"State the energy conversion when a ball hits the floor and bounces back.","m":2,"ans":["Kinetic $\rightarrow$ Elastic Potential (deformation) + Heat + Sound $\rightarrow$ Kinetic.","KE to EPE to KE."]},{"id":"WEP-056","q":"Calculate the work done by a crane lifting a 2000 kg load through 10 m.","m":2,"ans":["200,000 J","20,000 N * 10 m"]},{"id":"WEP-057","q":"Does a satellite orbiting Earth have kinetic energy?","m":1,"ans":["Yes (because it is moving).","Yes"]},{"id":"WEP-058","q":"Does a satellite orbiting Earth have gravitational potential energy?","m":1,"ans":["Yes (because it is at a height above the surface).","Yes"]},{"id":"WEP-059","q":"If friction is 5 N and distance is 10 m, how much energy is 'lost' as heat?","m":1,"ans":["50 J","5 * 10"]},{"id":"WEP-060","q":"Why do we use the term 'degraded energy'?","m":1,"ans":["Energy that has spread out into the surroundings as heat and is no longer useful for work.","Non-useful heat."]},{"id":"WEP-061","q":"A machine has a power of 2 kW. How much work can it do in 10 minutes?","m":3,"ans":["1,200,000 J","2000 * 600"]},{"id":"WEP-062","q":"Define 'Biomass' as an energy source.","m":1,"ans":["Organic material from plants and animals used as fuel.","Organic fuel."]},{"id":"WEP-063","q":"State the energy conversion in a steam engine.","m":2,"ans":["Chemical (fuel) $\rightarrow$ Thermal (steam) $\rightarrow$ Kinetic (piston/wheels).","Heat to Kinetic."]},{"id":"WEP-064","q":"Calculate the $E_k$ of a car of mass 1200 kg traveling at 20 m/s.","m":1,"ans":["240,000 J","0.5 * 1200 * 400"]},{"id":"WEP-065","q":"What happens to the power required if the time taken to do work is doubled?","m":1,"ans":["Power is halved.","P = W/2t"]},{"id":"WEP-066","q":"What is the energy conversion in a loudspeaker?","m":1,"ans":["Electrical Energy $\rightarrow$ Sound Energy.","Electric to Sound."]},{"id":"WEP-067","q":"Is geothermal energy renewable?","m":1,"ans":["Yes","Yes"]},{"id":"WEP-068","q":"A weightlifter holds a 100 kg barbell 2 m above his head. How much power is he exerting while holding it still?","m":1,"ans":["0 W","No work is being done if there is no movement."]},{"id":"WEP-069","q":"What is the formula for Power in terms of Force and Velocity?","m":1,"ans":["$P = F \times v$","Force * speed"]},{"id":"WEP-070","q":"Name the process by which energy is released in the Sun.","m":1,"ans":["Nuclear Fusion.","Fusion."]},{"id":"WEP-071","q":"How does a solar water heater differ from a solar cell?","m":2,"ans":["A solar heater produces thermal energy (heat); a solar cell produces electrical energy.","Heat vs Electricity."]},{"id":"WEP-072","q":"What is the work done against gravity when a 2 kg object is moved horizontally for 10 m?","m":1,"ans":["0 J","Force of gravity is perpendicular to movement."]},{"id":"WEP-073","q":"Calculate the energy used by a 2 kW kettle in 3 minutes.","m":2,"ans":["360,000 J","2000 * 180"]},{"id":"WEP-074","q":"What is the unit for energy consumption used by electricity companies?","m":1,"ans":["kilowatt-hour (kWh)","kWh"]},{"id":"WEP-075","q":"Convert 1 kWh into Joules.","m":2,"ans":["3,600,000 J","1000 * 3600"]},{"id":"WEP-076","q":"Name one disadvantage of fossil fuels.","m":1,"ans":["Produce carbon dioxide (Greenhouse gas) / Global warming / Non-renewable.","Pollution."]},{"id":"WEP-077","q":"What is the gravitational potential energy of a 100 g bird at a height of 20 m? ($g=10$)","m":2,"ans":["20 J","0.1 * 10 * 20"]},{"id":"WEP-078","q":"If an object falls from 10 m to 5 m, how much of its $E_p$ has it lost?","m":1,"ans":["50% (Half)","Half."]},{"id":"WEP-079","q":"Describe the energy flow in an electric motor.","m":1,"ans":["Electrical Energy $\rightarrow$ Kinetic Energy + Heat.","Electric to KE."]},{"id":"WEP-080","q":"What is the energy conversion in a windmill?","m":1,"ans":["Kinetic Energy (wind) $\rightarrow$ Kinetic Energy (blades) $\rightarrow$ Electrical Energy.","Wind KE to Electric."]},{"id":"WEP-081","q":"If you push a wall for 1 hour, have you done any work in the physics sense?","m":1,"ans":["No","Distance moved is zero."]},{"id":"WEP-082","q":"State the relationship between Power and Time.","m":1,"ans":["Power is inversely proportional to time for a fixed amount of work.","P = 1/t"]},{"id":"WEP-083","q":"Calculate the work done by a person of mass 70 kg climbing a 10 m ladder.","m":2,"ans":["7000 J","700 N * 10 m"]},{"id":"WEP-084","q":"A machine is 40% efficient. If it wastes 600 J, what was the total input?","m":3,"ans":["1000 J","600 is 60%, so 10% is 100, so 100% is 1000."]},{"id":"WEP-085","q":"What energy is stored in a compressed spring?","m":1,"ans":["Elastic Potential Energy.","EPE."]},{"id":"WEP-086","q":"Why is tidal energy predictable?","m":1,"ans":["Because tides are caused by the orbits of the Moon and Earth, which are regular.","Gravity of moon."]},{"id":"WEP-087","q":"What is the main energy transfer in a television?","m":2,"ans":["Electrical Energy $\rightarrow$ Light + Sound + Heat.","Electric to Light/Sound."]},{"id":"WEP-088","q":"State the energy conversion in a car's brakes.","m":1,"ans":["Kinetic Energy $\rightarrow$ Heat Energy.","KE to Heat."]},{"id":"WEP-089","q":"Which is more powerful: A motor that lifts 10 kg in 2 s or one that lifts 10 kg in 5 s?","m":1,"ans":["The one that takes 2 s.","Faster work = more power."]},{"id":"WEP-090","q":"Calculate the Kinetic Energy of a 0.5 kg ball moving at 20 m/s.","m":1,"ans":["100 J","0.5 * 0.5 * 400"]},{"id":"WEP-091","q":"What is the name for the energy an object has due to its motion?","m":1,"ans":["Kinetic Energy.","KE."]},{"id":"WEP-092","q":"What happens to the $E_k$ of a car when it stops?","m":1,"ans":["It is converted to heat in the brakes and surroundings.","Becomes heat."]},{"id":"WEP-093","q":"Calculate the work done to move a 5 kg object at constant speed for 2 m where friction is 20 N.","m":2,"ans":["40 J","20 N * 2 m (Weight is ignored as it's perpendicular)."]},{"id":"WEP-094","q":"Can an object have negative potential energy?","m":1,"ans":["Yes (if it is below the chosen 'zero' reference level).","Yes."]},{"id":"WEP-095","q":"What is the energy conversion in a battery charger?","m":1,"ans":["Electrical Energy $\rightarrow$ Chemical Potential Energy.","Electric to Chemical."]},{"id":"WEP-096","q":"Define 'system' in energy terms.","m":1,"ans":["An object or group of objects being studied.","Subject of study."]},{"id":"WEP-097","q":"What is the power of a 100 N force moving an object at 2 m/s?","m":1,"ans":["200 W","100 * 2"]},{"id":"WEP-098","q":"State one advantage of nuclear energy.","m":1,"ans":["No CO2 emissions / High energy density / Reliable output.","No greenhouse gases."]},{"id":"WEP-099","q":"Why does a bouncing ball eventually stop?","m":1,"ans":["Energy is lost as heat and sound to the surroundings during each bounce.","Energy dissipation."]},{"id":"WEP-100","q":"Calculate the efficiency of a motor that takes 500 J and lifts a 10 kg mass by 4 m.","m":3,"ans":["80%","Useful = 100 * 4 = 400 J. Efficiency = 400/500."]}]}];
